-- Full-depth (10-20 minute) reading summaries, batch 2: the remaining 12 published books
-- (Psychology of Money, Rich Dad Poor Dad, Cashflow Quadrant, Think and Grow Rich, Sapiens,
-- How to Win Friends, Eat That Frog, Millionaire Fastlane, Little Book of Ikigai,
-- Magic of Thinking Big, Elon Musk, Broken Money).
-- Generated from structured content; see scratchpad/gen-sql.mjs and content-*.mjs for source.

-- The Psychology of Money — Morgan Housel
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$Doing well with money is a soft skill about behavior, not a hard science about what you know.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ຄວາມສຳເລັດດ້ານການເງິນແມ່ນທັກສະດ້ານພຶດຕິກຳ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮູ້ທາງວິຊາການລ້ວນໆ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Morgan Housel's argument is that money success is not primarily a function of intelligence, formulas, or spreadsheets — it is a function of behavior, and behavior is stubborn to change because it is shaped by ego, incentives, and the narrow slice of economic history each of us happens to live through. He tells the story through short, independent chapters built around real people whose financial lives illustrate a single psychological point, from a janitor who quietly amassed a fortune to a Wall Street executive who lost everything chasing more. This lesson works through the book's core arguments about luck, risk, compounding, enough, and the true dividend money buys, and closes with a clear-eyed look at where the book's advice runs thinnest.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ Morgan Housel ຄື ຄວາມສຳເລັດດ້ານການເງິນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຜົນຈາກຄວາມສະຫຼາດ, ສູດຄຳນວນ ຫຼື ຕາຕະລາງຄິດໄລ່ ແຕ່ເປັນຜົນຈາກພຶດຕິກຳ — ແລະ ພຶດຕິກຳແມ່ນປ່ຽນຍາກ ເພາະຖືກກຳນົດໂດຍອັດຕາຕົວຕົນ (ego), ແຮງຈູງໃຈ ແລະ ປະສົບການປະຫວັດສາດເສດຖະກິດແຄບໆທີ່ແຕ່ລະຄົນເຄີຍຜ່ານມາ. ລາວເລົ່າເລື່ອງຜ່ານບົດສັ້ນໆທີ່ເປັນເອກະລາດຈາກກັນ ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບຄົນຈິງທີ່ຊີວິດການເງິນຂອງເຂົາເຈົ້າສະທ້ອນແນວຄິດທາງຈິດຕະວິທະຍາອັນດຽວ ຕັ້ງແຕ່ຄົນເຮັດຄວາມສະອາດທີ່ສະສົມຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຢ່າງງຽບໆ ຈົນເຖິງຜູ້ບໍລິຫານວໍລສະຕຣີດທີ່ເສຍທຸກຢ່າງເພາະໄລ່ຫາຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນເລື້ອຍໆ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຫຼັກຂອງປຶ້ມກ່ຽວກັບໂຊກ, ຄວາມສ່ຽງ, ດອກເບ້ຍທົບຕົ້ນ, ຄວາມພໍດີ ແລະ ຜົນຕອບແທນທີ່ແທ້ຈິງທີ່ເງິນຊື້ໄດ້ ແລະ ປິດທ້າຍດ້ວຍການພິຈາລະນາຢ່າງກົງໄປກົງມາ ວ່າຄຳແນະນຳຂອງປຶ້ມອ່ອນທີ່ສຸດຢູ່ຈຸດໃດ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Why financial success is a soft skill, not a hard science$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel opens with a blunt observation: managing money well has surprisingly little to do with how smart you are. He points to people with no financial training who quietly build significant wealth through patience and restraint, alongside brilliant, highly educated professionals who blow up their finances through overconfidence and impatience. The gap between the two is not knowledge — it is behavior.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This matters because most financial education is delivered as if money were a math problem: learn the right formulas, calculate the right numbers, and good outcomes follow. Housel argues this framing is mostly wrong. Money decisions are made by ordinary people with egos, historical baggage, family pressure, and incomplete information, not by neutral calculators sitting in a vacuum. A financial plan that ignores how the person actually feels and behaves under stress is not a realistic plan at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The book's structure follows from this premise. Rather than building one unified theory, Housel offers a series of short, largely independent stories, each isolating one psychological tendency — how we think about risk, luck, greed, or enough — because he believes the messy, personal nature of money is better captured in stories than in formulas.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Doing well with money has little to do with how smart you are and everything to do with how you behave.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Luck and risk: the invisible forces behind every outcome$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel opens with two contrasting real-world figures whose financial paths were shaped by forces largely outside their control: one had extraordinary luck feed a fortune, the other had extraordinary risk sink one. His point is not that skill is irrelevant — it is that luck and risk are siblings, both products of the fact that every outcome in life is guided by forces other than individual effort, and neither is easy to measure from the outside.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Luck and risk$$, 'body', $$Two sides of the same coin: the invisible role that forces beyond an individual's control — good and bad — play in every financial outcome. Because they are invisible, it is tempting to overcredit skill for good outcomes and overblame bad decisions for bad ones.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The practical danger is twofold. First, we tend to study extreme successes and extreme failures too literally, copying the exact moves of the wildly successful or condemning the exact moves of the wildly unsuccessful, without accounting for how much luck or risk was baked into each story. Second, this same blindness makes us harsh judges of other people's decisions and of our own past mistakes, because it's hard to tell in hindsight which choices were genuinely bad and which were merely unlucky.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel's suggested response is not paralysis but humility: focus less on identifying specific winners and losers to imitate or avoid, and more on broad patterns that hold across many outcomes, while building enough room in your own plans that bad luck alone cannot wipe you out. Respecting risk means assuming a chunk of any result — yours or anyone else's — came from something you cannot fully see.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Every financial outcome is shaped by luck and risk as much as by skill, and both are nearly invisible from the outside.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Never enough: the moving goalpost$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel tells the story of a hedge fund manager who had already made more money than his family could spend across generations, and who nonetheless committed a crime chasing more — risking prison for a sum that could not meaningfully improve a life already secured. The lesson is not about greed in the cartoonish sense; it is about a much more ordinary failure to recognize when enough has been reached.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Never enough$$, 'body', $$The tendency for the goalposts of what counts as "enough" money, status, or possessions to move outward every time they are reached, so that satisfaction never actually arrives no matter how much is accumulated.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The danger of not having a concept of enough, Housel argues, is that it pushes people to take on the single most dangerous risk in personal finance: risking something they have and need for something they don't have and don't need, purely for the sake of a higher number. Social comparison makes this worse — there will always be someone with more, so a goalpost defined relative to other people can never be reached, only chased.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His practical anchor is to define enough for yourself in advance, deliberately, rather than letting it drift upward automatically with every raise or windfall. He is careful to note this is not an argument against ambition — it is an argument against the specific, corrosive habit of risking what matters for what doesn't.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The single greatest risk to your financial life is not knowing when you have enough.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Compounding and the power of time$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel uses the arc of a famous investor's career to make a point that is easy to state and hard to internalize: the overwhelming majority of that fortune was built not from an unusually high annual return, but from an unusually long runway of decades during which ordinary returns were allowed to compound uninterrupted. Skill explains part of the story; time explains far more of it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Compounding$$, 'body', $$Growth that builds on itself — returns earning returns on previously earned returns — so that the majority of the eventual value accumulates very late in the process, after decades of unspectacular growth quietly stack on top of each other.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Compounding is counterintuitive precisely because its curve looks flat for a long time and then bends sharply upward near the end, which means the most important financial skill it rewards is not brilliance — it is endurance. The investors who lose out on compounding are rarely the ones who pick the wrong assets; more often they are the ones who interrupt a decent process by panic-selling, chasing something flashier, or simply running out of patience before the curve bends.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The implication Housel draws is that a merely good strategy you can stick with for decades will beat a supposedly optimal strategy you abandon after a few bad years — good investing is less about maximizing returns and more about earning pretty good returns you can sustain for the longest period of time.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Wealth built through compounding comes far more from time left uninterrupted than from any single brilliant decision.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Getting wealthy vs. staying wealthy$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel separates two skills that are often confused: getting money and keeping it. Getting money usually requires taking risks, being optimistic, and putting yourself out there. Keeping money requires almost the opposite set of traits — humility, fear that what you've made can be taken away, and frugality, an acceptance that at least some of what you made was luck, so past success is no guarantee it will repeat.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Because these are different skills, someone excellent at building wealth is not automatically good at preserving it, and the historical graveyard of investors and entrepreneurs is full of people who made a fortune with bold bets and then lost it with equally bold bets that didn't pay off. Survival, not brilliance, is what allows compounding to keep working across the long run.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Room for error$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The practical tool Housel offers for staying wealthy is deliberately planning for your own plans to be wrong: building slack into savings, spending, and investment assumptions so that a single miscalculation, one bad year, or an unlucky surprise does not force you out of the game entirely. He calls this a margin of safety applied to behavior and psychology, not just to a spreadsheet — room to be wrong without being ruined.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Building wealth and keeping it require almost opposite skills, and only the second one lets compounding run for decades.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Tails, you win: why a few decisions drive most outcomes$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel points out that in investing, as in venture capital and many other fields, a small number of outsized winners typically account for the majority of total returns, while most individual decisions turn out mediocre, wrong, or simply unremarkable. A venture fund can be a triumph even if most of its bets fail, so long as one or two investments return enough to cover everything else many times over.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Tail events$$, 'body', $$The small number of extreme outcomes — a handful of enormous winners, a handful of catastrophic losers — that end up accounting for a disproportionate share of total results, even though most individual events cluster around average.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The psychological consequence is that it is entirely normal, even for skilled investors, to be wrong most of the time and still do extremely well overall, because you only need a few things to go right, spectacularly, to offset a long list of things that went nowhere. This reframes what "being wrong" even means in a financial context — a portfolio full of individually disappointing bets can still be a great portfolio if it stayed in the game long enough to catch its few big winners.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$It also explains why judging any individual decision by its outcome alone is misleading: a good decision that happened to lose is not automatically a bad decision, and a reckless one that happened to pay off is not automatically a good one. What matters is the process and the odds behind the decision, not just how that one instance turned out.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Most individual financial decisions will be mediocre or wrong — what matters is staying in the game long enough for the rare big winners to show up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Freedom: the highest dividend money pays$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel argues that the highest form of wealth is not a possession or a lifestyle but control over your own time — the ability to wake up and decide what to do with your day, who to spend it with, and when to stop, without asking anyone's permission. Money's greatest intrinsic value, in this framing, is its capacity to give you that control.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Freedom (control over time)$$, 'body', $$The ability to do what you want, when you want, with whom you want, for as long as you want — the specific dividend Housel argues money's real value is measured against, above any particular purchase or possession.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He contrasts this with the more visible, easier-to-photograph markers of wealth — houses, cars, vacations — which often buy status or comfort but not autonomy, and can even reduce autonomy if they come with debt, upkeep, or the pressure to maintain appearances. A person with a modest lifestyle and a large cushion of savings may hold more real financial freedom than someone with a much larger income who has no slack at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This reframing matters because it gives a concrete answer to the vague question "why save money at all." The answer is not a specific future purchase; it is the optionality itself — the ability to change jobs, weather a bad year, take a risk, or simply say no to something you don't want to do, all of which quietly compound into a better life independent of any single dollar amount.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The real return money pays is control over your own time, not any particular thing it can buy.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Wealth is what you don't see$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel draws a sharp line between being rich and being wealthy. Rich is visible income — the car, the watch, the vacation photos — and it is easy to spot because it has already been converted into something you can see. Wealth is the opposite: it is the income that was not converted into stuff, the assets sitting unspent and largely invisible to everyone, including sometimes to the wealthy person's own neighbors.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Rich vs. wealthy$$, 'body', $$Rich describes visible current income and spending — what people can see you buy. Wealthy describes unspent financial assets — income deliberately not converted into visible things, and therefore hidden from view.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This creates a systematic illusion: because wealth is, by definition, the money not spent on visible signals, we mostly learn about how to build wealth by studying people who look rich, which is close to the opposite of useful information. Someone flashing obvious signs of wealth may simply be spending aggressively against a much smaller cushion than someone living modestly beside them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The behavioral trap this sets is that spending money to show people how much money you have is often the fastest way to end up with less of it, because every dollar converted into a visible signal is a dollar that stops compounding. Housel's point is not that visible spending is wrong, only that it should be a deliberate choice weighed against the quieter, invisible alternative of simply keeping the option open.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Wealth is the money you didn't spend, which means it is, by definition, invisible — including to you.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Save without a reason, and respect the cost of admission$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Saving without a specific goal$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel argues for a form of saving that does not require a justification — not saving specifically for a house, a car, or retirement, but saving simply to create a buffer against a future you cannot predict. Building savings tied only to a specific known goal leaves you unprotected against the far larger category of expenses and shocks you never saw coming, which is most of what actually happens in a life.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He connects this to flexibility as an asset in its own right: a person with unallocated savings can take a job opportunity that requires short-term income loss, ride out an unexpected medical bill, or simply wait for a bad market to pass rather than being forced to sell into it. The value of that flexibility is real even though it never shows up as a return on any statement.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$The cost of admission$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel's other major reframe concerns volatility itself. He argues that market declines, unpredictability, and the general discomfort of investing are not a fine to be avoided by the clever — they are the entry price paid for the long-run gains that markets have historically delivered. Treating volatility as an unexpected penalty, rather than as the expected cost of participating, is what causes people to bail out at exactly the wrong moments.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Framed this way, a market crash is less a sign something has gone wrong and more a routine bill coming due for a service that, historically, has been worth paying for — the fee, not the fine.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Save for no specific reason to buy flexibility, and treat market volatility as the price of admission, not a penalty for a mistake.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Reasonable over rational, and the danger of extrapolating your own experience$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel argues that the textbook-optimal financial decision, calculated coldly, is often not the decision a real person can actually sustain, and a merely reasonable decision you can live with is worth more in practice than a mathematically superior one you will abandon under stress. Paying off a low-interest mortgage early may not be the optimal use of capital on a spreadsheet, but if it lets someone sleep at night and stick to the rest of their plan, the emotional return is a real return.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Reasonable over rational$$, 'body', $$Choosing the financial strategy you can realistically stick with through good years and bad, even if a purely rational, textbook-optimal alternative technically exists — because a good plan you abandon under stress is worth less than an imperfect plan you keep.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He pairs this with a separate observation about why people disagree so sharply about money in the first place: each of us forms our core beliefs about risk, saving, and investing from the narrow, specific slice of economic history we happened to live through — the inflation of our childhood, the market crash we watched our parents survive or not survive, the decade we entered the workforce. Two intelligent, well-informed people can look at identical facts and reach opposite conclusions, not because one is wrong, but because their lived experience of money differs.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This has a humbling implication: nobody is crazy. Decisions that look irrational from the outside are usually internally consistent given the information and experience the person making them actually had. Housel's advice is to hold your own financial convictions a little more loosely, and to judge other people's money decisions with the assumption that they are working from a different, not necessarily worse, set of lived experiences than your own.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A reasonable plan you can actually live with beats an optimal plan you cannot, and everyone's sense of risk is shaped by the narrow slice of history they happened to live through.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Confessions: how Housel manages his own money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel closes the book by turning the lens on himself, describing the actual, fairly unglamorous financial habits he and his wife follow — a deliberately high savings rate, a paid-off home, broad and boring low-cost index investments rather than picking individual stocks, and a strong preference for financial independence over any particular lifestyle upgrade.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He is explicit that none of this is presented as the objectively optimal strategy, and he is not urging readers to copy it line for line. It is presented instead as a working example of the book's core argument in practice: a plan chosen to fit his own temperament, his own tolerance for risk and regret, and his own definition of enough, rather than a plan optimized on paper for someone else's circumstances.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The closing chapter is really a demonstration of the book's central claim turned inward — that the right financial plan is a personal one, built around behavior you can sustain and a version of enough you actually believe, not a universal formula lifted from someone else's spreadsheet.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Housel's own financial habits are offered not as a template to copy but as one honest example of building a plan around personal temperament rather than theoretical optimality.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Psychology of Money is written as a collection of accessible, story-driven essays rather than a rigorous academic text, and that shapes both its appeal and its limits. Read it with the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Its stories illustrate ideas, they don't prove them.$$, 'body', $$Housel builds nearly every chapter around a single vivid, real anecdote — a janitor, a hedge fund manager, a famous investor's career arc. These stories are memorable and genuinely clarifying, but a single story is an illustration, not statistical evidence, and the book cites relatively little rigorous behavioral-economics research compared to how much weight the anecdotes carry.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It is written from a comfortable, Western, market-access vantage point.$$, 'body', $$The book assumes a reader who has meaningful disposable income, access to functioning stock markets and retirement accounts, and a degree of financial slack to begin with. Its advice on saving, index investing, and building "room for error" is much harder to apply for someone living paycheck to paycheck or without access to formal financial markets at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$"Just spend less than you earn" underweights genuine constraints.$$, 'body', $$Housel's frugality-focused advice works best for readers who have real discretionary spending to cut. For readers whose income barely or does not cover basic needs, the gap between income and expenses is not primarily a behavioral problem to fix with better habits — it is a structural one, and the book has comparatively little to say about that situation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Its examples and market history are heavily U.S.-centric.$$, 'body', $$The famous investor, the specific market crashes, and the decades of stock-market returns referenced throughout are almost entirely American. Readers in Laos and the broader region face a very different financial landscape — a much smaller and less liquid local equity market, less developed retirement-account infrastructure, and savings and investment norms shaped more by land, gold, family businesses, and informal lending circles than by index funds.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It has comparatively little to say about family-based and informal safety nets.$$, 'body', $$The book's picture of financial security centers on individual savings and personal investment accounts. In contexts where extended family support, community lending, or informal social obligations play a large role in how people actually weather financial shocks, the book's framework of purely individual "room for error" captures only part of the real picture.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Some of its behavioral claims lean on plausibility more than direct citation.$$, 'body', $$Concepts like "the moving goalpost of enough" or "reasonable over rational" are compelling and consistent with broader behavioral-economics findings, but Housel often asserts them through narrative logic rather than pointing to a specific study behind each individual claim. Treat them as well-argued observations, not settled science.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's real contribution stands: a set of durable, well-told reminders that financial outcomes are driven by behavior, humility about luck and risk, and a personal sense of enough — not by finding a cleverer formula. Read it as a lens for examining your own money behavior, and adapt its specific advice to the market access, income constraints, and family structures of your own life rather than importing it wholesale.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ເປັນຫຍັງຄວາມສຳເລັດດ້ານການເງິນຈຶ່ງເປັນທັກສະດ້ານພຶດຕິກຳ ບໍ່ແມ່ນວິຊາການລ້ວນໆ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນດ້ວຍການສັງເກດແບບກົງໄປກົງມາ: ການບໍລິຫານເງິນໃຫ້ດີ ບໍ່ໄດ້ກ່ຽວຂ້ອງກັບຄວາມສະຫຼາດຫຼາຍປານໃດ. ລາວຍົກຕົວຢ່າງຄົນທີ່ບໍ່ມີການສຶກສາດ້ານການເງິນ ແຕ່ສາມາດສ້າງຄວາມຮັ່ງມີໄດ້ຢ່າງງຽບໆ ດ້ວຍຄວາມອົດທົນ ແລະ ການຄວບຄຸມຕົນເອງ ຄຽງຄູ່ກັບຜູ້ຊ່ຽວຊານທີ່ສະຫຼາດ ແລະ ມີການສຶກສາສູງ ແຕ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຖານະການເງິນຂອງຕົນລົ້ມລົງ ເພາະຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈເກີນໄປ ແລະ ຄວາມໃຈຮ້ອນ. ຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງລະຫວ່າງທັງສອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮູ້ — ແຕ່ແມ່ນພຶດຕິກຳ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ສຳຄັນ ເພາະການສຶກສາດ້ານການເງິນສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ຖືກສອນຄືກັບວ່າເງິນເປັນໂຈດເລກ: ຮຽນສູດທີ່ຖືກຕ້ອງ, ຄິດໄລ່ຕົວເລກທີ່ຖືກຕ້ອງ ແລ້ວຜົນລັບທີ່ດີກໍ່ຈະຕາມມາ. Housel ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າແນວຄິດນີ້ຜິດເປັນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່. ການຕັດສິນໃຈກ່ຽວກັບເງິນ ຖືກເຮັດໂດຍຄົນທຳມະດາທີ່ມີອັດຕາຕົວຕົນ, ພາລະປະຫວັດສາດ, ຄວາມກົດດັນຈາກຄອບຄົວ ແລະ ຂໍ້ມູນທີ່ບໍ່ຄົບຖ້ວນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຄື່ອງຄິດເລກທີ່ເປັນກາງໆຢູ່ໃນສູນຍາກາດ. ແຜນການເງິນທີ່ບໍ່ສົນໃຈຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກ ແລະ ພຶດຕິກຳຂອງຄົນຈິງໆພາຍໃຕ້ຄວາມກົດດັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແຜນທີ່ໃຊ້ໄດ້ຈິງເລີຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ໂຄງສ້າງຂອງປຶ້ມກໍ່ມາຈາກຫຼັກການນີ້. ແທນທີ່ຈະສ້າງທິດສະດີໜຶ່ງດຽວທີ່ລວມທຸກຢ່າງ Housel ນຳສະເໜີເລື່ອງສັ້ນໆ ທີ່ເປັນເອກະລາດຈາກກັນສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ແຕ່ລະເລື່ອງແຍກອອກມາໃຫ້ເຫັນແນວໂນ້ມທາງຈິດຕະວິທະຍາອັນດຽວ — ວິທີທີ່ເຮົາຄິດເລື່ອງຄວາມສ່ຽງ, ໂຊກ, ຄວາມໂລບ ຫຼື ຄວາມພໍດີ — ເພາະລາວເຊື່ອວ່າ ລັກສະນະທີ່ຫຍຸ້ງຍາກ ແລະ ເປັນສ່ວນຕົວຂອງເລື່ອງເງິນ ຖືກສະທ້ອນອອກມາໄດ້ດີກວ່າຜ່ານເລື່ອງເລົ່າ ຫຼາຍກວ່າສູດຄຳນວນ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ຄວາມສຳເລັດດ້ານການເງິນ ບໍ່ໄດ້ຂຶ້ນກັບຄວາມສະຫຼາດ ແຕ່ຂຶ້ນກັບພຶດຕິກຳຂອງທ່ານທັງໝົດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງ: ພະລັງທີ່ເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຢູ່ເບື້ອງຫຼັງທຸກຜົນລັບ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel ເລີ່ມດ້ວຍບຸກຄົນສອງຄົນທີ່ຊີວິດການເງິນຂອງພວກເຂົາຖືກກຳນົດໂດຍພະລັງທີ່ຢູ່ນອກເໜືອການຄວບຄຸມເປັນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່: ຄົນໜຶ່ງມີໂຊກອັນມະຫາສານພາໃຫ້ຮັ່ງມີ, ອີກຄົນມີຄວາມສ່ຽງອັນມະຫາສານພາໃຫ້ລົ້ມລົງ. ຈຸດສຳຄັນຂອງລາວບໍ່ແມ່ນວ່າຄວາມສາມາດບໍ່ມີຄວາມໝາຍ — ແຕ່ໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງແມ່ນຄູ່ແຝດກັນ ທັງສອງເປັນຜົນຈາກຄວາມຈິງທີ່ວ່າ ທຸກຜົນລັບໃນຊີວິດຖືກນຳພາໂດຍພະລັງອື່ນທີ່ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມພະຍາຍາມສ່ວນຕົວ ແລະ ທັງສອງຢ່າງກໍ່ວັດແທກຈາກພາຍນອກໄດ້ຍາກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງ (Luck and risk)$$, 'body', $$ສອງດ້ານຂອງເຫຼຍນອັນດຽວກັນ: ບົດບາດທີ່ເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຂອງພະລັງທີ່ຢູ່ນອກເໜືອການຄວບຄຸມຂອງບຸກຄົນ — ດີ ແລະ ບໍ່ດີ — ໃນທຸກຜົນລັບການເງິນ. ເນື່ອງຈາກມັນເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນ ຈຶ່ງງ່າຍທີ່ຈະໃຫ້ຄວາມດີຄວາມຄອບແກ່ຄວາມສາມາດຫຼາຍເກີນໄປສຳລັບຜົນລັບດີ ແລະ ໂທດການຕັດສິນໃຈຜິດຫຼາຍເກີນໄປສຳລັບຜົນລັບບໍ່ດີ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອັນຕະລາຍໃນທາງປະຕິບັດມີສອງດ້ານ. ທຳອິດ ເຮົາມັກສຶກສາຄວາມສຳເລັດອັນສຸດຂີດ ແລະ ຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວອັນສຸດຂີດແບບຕົງໆເກີນໄປ ລອກແບບການເຄື່ອນໄຫວແທ້ໆຂອງຄົນສຳເລັດຢ່າງລົ້ນເຫຼືອ ຫຼື ຕຳໜິການເຄື່ອນໄຫວແທ້ໆຂອງຄົນລົ້ມເຫຼວຢ່າງລົ້ນເຫຼືອ ໂດຍບໍ່ຄິດເຖິງວ່າໂຊກ ຫຼື ຄວາມສ່ຽງມີສ່ວນຫຼາຍປານໃດໃນແຕ່ລະເລື່ອງ. ອັນທີສອງ ຄວາມບອດອັນດຽວກັນນີ້ ເຮັດໃຫ້ເຮົາເປັນຜູ້ຕັດສິນທີ່ໂຫດຮ້າຍຕໍ່ການຕັດສິນໃຈຂອງຄົນອື່ນ ແລະ ຄວາມຜິດພາດໃນອະດີດຂອງຕົນເອງ ເພາະຍາກທີ່ຈະບອກໄດ້ໃນພາຍຫຼັງວ່າ ທາງເລືອກໃດແທ້ໆບໍ່ດີ ແລະ ທາງເລືອກໃດພຽງແຕ່ໂຊກບໍ່ດີ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄຳຕອບທີ່ Housel ສະເໜີ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມນິ້ງງັນ ແຕ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຖ່ອມຕົນ: ໃຫ້ຄວາມສຳຄັນໜ້ອຍລົງກັບການລະບຸຜູ້ຊະນະ ຫຼື ຜູ້ແພ້ສະເພາະຄົນເພື່ອລອກແບບ ຫຼື ຫຼີກລ່ຽງ ແລະ ໃຫ້ຄວາມສຳຄັນຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນກັບຮູບແບບກວ້າງໆທີ່ໃຊ້ໄດ້ກັບຜົນລັບຫຼາຍໆອັນ ພ້ອມທັງສ້າງພື້ນທີ່ພຽງພໍໃນແຜນຂອງຕົນເອງ ເພື່ອບໍ່ໃຫ້ໂຊກບໍ່ດີພຽງຢ່າງດຽວທຳລາຍທຸກຢ່າງໄດ້. ການເຄົາລົບຄວາມສ່ຽງໝາຍເຖິງການສົມມຸດວ່າ ສ່ວນໜຶ່ງຂອງຜົນລັບໃດໜຶ່ງ — ບໍ່ວ່າຈະເປັນຂອງທ່ານ ຫຼື ຂອງໃຜກໍ່ຕາມ — ມາຈາກສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນທັງໝົດ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ທຸກຜົນລັບການເງິນຖືກກຳນົດໂດຍໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງພໍໆກັບຄວາມສາມາດ ແລະ ທັງສອງເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຈາກພາຍນອກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ບໍ່ເຄີຍພໍ: ເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຍ້າຍໄປເລື້ອຍໆ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Housel ເລົ່າເລື່ອງຜູ້ບໍລິຫານກອງທຶນເຮັດຈ໌ (hedge fund) ຄົນໜຶ່ງ ຜູ້ທີ່ຫາເງິນໄດ້ຫຼາຍກວ່າທີ່ຄອບຄົວຂອງລາວຈະໃຊ້ໝົດໄດ້ຫຼາຍລຸ້ນ ແຕ່ກໍ່ຍັງກໍ່ອາຊະຍາກຳໄລ່ຫາຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນອີກ — ສ່ຽງຕິດຄຸກເພື່ອຈຳນວນເງິນທີ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງແລ້ວດີຂຶ້ນຢ່າງມີຄວາມໝາຍເລີຍ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ບໍ່ແມ່ນເລື່ອງຄວາມໂລບແບບກາຕູນ ແຕ່ແມ່ນຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວທຳມະດາຫຼາຍກວ່າ ໃນການຮັບຮູ້ວ່າ ຕົນເອງໄດ້ພໍແລ້ວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ບໍ່ເຄີຍພໍ (Never enough)$$, 'body', $$ແນວໂນ້ມທີ່ເປົ້າໝາຍຂອງຄຳວ່າ "ພໍແລ້ວ" ບໍ່ວ່າຈະເປັນເງິນ, ສະຖານະພາບ ຫຼື ຊັບສິນ ຈະຍ້າຍອອກໄປໄກກວ່າເດີມທຸກຄັ້ງທີ່ໄປເຖິງ ເຮັດໃຫ້ຄວາມພໍໃຈບໍ່ເຄີຍມາເຖິງແທ້ໆ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະສະສົມໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດກໍ່ຕາມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອັນຕະລາຍຂອງການບໍ່ມີແນວຄິດເລື່ອງ "ພໍແລ້ວ" Housel ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ມັນຊັກຈູງໃຫ້ຄົນຮັບເອົາຄວາມສ່ຽງທີ່ອັນຕະລາຍທີ່ສຸດອັນດຽວໃນການເງິນສ່ວນຕົວ: ການສ່ຽງສິ່ງທີ່ຕົນມີ ແລະ ຕ້ອງການ ເພື່ອສິ່ງທີ່ຕົນບໍ່ມີ ແລະ ບໍ່ໄດ້ຕ້ອງການແທ້ໆ ພຽງເພື່ອຕົວເລກທີ່ສູງຂຶ້ນ. ການປຽບທຽບກັບສັງຄົມເຮັດໃຫ້ຮ້າຍແຮງຂຶ້ນ — ຈະມີຄົນທີ່ມີຫຼາຍກວ່າຢູ່ສະເໝີ ດັ່ງນັ້ນເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ກຳນົດໂດຍປຽບທຽບກັບຄົນອື່ນ ຈຶ່ງບໍ່ມີວັນໄປເຖິງໄດ້ ມີແຕ່ໄລ່ຕາມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຈຸດອີງແທ້ໆຂອງລາວ ຄື ໃຫ້ກຳນົດຄວາມໝາຍຂອງ "ພໍແລ້ວ" ສຳລັບຕົນເອງລ່ວງໜ້າ ຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ແທນທີ່ຈະປ່ອຍໃຫ້ມັນຄ່ອຍໆສູງຂຶ້ນເອງທຸກຄັ້ງທີ່ໄດ້ຂຶ້ນເງິນເດືອນ ຫຼື ໄດ້ເງິນກ້ອນໃຫຍ່. ລາວລະມັດລະວັງທີ່ຈະຊີ້ແຈງວ່ານີ້ບໍ່ແມ່ນການໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຄວາມມີຄວາມມຸ່ງໝັ້ນ — ແຕ່ແມ່ນການໂຕ້ແຍ້ງນິໄສທີ່ເສື່ອມຊາມສະເພາະ ຄືການສ່ຽງສິ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນ ເພື່ອສິ່ງທີ່ບໍ່ສຳຄັນ.$$)
