  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງ (Luck and risk)$$, 'body', $$ບົດບາດທີ່ເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຂອງພະລັງທີ່ຢູ່ນອກເໜືອການຄວບຄຸມ ໃນທຸກຜົນລັບການເງິນ ຊຶ່ງງ່າຍທີ່ຈະຖືກມອງຂ້າມຈາກພາຍນອກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ບໍ່ເຄີຍພໍ (Never enough)$$, 'body', $$ແນວໂນ້ມທີ່ເປົ້າໝາຍຂອງ "ພໍແລ້ວ" ຈະຍ້າຍອອກໄປໄກກວ່າເດີມທຸກຄັ້ງທີ່ໄປເຖິງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ດອກເບ້ຍທົບຕົ້ນ (Compounding)$$, 'body', $$ການເຕີບໂຕທີ່ສ້າງໃສ່ການເຕີບໂຕທີ່ໄດ້ມາກ່ອນ ໂດຍມູນຄ່າສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ໃນທີ່ສຸດ ມາເຖິງຊ້າ ຫຼັງຈາກຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດຂອງການສະສົມແບບງຽບໆ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ພື້ນທີ່ໃຫ້ຜິດພາດ (Room for error)$$, 'body', $$ການວາງແຜນຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ໂດຍຄາດວ່າສົມມຸດຖານຂອງຕົນເອງອາດຜິດ ເພື່ອບໍ່ໃຫ້ປີບໍ່ດີໜຶ່ງປີ ຫຼືຄວາມຄິດຄຳນວນຜິດພາດ ບັງຄັບໃຫ້ອອກຈາກເກມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ເຫດການດ້ານທ້າຍ (Tail events)$$, 'body', $$ຈຳນວນນ້ອຍໆຂອງຜົນລັບອັນສຸດຂີດ ທີ່ໃນທີ່ສຸດແລ້ວກາຍເປັນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຂອງຜົນລັບລວມທັງໝົດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ລວຍ ທຽບກັບ ຮັ່ງມີ$$, 'body', $$ລວຍ ຄືການໃຊ້ຈ່າຍປັດຈຸບັນທີ່ເບິ່ງເຫັນໄດ້; ຮັ່ງມີ ຄືຊັບສິນທາງການເງິນທີ່ຍັງບໍ່ໄດ້ໃຊ້ ຊຶ່ງເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນໂດຍທຳມະຊາດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ສົມເຫດສົມຜົນ ດີກວ່າ ມີເຫດຜົນລ້ວນໆ$$, 'body', $$ການເລືອກຍຸດທະສາດການເງິນທີ່ຮັກສາໄດ້ຕົວຈິງ ຜ່ານປີດີ ແລະ ປີບໍ່ດີ ດີກວ່າທາງເລືອກທີ່ດີກວ່າຕາມທິດສະດີ ແຕ່ອາດຖືກປະຖິ້ມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ລາຄາເຂົ້າຮ່ວມ (Cost of admission)$$, 'body', $$ການຖືວ່າຄວາມຜັນຜວນ ແລະ ການຕົກຂອງຕະຫຼາດ ຄືລາຄາປົກກະຕິທີ່ຈ່າຍເພື່ອຜົນຕອບແທນໄລຍະຍາວ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄ່າປັບທີ່ບໍ່ຄາດຄິດ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Where in your own life has luck or risk played a bigger role in an outcome than you've been willing to admit — either a success you overcredited to skill, or a failure you overcredited to a bad decision?$$, $$Housel says the most dangerous risk is not knowing when you have enough. Have you actually defined what "enough" looks like for you, or is your goalpost still quietly moving?$$, $$Housel's own plan — high savings, a paid-off home, boring index funds — reflects his particular temperament and market access. What would a version of "reasonable over rational" look like for your specific circumstances, risk tolerance, and access to markets?$$, $$The book assumes meaningful disposable income and functioning stock markets. If those assumptions don't fully hold for you, which of its ideas still transfer, and which need to be rebuilt around family support, informal savings, or a smaller local market?$$, $$Housel argues wealth is invisible by definition. Who in your life do you assume is doing well financially based only on what you can see them spend, and how confident are you that assumption is actually correct?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ໃນຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານເອງ ໂຊກ ຫຼື ຄວາມສ່ຽງມີບົດບາດຫຼາຍກວ່າທີ່ທ່ານຍອມຮັບຢູ່ບ່ອນໃດ — ບໍ່ວ່າຈະເປັນຄວາມສຳເລັດທີ່ທ່ານໃຫ້ຄວາມດີຄວາມຊອບແກ່ຄວາມສາມາດຫຼາຍເກີນໄປ ຫຼືຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວທີ່ທ່ານໂທດການຕັດສິນໃຈຜິດຫຼາຍເກີນໄປ?$$, $$Housel ບອກວ່າ ຄວາມສ່ຽງທີ່ອັນຕະລາຍທີ່ສຸດ ຄືການບໍ່ຮູ້ວ່າຕົນເອງພໍແລ້ວເມື່ອໃດ. ທ່ານໄດ້ກຳນົດຄວາມໝາຍຂອງ "ພໍແລ້ວ" ສຳລັບຕົນເອງແທ້ໆບໍ ຫຼືເປົ້າໝາຍຂອງທ່ານຍັງຄ່ອຍໆຍ້າຍໄປຢູ່ຢ່າງງຽບໆ?$$, $$ແຜນການເງິນຂອງ Housel ເອງ — ອອມສູງ, ບ້ານຊຳລະໝົດ, ກອງທຶນດັດຊະນີທີ່ໜ້າເບື່ອ — ສະທ້ອນອຸປະນິໄສ ແລະ ການເຂົ້າເຖິງຕະຫຼາດສະເພາະຂອງລາວ. ສະບັບ "ສົມເຫດສົມຜົນດີກວ່າມີເຫດຜົນລ້ວນໆ" ຂອງທ່ານເອງຈະເປັນແນວໃດ ໃຫ້ເໝາະກັບສະຖານະການ, ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ແລະ ການເຂົ້າເຖິງຕະຫຼາດຂອງທ່ານ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມສົມມຸດວ່າມີລາຍໄດ້ສ່ວນເກີນທີ່ມີຄວາມໝາຍ ແລະ ຕະຫຼາດຫຸ້ນທີ່ເຮັດວຽກໄດ້. ຖ້າສົມມຸດຖານເຫຼົ່ານັ້ນບໍ່ກົງກັບຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານທັງໝົດ ແນວຄິດອັນໃດຍັງນຳໃຊ້ໄດ້ ແລະ ອັນໃດຕ້ອງສ້າງໃໝ່ ອ້ອມຮອບການສະໜັບສະໜູນຈາກຄອບຄົວ, ການອອມແບບບໍ່ເປັນທາງການ ຫຼືຕະຫຼາດທ້ອງຖິ່ນທີ່ນ້ອຍກວ່າ?$$, $$Housel ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຕາມນິຍາມ. ໃຜໃນຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານທີ່ທ່ານສົມມຸດວ່າຖານະການເງິນດີ ໂດຍອີງໃສ່ພຽງແຕ່ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຫັນເຂົາເຈົ້າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ແລະ ທ່ານໝັ້ນໃຈປານໃດວ່າ ຂໍ້ສົມມຸດນັ້ນຖືກຕ້ອງແທ້ໆ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Doing well with money is a matter of behavior, not intelligence — ordinary people who behave well often outperform brilliant people who don't.$$, $$Luck and risk are invisible siblings behind every financial outcome; be careful copying winners or condemning losers too literally.$$, $$Not knowing when you have enough is the most dangerous financial risk, because it pushes people to risk what they need for what they don't.$$, $$Compounding rewards time far more than it rewards skill — a long, uninterrupted runway matters more than a spectacular annual return.$$, $$Getting wealthy and staying wealthy require nearly opposite skills; survival, not brilliance, is what lets compounding keep working.$$, $$A small number of tail events drive most investment returns, so being wrong often is normal as long as you stay in the game.$$, $$The highest dividend money pays is control over your own time, not any specific thing it can buy.$$, $$Wealth is the money you don't spend, which makes it invisible by definition — rich is what you see, wealthy is what you don't.$$, $$Save without a specific reason to buy flexibility, and treat market volatility as the cost of admission, not a penalty.$$, $$A reasonable plan you can stick with beats an optimal plan you abandon, and everyone's risk tolerance is shaped by the narrow slice of history they lived through.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ຄວາມສຳເລັດດ້ານການເງິນ ແມ່ນເລື່ອງພຶດຕິກຳ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມສະຫຼາດ — ຄົນທຳມະດາທີ່ມີພຶດຕິກຳດີ ມັກເຮັດໄດ້ດີກວ່າຄົນສະຫຼາດແຕ່ພຶດຕິກຳບໍ່ດີ.$$, $$ໂຊກ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ເປັນຄູ່ແຝດທີ່ເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຢູ່ເບື້ອງຫຼັງທຸກຜົນລັບການເງິນ ຈົ່ງລະມັດລະວັງໃນການລອກແບບຜູ້ຊະນະ ຫຼື ຕັດສິນຜູ້ແພ້ຫຼາຍເກີນໄປ.$$, $$ການບໍ່ຮູ້ວ່າຕົນເອງພໍແລ້ວເມື່ອໃດ ຄືຄວາມສ່ຽງທາງການເງິນທີ່ອັນຕະລາຍທີ່ສຸດ ເພາະມັນຊັກຈູງໃຫ້ຄົນສ່ຽງສິ່ງທີ່ຕ້ອງການ ເພື່ອສິ່ງທີ່ບໍ່ຕ້ອງການ.$$, $$ດອກເບ້ຍທົບຕົ້ນໃຫ້ລາງວັນຕໍ່ເວລາຫຼາຍກວ່າຄວາມສາມາດ — ໄລຍະທາງທີ່ຍາວ ແລະ ບໍ່ຂາດຕອນ ສຳຄັນກວ່າຜົນຕອບແທນປະຈຳປີທີ່ໂດດເດັ່ນ.$$, $$ການສ້າງຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ ແລະ ການຮັກສາມັນ ຕ້ອງການທັກສະທີ່ກົງກັນຂ້າມເກືອບໝົດ — ການຢູ່ລອດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມສະຫຼາດ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ຮັກສາການທົບຕົ້ນໃຫ້ດຳເນີນຕໍ່ໄປ.$$, $$ເຫດການດ້ານທ້າຍຈຳນວນນ້ອຍ ຄືຕົວກຳນົດຜົນຕອບແທນການລົງທຶນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ ດັ່ງນັ້ນການຜິດເລື້ອຍໆຈຶ່ງເປັນເລື່ອງທຳມະດາ ຕາບໃດທີ່ຍັງຢູ່ໃນເກມ.$$, $$ຜົນຕອບແທນສູງສຸດທີ່ເງິນຈ່າຍໃຫ້ ຄືການຄວບຄຸມເວລາຂອງຕົນເອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນສິ່ງໃດໜຶ່ງທີ່ມັນຊື້ໄດ້.$$, $$ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ ຄືເງິນທີ່ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ໃຊ້ ຊຶ່ງເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນຕາມນິຍາມ — ລວຍຄືສິ່ງທີ່ເຫັນ ຮັ່ງມີຄືສິ່ງທີ່ບໍ່ເຫັນ.$$, $$ອອມໂດຍບໍ່ມີເຫດຜົນສະເພາະ ເພື່ອຊື້ຄວາມຢືດຢຸ່ນ ແລະ ຖືວ່າຄວາມຜັນຜວນຂອງຕະຫຼາດ ຄືລາຄາເຂົ້າຮ່ວມ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄ່າປັບ.$$, $$ແຜນທີ່ສົມເຫດສົມຜົນທີ່ຮັກສາໄດ້ ຊະນະແຜນທີ່ດີທີ່ສຸດແຕ່ຮັກສາບໍ່ໄດ້ ແລະ ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງຂອງແຕ່ລະຄົນ ຖືກກຳນົດໂດຍປະຫວັດສາດແຄບໆທີ່ຕົນເອງເຄີຍຜ່ານມາ.$$]
where book_id in ('1973dcdf-a04a-41ea-a626-1462b37b8e1c', '0e8c4061-3a87-4ff7-99cd-2b907743cab1');

-- Rich Dad Poor Dad — Robert Kiyosaki
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A personal-finance classic that reframes wealth around assets, liabilities, and financial literacy.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ປຶ້ມການເງິນຄລາສສິກ ທີ່ອະທິບາຍຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຜ່ານຊັບສິນ, ໜີ້ສິນ ແລະ ຄວາມຮູ້ດ້ານການເງິນ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Robert Kiyosaki builds his argument as a memoir, contrasting the money lessons of two father figures — his own highly educated but financially struggling father, and his best friend's entrepreneur father — to make a simple, provocative point: schools teach people to work for money, but almost nobody teaches people how to make money work for them. This lesson walks through the book's core distinctions between assets and liabilities, its argument for building a personal asset column outside your job, its case for financial education as a learnable skill, and the specific caveats worth holding onto given how much of the book rests on one man's remembered parables rather than verifiable case studies.$$,
  deck_lo = $$Robert Kiyosaki ສ້າງຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງລາວໃນຮູບແບບບັນທຶກຊີວິດ ໂດຍປຽບທຽບບົດຮຽນເລື່ອງເງິນຈາກພໍ່ສອງຄົນ — ພໍ່ແທ້ຂອງລາວທີ່ຮຽນສູງແຕ່ດີ້ນລົນທາງການເງິນ ແລະ ພໍ່ຂອງໝູ່ຮັກທີ່ເປັນນັກທຸລະກິດສ້າງຕົວເອງ — ເພື່ອສະເໜີຈຸດທີ່ທ້າທາຍແຕ່ງ່າຍໆຄື: ໂຮງຮຽນສອນໃຫ້ຄົນເຮັດວຽກເພື່ອເງິນ ແຕ່ເກືອບບໍ່ມີໃຜສອນວິທີເຮັດໃຫ້ເງິນເຮັດວຽກແທນຄົນ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງລະຫວ່າງຊັບສິນ ແລະ ໜີ້ສິນ, ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງເລື່ອງການສ້າງ "ຖັນຊັບສິນ" ຂອງຕົນເອງນອກເໜືອຈາກວຽກປະຈຳ, ເຫດຜົນທີ່ຄວາມຮູ້ດ້ານການເງິນເປັນທັກສະທີ່ຝຶກໄດ້ ແລະ ຂໍ້ຄວນລະວັງສະເພາະ ເນື່ອງຈາກເນື້ອຫາຈຳນວນຫຼາຍໃນປຶ້ມອີງໃສ່ນິທານທີ່ຜູ້ຂຽນຈື່ໄດ້ ຫຼາຍກວ່າກໍລະນີສຶກສາທີ່ພິສູດໄດ້.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Two dads, two philosophies$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki frames the entire book as a memoir built around two father figures he grew up learning from at the same time. His own father — highly educated, head of the state education department in Hawaii, a man he calls "poor dad" — represented the conventional path: excel in school, get a secure job, climb a career ladder. His best friend's father — a businessman who never finished the eighth grade, but who built a real estate and business empire from nothing, whom he calls "rich dad" — represented a completely different set of instincts about money, risk, and work.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The two men's advice was nearly a mirror image of each other. Poor dad said "study hard so you can find a good company to work for." Rich dad said "study hard so you can find a good company to buy." Poor dad said "I can't afford it." Rich dad forbade that phrase in his presence and insisted on the question "how CAN I afford it?" — a small linguistic swap that, in Kiyosaki's telling, keeps the brain searching for options instead of shutting down.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$It matters for how to read the book that this structure is explicitly a memoir, not a curriculum. The lessons arrive as remembered childhood scenes and reconstructed dialogue from decades earlier, not as a documented case study with verifiable dates, numbers, or independent confirmation that "rich dad" said any particular line, or in some tellings, existed exactly as described. Treat the parable as a vehicle for the ideas, not as evidence for them.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The book runs two opposing money philosophies against each other as lived experience, not as a controlled comparison.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The rich don't work for money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The book's opening lesson is delivered through a deliberately uncomfortable exercise: rich dad puts nine-year-old Kiyosaki to work in his grocery store for a token wage, then — once the boy is hooked on the paycheck and asks for a raise — refuses, and eventually has him work for free. The point is not stinginess; it is to make a young mind feel, physically, what it is like to organize your life around a paycheck, and how a raise only tightens the leash rather than loosening it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki generalizes this into what he calls the rat race: fear of not having enough money pushes people to get a job; then, once the paycheck arrives, desire for more takes over, and people work harder, earn more, spend more to match it, and end up exactly as dependent on that paycheck as before — just at a higher standard of living. The emotions of fear and desire, not any deliberate financial plan, are doing most of the driving.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The rat race$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki's term for the cycle of working for a paycheck, spending it on rising expenses as income rises, and remaining financially dependent on continued employment regardless of how much you earn.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His proposed exit is not to work harder inside the system, but to look past the paycheck at what the job or business is teaching you, and to start directing surplus money toward acquiring things that generate income independent of your own labor — the subject of the next several sections.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A raise does not buy freedom from the paycheck — it usually buys a bigger version of the same dependence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Assets, liabilities, and how the three classes handle cash flow$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki argues that financial literacy does not start with how much money a person makes; it starts with being able to correctly sort what you own into two categories, using a definition simpler than any accountant's textbook.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Asset$$, 'body', $$Anything that puts money into your pocket, whether or not you work — a rental property with positive cash flow, a stock paying dividends, a business that runs without your daily presence, royalties on intellectual property.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Liability$$, 'body', $$Anything that takes money out of your pocket, whether you use it or not — a car loan, credit card balances, and, in Kiyosaki's more provocative framing, often the mortgage and upkeep on the house you live in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$From that split, he sketches three characteristic cash-flow patterns. The poor spend income directly on expenses, with nothing left to redirect anywhere. The middle class earns more but routes much of it into things that look like assets on the surface — a bigger house, a new car, more possessions bought on credit — that are, by his definition, actually liabilities, so the outflow simply grows in step with the income. The rich direct income first into acquiring genuine income-producing assets, and only afterward spend the income those assets generate on lifestyle — so the asset column, not the paycheck, becomes the actual engine of their spending power.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The habit he is arguing for, stripped of the diagrams, is deceptively simple: before buying anything that looks like a reward for earning more, ask whether it will put money into your pocket later or continue taking money out of it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$An asset feeds your pocket, a liability drains it — and Kiyosaki argues most people never sort their own purchases correctly.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Is your house really an asset?$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's most argued-over claim is that, for most owner-occupiers, a primary home is a liability rather than an asset — a claim that directly contradicts the conventional wisdom that buying a house is the cornerstone of building wealth.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His reasoning follows straight from his own definitions. A home you live in does not put money into your pocket; instead it steadily takes money out, through mortgage interest, property taxes, insurance, and maintenance, none of which stop just because the home's paper value might be rising. He also points to an opportunity cost most buyers do not count: money tied up as a down payment and ongoing payments on a home is money that is not being deployed into assets that generate income, so the more house a family buys, the more it can crowd out their ability to build an actual asset column.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He is not telling readers never to own a home — he is careful to frame it as a want, not a rule, and he owned property himself. The instruction is sequencing: build a genuine asset column first, using the income it produces to eventually help pay for the house you want, rather than buying the biggest house you can qualify for first and hoping an asset column happens later.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$By Kiyosaki's own definition, a home you live in is money going out, not in — which is exactly why the claim is so contested.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Mind your own business$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki draws a distinction he considers frequently confused: your profession is not your business. A person can spend an entire career as, say, a bank manager or an engineer, and never build a business of their own — they are, in his phrase, minding someone else's business, growing someone else's asset column, all day.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His advice is to keep the job — the steady paycheck and its benefits are real — while treating the after-hours effort of acquiring income-generating assets as a separate, parallel project. Over years, the asset column compounds independently of whatever happens to the job title.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Real estate that produces cash flow.$$, 'body', $$Property bought to generate rental income, not merely to hope its price rises.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Stocks, bonds, and income-producing paper assets.$$, 'body', $$Ownership stakes or debt instruments that pay dividends or interest without requiring your daily labor.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Businesses that do not require your presence.$$, 'body', $$An operation with systems and other people running it, so it produces income whether or not you personally show up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Royalties and intellectual property.$$, 'body', $$Books, music, patents, and franchises that keep paying out long after the original work that created them is finished.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Your job builds someone else's asset column all day — mind your own business means building yours in parallel.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The history of taxes and the power of corporations$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki includes a potted history of income tax as a rhetorical device: he describes it as originally introduced and sold to the public as a tax that would fall only on the wealthy, before over time being extended to the general population through mechanisms like payroll withholding — his broader point being that once a government mechanism for taking a share of income exists, its reach tends to expand rather than stay contained to its original promise.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$From there he contrasts two orders of operation. An employee typically earns, then pays tax on that income, then spends whatever remains. Kiyosaki argues the wealthy, operating through a corporation, more often earn, spend and invest through the corporate entity in pursuit of generating more income, and pay tax later on a comparatively smaller remaining amount — using the corporation as a legal structure that changes both the order of those steps and what counts as a deductible business expense along the way.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Corporation (as Kiyosaki uses it)$$, 'body', $$A legal entity, separate from the individual who owns it, that the wealthy use both to limit personal liability and to route income and expenses through a different, often more favorable, set of tax and legal rules than an individual employee faces.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He frames financial literacy generally as knowing that these structures exist and are legally available — not as a suggestion that ordinary employees are missing some secret, but as an argument that the legal and tax knowledge itself is a skill schools rarely teach, and one that compounds in value the more assets a person accumulates.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The same legal system offers different tools to an employee and to a corporation — and Kiyosaki argues most people are never taught the second set exists.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The rich invent money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's next claim is that opportunity is not scarce; the ability to recognize and structure it is. He describes financial intelligence as a combination of several distinct competencies working together: reading financial statements accurately, understanding how markets respond to supply and demand, negotiating and structuring deals, and enough legal literacy to use the protections available through corporations and contracts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The recurring example throughout the book is of ordinary-looking opportunities — an undervalued property, a small business with an inefficiency others walked past — that most people do not notice because they have not trained themselves to look, or that they notice but dismiss with an assumption that acting on it is someone else's privilege, not theirs. Kiyosaki's claim is that this pattern-recognition is trainable, not innate, built through repeated deals, mistakes, and deliberate study, in the same way a skill in any other field is built through repetition.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The chapter's sharper, more debatable claim is that money itself is, in a sense, invented by the person who spots and structures the deal — that the deal did not exist as an opportunity until someone's financial intelligence made it visible and workable. It is a motivating frame more than a technical one, and it deliberately downplays how much starting capital, credit access, and tolerance for loss shape who gets to run this pattern-recognition experiment in the first place.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Kiyosaki treats deal-finding as a trainable skill — though the book says comparatively little about who can afford to fail while training it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Work to learn, not to earn$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's advice for early-career decisions inverts the usual filter. Instead of choosing a job for the highest available salary, he argues for choosing it for the range of skills it will force you to build — especially sales, communication, marketing, accounting, and leadership, which he considers the core skill set behind almost any successful business, regardless of industry.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He illustrates this with his own choice, after business school, to take a low-paying sales job at a corporation specifically because he was terrified of selling and public rejection — reasoning that the skill deficit, not the salary, was the more urgent problem to fix early, since a gap in a foundational skill quietly limits every later opportunity that depends on it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He also pushes back on deep specialization for anyone hoping to run their own business rather than be employed by one: a specialist who is excellent at one narrow function but weak in sales, accounting, or managing people ends up structurally dependent on hiring out, or partnering for, the skills they never built — while a generalist who is competent across several core business functions can assemble and run a venture others structurally cannot.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Choosing a job for the skills it teaches, not the salary it pays, is a short-term pay cut Kiyosaki argues compounds for decades.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Overcoming the five obstacles$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki names five internal obstacles he considers the real barrier to building an asset column — more decisive, in his account, than any lack of information, capital, or opportunity.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear.$$, 'body', $$Fear of losing money keeps most people entirely out of the game rather than merely cautious within it. Kiyosaki's counter is not to eliminate fear but to treat calculated loss as a normal, even necessary, part of gaining experience — the same way falling is part of learning to ride a bicycle.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Cynicism.$$, 'body', $$Doubt dressed up as realism — "that could never work," "I don't have the connections," "it's too risky for someone like me" — that talks a person out of ever testing an opportunity in the real world. Kiyosaki's term for this reflex is the "chicken little" mindset, always anticipating the sky is about to fall.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Laziness.$$, 'body', $$Paradoxically, Kiyosaki describes busy people as often the laziest in financial terms — staying constantly occupied with work and errands is, in his account, sometimes a convenient way to avoid the harder, less comfortable work of confronting one's own finances.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Bad habits.$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki argues habits, not one-off decisions, run most of a person's financial life, and that the habit of paying yourself first — setting aside money for investment before paying any other bill — deliberately builds pressure to find the income to cover the rest, rather than leaving investing as whatever happens to be left over.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Arrogance.$$, 'body', $$Assuming that what you already know is sufficient, and that you do not need to learn a subject before acting in it — a stance Kiyosaki considers especially dangerous in areas like tax law, investing, and legal structure, where confident ignorance is expensive.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Kiyosaki's real obstacles to wealth are internal and emotional, not informational — fear and arrogance do more damage than a lack of facts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Getting started$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The closing practical chapters compress the book's advice into a short list of starting moves, aimed less at giving a complete plan and more at lowering the barrier to beginning at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Find a reason greater than reality.$$, 'body', $$A strong enough "why" — the desire for freedom, security for a family, the ability to make a real choice about how to spend one's time — is what carries someone through the discomfort and setbacks that come before results, more than motivation alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Pay yourself first.$$, 'body', $$Direct money toward investment before paying discretionary bills, deliberately creating pressure that Kiyosaki argues sharpens financial creativity rather than simply courting missed payments.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Treat financial intelligence as a habit of continuous learning.$$, 'body', $$Read, ask people who have already done what you want to do, attend relevant seminars, and study deals even ones you do not take — building the pattern-recognition described earlier through repetition over years, not through a single course or book.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki is explicit that none of this guarantees an outcome; the point of the chapter is to convert everything discussed earlier from an interesting idea into a specific first action a reader can take this week.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The book's final advice is less a plan than a nudge: start small, start now, and let the habit of paying yourself first do the rest.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Rich Dad Poor Dad reads more like a fable built to change how you think about money than a rigorous financial manual, and that distinction should shape how much weight you put on any individual claim inside it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The central character is unverifiable.$$, 'body', $$Whether "rich dad" existed exactly as described, said the specific lines attributed to him, or achieved the results Kiyosaki credits him with cannot be independently confirmed. Treat the parable as a device for organizing the ideas, not as documented evidence that they work.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The house-as-liability claim oversimplifies real trade-offs.$$, 'body', $$The strict asset/liability definition ignores that a home also provides guaranteed shelter, forced savings through mortgage principal paydown, protection from rent inflation, and psychological security — none of which show up in a pure cash-flow test, and all of which matter to most households' actual financial lives.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The tax and corporate strategies described are US-specific and dated.$$, 'body', $$The history of income tax, payroll withholding, and the specific advantages of operating through a corporation all describe the US legal and tax system as it existed decades ago. None of this transfers directly to Lao tax law or business registration, and readers applying it elsewhere need local professional advice, not this book, for the mechanics.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The book is stronger on mindset than on concrete mechanics.$$, 'body', $$It rarely walks through how to actually evaluate a specific property, read a real set of financial statements, or negotiate an actual deal step by step. It is best read as a reframe of attitude and vocabulary — asset versus liability, cash flow versus net worth on paper — to carry into more technical material elsewhere.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Survivorship bias runs through the risk-taking advice.$$, 'body', $$The book is told from the point of view of someone whose bets worked out, and it says comparatively little about the base rate of business and investment failure, or about readers whose capital cushion, credit access, or safety net are too thin to absorb a loss the way Kiyosaki's stories assume is affordable.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's durable contribution is the reframe itself: sorting purchases into what feeds your pocket and what drains it, separating your job from your asset column, and treating financial education as a skill worth deliberately building. Take the mindset seriously; verify the mechanics elsewhere.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ພໍ່ສອງຄົນ, ແນວຄິດສອງແບບ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki ວາງໂຄງສ້າງປຶ້ມທັງເຫຼັ້ມເປັນບັນທຶກຊີວິດ ທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບພໍ່ສອງຄົນທີ່ລາວຮຽນຮູ້ນຳໃນເວລາດຽວກັນຕອນເປັນເດັກ. ພໍ່ແທ້ຂອງລາວ — ຮຽນສູງ, ເປັນຫົວໜ້າພະແນກການສຶກສາລັດຢູ່ Hawaii, ຄົນທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນວ່າ "ພໍ່ຈົນ" — ເປັນຕົວແທນເສັ້ນທາງແບບດັ້ງເດີມ: ຮຽນເກັ່ງ, ໄດ້ວຽກໝັ້ນຄົງ, ໄຕ່ຂັ້ນອາຊີບ. ພໍ່ຂອງໝູ່ຮັກສະໜິດຂອງລາວ — ນັກທຸລະກິດທີ່ບໍ່ຈົບຊັ້ນ ມ.1 ດ້ວຍຊ້ຳ ແຕ່ສ້າງອານາຈັກອະສັງຫາລິມະຊັບ ແລະ ທຸລະກິດຈາກສູນ, ຄົນທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນວ່າ "ພໍ່ຮັ່ງມີ" — ເປັນຕົວແທນສັນຊາດຕະຍານທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຄົນລະໂລກ ກ່ຽວກັບເງິນ, ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ແລະ ການເຮັດວຽກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄຳແນະນຳຂອງພໍ່ທັງສອງແທບເປັນຮູບເງົາຂອງກັນແລະກັນ. ພໍ່ຈົນເວົ້າວ່າ "ຮຽນໜັກໆ ເພື່ອຈະໄດ້ຫາບໍລິສັດດີໆເຮັດວຽກນຳ." ພໍ່ຮັ່ງມີເວົ້າວ່າ "ຮຽນໜັກໆ ເພື່ອຈະໄດ້ຫາບໍລິສັດດີໆຊື້." ພໍ່ຈົນເວົ້າວ່າ "ຂ້ອຍຊື້ບໍ່ໄດ້." ພໍ່ຮັ່ງມີຫ້າມປະໂຫຍກນັ້ນຢູ່ຕໍ່ໜ້າລາວ ແລ້ວຢືນຢັນໃຫ້ຖາມແທນວ່າ "ຈະຊື້ໄດ້ແນວໃດ?" — ການປ່ຽນຄຳເວົ້ານ້ອຍໆ ແຕ່ໃນຄຳບອກເລົ່າຂອງ Kiyosaki ມັນຮັກສາໃຫ້ສະໝອງສືບຫາທາງອອກ ແທນທີ່ຈະປິດຕົວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນຕໍ່ວິທີອ່ານປຶ້ມນີ້ ຄືໂຄງສ້າງນີ້ເປັນບັນທຶກຊີວິດຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຫຼັກສູດ. ບົດຮຽນຕ່າງໆມາໃນຮູບແບບຄວາມຊົງຈຳຈາກໄວເດັກ ແລະ ບົດສົນທະນາທີ່ຈື່ຄືນມາຈາກຫຼາຍສິບປີກ່ອນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນກໍລະນີສຶກສາທີ່ມີວັນທີ, ຕົວເລກ ຫຼື ການຢືນຢັນເປັນເອກະລາດວ່າ "ພໍ່ຮັ່ງມີ" ເວົ້າປະໂຫຍກໃດແທ້ໆ ຫຼື ໃນບາງເລື່ອງເລົ່າ ມີຕົວຕົນຢູ່ຈິງຕາມທີ່ອະທິບາຍ. ຄວນຖືນິທານນີ້ເປັນເຄື່ອງມືນຳສະເໜີແນວຄິດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຫຼັກຖານພິສູດແນວຄິດ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ນຳສະເໜີແນວຄິດການເງິນສອງແບບທີ່ຄ້ານກັນ ຜ່ານປະສົບການທີ່ໃຊ້ຊີວິດຈິງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນການປຽບທຽບແບບຄວບຄຸມໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຄົນຮັ່ງມີບໍ່ໄດ້ເຮັດວຽກເພື່ອເງິນ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ບົດຮຽນເປີດຂອງປຶ້ມ ຖືກນຳສະເໜີຜ່ານແບບຝຶກຫັດທີ່ຕັ້ງໃຈໃຫ້ຮູ້ສຶກອຶດອັດ: ພໍ່ຮັ່ງມີໃຫ້ Kiyosaki ອາຍຸ 9 ປີ ໄປເຮັດວຽກຢູ່ຮ້ານຂາຍເຄື່ອງແຫ້ງໂດຍໄດ້ຄ່າແຮງໜ້ອຍໆ ຈາກນັ້ນ — ເມື່ອເດັກນ້ອຍຕິດໃຈຄ່າແຮງ ແລ້ວຂໍຂຶ້ນເງິນເດືອນ — ລາວກໍ່ປະຕິເສດ ແລະ ໃນທີ່ສຸດກໍ່ໃຫ້ເຮັດວຽກຟຣີ. ຈຸດປະສົງບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຂີ້ຄ້ານ ແຕ່ແມ່ນເພື່ອໃຫ້ຈິດໃຈຄົນອາຍຸນ້ອຍ ຮູ້ສຶກທາງກາຍະພາບວ່າ ການຈັດຊີວິດອ້ອມຮອບເງິນເດືອນເປັນແນວໃດ ແລະ ການຂຶ້ນເງິນເດືອນພຽງແຕ່ຮັດສາຍໂສ້ໃຫ້ແໜ້ນຂຶ້ນ ບໍ່ໄດ້ຄາຍມັນອອກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki ຂະຫຍາຍນີ້ໃຫ້ເປັນສິ່ງທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນວ່າ "ການແຂ່ງແລ່ນຂອງໜູ" (rat race): ຄວາມຢ້ານບໍ່ມີເງິນພຽງພໍ ຜັກດັນຄົນໃຫ້ຫາວຽກເຮັດ; ຈາກນັ້ນ ເມື່ອເງິນເດືອນມາເຖິງ ຄວາມຢາກໄດ້ຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນກໍ່ເຂົ້າມາຄວບຄຸມ ຄົນຈຶ່ງເຮັດວຽກໜັກຂຶ້ນ, ໄດ້ຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນ, ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນຕາມກັນ ແລ້ວກໍ່ຈົບລົງດ້ວຍການເພິ່ງພາເງິນເດືອນນັ້ນຄືເກົ່າ — ພຽງແຕ່ຢູ່ໃນລະດັບຄວາມເປັນຢູ່ທີ່ສູງຂຶ້ນ. ອາລົມຄວາມຢ້ານ ແລະ ຄວາມຢາກ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແຜນການເງິນທີ່ຕັ້ງໃຈ ຕ່າງຫາກທີ່ເປັນຕົວຂັບເຄື່ອນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ການແຂ່ງແລ່ນຂອງໜູ (The rat race)$$, 'body', $$ຄຳສັບຂອງ Kiyosaki ສຳລັບວົງຈອນການເຮັດວຽກເພື່ອເງິນເດືອນ, ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍໄປກັບຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍທີ່ເພີ່ມຂຶ້ນຕາມລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ຄົງເພິ່ງພາທາງການເງິນຕໍ່ການເຮັດວຽກຕໍ່ໄປ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະຫາໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດກໍ່ຕາມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ທາງອອກທີ່ລາວສະເໜີ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຮັດວຽກໜັກຂຶ້ນພາຍໃນລະບົບເກົ່າ ແຕ່ໃຫ້ມອງຂ້າມເງິນເດືອນ ໄປເບິ່ງວ່າວຽກ ຫຼື ທຸລະກິດນັ້ນກຳລັງສອນຫຍັງໃຫ້ ແລະ ເລີ່ມນຳເອົາເງິນສ່ວນເກີນ ໄປຊື້ສິ່ງທີ່ສ້າງລາຍໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບແຮງງານຂອງຕົນເອງ — ຫົວຂໍ້ຂອງຫຼາຍຫົວຂໍ້ຕໍ່ໄປ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ການຂຶ້ນເງິນເດືອນບໍ່ໄດ້ຊື້ອິດສະຫຼະຈາກເງິນເດືອນ — ສ່ວນຫຼາຍມັນພຽງແຕ່ຊື້ຄວາມເພິ່ງພາແບບດຽວກັນ ໃນຂະໜາດໃຫຍ່ຂຶ້ນ.$$),
