  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Asset$$, 'body', $$Anything that puts money into your pocket whether or not you work, such as rental income, dividends, or a business that runs without your daily presence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Liability$$, 'body', $$Anything that takes money out of your pocket, whether or not you use it — including, in Kiyosaki's framing, the home you live in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The rat race$$, 'body', $$The cycle of working for a paycheck, spending more as income rises, and staying financially dependent on continued employment regardless of how much is earned.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Financial literacy (financial IQ)$$, 'body', $$The combined ability to read financial statements, understand markets, negotiate deals, and use legal and tax structures — treated as a learnable skill, not an inborn talent.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Mind your own business$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki's instruction to build a personal asset column alongside your job or profession, rather than only growing someone else's business through your labor.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Corporation (as Kiyosaki uses it)$$, 'body', $$A legal entity separate from its owner, used to limit personal liability and to route income, expenses, and taxes differently than an individual employee can.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Pay yourself first$$, 'body', $$Setting money aside for investment before paying discretionary bills, deliberately creating pressure to find the income to cover the rest.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The five obstacles$$, 'body', $$Fear, cynicism, laziness, bad habits, and arrogance — the internal barriers Kiyosaki treats as the real reason most people never build an asset column.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຊັບສິນ (Asset)$$, 'body', $$ສິ່ງໃດກໍ່ຕາມທີ່ນຳເງິນເຂົ້າກະເປົາທ່ານ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະເຮັດວຽກຫຼືບໍ່ ເຊັ່ນ ລາຍໄດ້ຄ່າເຊົ່າ, ເງິນປັນຜົນ ຫຼື ທຸລະກິດທີ່ດຳເນີນໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຕ້ອງມີທ່ານປະຈຳວັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ໜີ້ສິນ (Liability)$$, 'body', $$ສິ່ງໃດກໍ່ຕາມທີ່ດຶງເງິນອອກຈາກກະເປົາທ່ານ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະໃຊ້ຫຼືບໍ່ — ລວມທັງ ຕາມກອບຄິດຂອງ Kiyosaki, ເຮືອນທີ່ທ່ານອາໄສຢູ່.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການແຂ່ງແລ່ນຂອງໜູ (The rat race)$$, 'body', $$ວົງຈອນການເຮັດວຽກເພື່ອເງິນເດືອນ, ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນຕາມລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ຄົງເພິ່ງພາທາງການເງິນຕໍ່ການເຮັດວຽກຕໍ່ໄປ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະຫາໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມຮູ້ດ້ານການເງິນ (Financial IQ)$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມສາມາດລວມກັນ ໃນການອ່ານງົບການເງິນ, ເຂົ້າໃຈຕະຫຼາດ, ເຈລະຈາຂໍ້ຕົກລົງ ແລະ ໃຊ້ໂຄງສ້າງກົດໝາຍ ແລະ ພາສີ — ຖືເປັນທັກສະທີ່ຮຽນຮູ້ໄດ້ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພອນສະຫວັນມາແຕ່ກຳເນີດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ໃສ່ໃຈທຸລະກິດຂອງຕົນເອງ (Mind your own business)$$, 'body', $$ຄຳແນະນຳຂອງ Kiyosaki ໃຫ້ສ້າງຖັນຊັບສິນສ່ວນຕົວ ຄຽງຄູ່ກັບວຽກ ຫຼື ອາຊີບ ແທນທີ່ຈະຂະຫຍາຍແຕ່ທຸລະກິດຄົນອື່ນ ຜ່ານແຮງງານຂອງທ່ານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ບໍລິສັດ (ຕາມທີ່ Kiyosaki ໃຊ້)$$, 'body', $$ນິຕິບຸກຄົນທີ່ແຍກຕ່າງຫາກຈາກເຈົ້າຂອງ ໃຊ້ເພື່ອຈຳກັດຄວາມຮັບຜິດຊອບສ່ວນຕົວ ແລະ ນຳລາຍໄດ້, ຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ແລະ ພາສີ ຜ່ານທາງທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງຈາກພະນັກງານແຕ່ລະຄົນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈ່າຍໃຫ້ຕົນເອງກ່ອນ (Pay yourself first)$$, 'body', $$ການແຍກເງິນໄວ້ລົງທຶນ ກ່ອນຈ່າຍບິນທີ່ບໍ່ຈຳເປັນ ຕັ້ງໃຈສ້າງແຮງກົດດັນໃຫ້ຫາລາຍໄດ້ມາຈ່າຍສ່ວນທີ່ເຫຼືອ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ອຸປະສັກທັງຫ້າ (The five obstacles)$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມຢ້ານ, ຄວາມສົງໄສ, ຄວາມຂີ້ຄ້ານ, ນິໄສບໍ່ດີ ແລະ ຄວາມອວດດີ — ອຸປະສັກພາຍໃນ ທີ່ Kiyosaki ຖືວ່າ ເປັນເຫດຜົນແທ້ຈິງທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ບໍ່ເຄີຍສ້າງຖັນຊັບສິນ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Using Kiyosaki's strict definition, walk through your own recent purchases — how many were assets, how many were liabilities you may have been calling assets?$$, $$The house-as-liability argument ignores shelter value, forced savings, and security. Where do you think Kiyosaki's framework is right, and where does it oversimplify your own housing decision?$$, $$Which of the five obstacles — fear, cynicism, laziness, bad habits, arrogance — is actually the one holding you back, and what would it look like to test that honestly?$$, $$The corporate and tax strategies in the book are US-specific. What would "mind your own business" and using legal structures wisely actually look like under Lao law and your own circumstances?$$, $$Kiyosaki's stories are told by someone whose bets paid off. If you took the same risks he describes and they failed, what safety net, or lack of one, would determine how badly that failure hurt you?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ໂດຍໃຊ້ນິຍາມທີ່ເຂັ້ມງວດຂອງ Kiyosaki, ລອງທົບທວນການຊື້ຂອງທ່ານໃນຊ່ວງທີ່ຜ່ານມາ — ຈັກອັນທີ່ເປັນຊັບສິນ ແລະ ຈັກອັນທີ່ອາດເປັນໜີ້ສິນທີ່ທ່ານເອີ້ນວ່າຊັບສິນ?$$, $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງເລື່ອງເຮືອນເປັນໜີ້ສິນ ບໍ່ນັບເຖິງມູນຄ່າທີ່ພັກອາໄສ, ການອອມແບບບັງຄັບ ແລະ ຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງ. ທ່ານຄິດວ່າ ກອບຄິດຂອງ Kiyosaki ຖືກຕ້ອງບ່ອນໃດ ແລະ ຫຍໍ້ຄວາມຊັບຊ້ອນຂອງການຕັດສິນໃຈເລື່ອງທີ່ຢູ່ອາໄສຂອງທ່ານເອງບ່ອນໃດ?$$, $$ໃນອຸປະສັກທັງຫ້າ — ຄວາມຢ້ານ, ຄວາມສົງໄສ, ຄວາມຂີ້ຄ້ານ, ນິໄສບໍ່ດີ, ຄວາມອວດດີ — ອັນໃດແທ້ໆທີ່ກຳລັງເປັນຕົວຫຼຸດຮັ້ງທ່ານ ແລະ ການທົດສອບມັນຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ ຈະມີໜ້າຕາເປັນແນວໃດ?$$, $$ຍຸດທະສາດບໍລິສັດ ແລະ ພາສີໃນປຶ້ມ ເປັນສະເພາະຂອງສະຫະລັດ. ການ "ໃສ່ໃຈທຸລະກິດຂອງຕົນເອງ" ແລະ ການໃຊ້ໂຄງສ້າງທາງກົດໝາຍຢ່າງສະຫຼາດ ຈະມີໜ້າຕາເປັນແນວໃດແທ້ໆ ພາຍໃຕ້ກົດໝາຍລາວ ແລະ ສະຖານະການຂອງທ່ານເອງ?$$, $$ເລື່ອງເລົ່າຂອງ Kiyosaki ຖືກເລົ່າໂດຍຄົນທີ່ການພະນັນຂອງລາວສຳເລັດ. ຖ້າທ່ານຮັບຄວາມສ່ຽງແບບດຽວກັນທີ່ລາວອະທິບາຍ ແລ້ວມັນລົ້ມເຫຼວ ຕາໜ່າຍຄວາມປອດໄພ ຫຼື ການຂາດມັນ ຈະເປັນຕົວກຳນົດວ່າຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວນັ້ນຈະທຳຮ້າຍທ່ານໜັກປານໃດ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$An asset puts money into your pocket; a liability takes money out — regardless of what the item looks like or is called.$$, $$The rat race is a cycle where fear drives you to work, desire drives you to spend more as you earn more, and the paycheck dependency never actually shrinks.$$, $$The poor spend on expenses, the middle class spends on liabilities disguised as assets, and the rich buy assets first and fund their lifestyle from the income those assets produce.$$, $$Kiyosaki's most contested claim is that a primary home is usually a liability, not an asset, once judged strictly by cash flow.$$, $$Mind your own business: keep the job, but build a personal asset column in parallel — your job grows someone else's asset column all day.$$, $$The wealthy often use corporations to change the order of earning, spending, and paying tax, and to access legal protections an individual employee does not have.$$, $$Financial intelligence is a trainable combination of accounting, investing, market, and legal literacy — not an innate gift.$$, $$Choose early jobs for the skills they force you to build, especially sales and communication, not just for the salary.$$, $$Five internal obstacles — fear, cynicism, laziness, bad habits, and arrogance — do more to block wealth-building than a lack of information.$$, $$Paying yourself first, before other bills, deliberately creates pressure that sharpens financial creativity instead of leaving investment as leftover money.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ຊັບສິນນຳເງິນເຂົ້າກະເປົາທ່ານ ໜີ້ສິນດຶງເງິນອອກ — ບໍ່ວ່າສິ່ງນັ້ນຈະໜ້າຕາເປັນແນວໃດ ຫຼື ຖືກເອີ້ນວ່າຫຍັງ.$$, $$ການແຂ່ງແລ່ນຂອງໜູ ຄືວົງຈອນທີ່ຄວາມຢ້ານຜັກດັນໃຫ້ທ່ານເຮັດວຽກ ຄວາມຢາກຜັກດັນໃຫ້ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນຕາມລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ຄວາມເພິ່ງພາເງິນເດືອນບໍ່ເຄີຍນ້ອຍລົງແທ້ໆ.$$, $$ຄົນຈົນໃຊ້ຈ່າຍໄປກັບຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ, ຊົນຊັ້ນກາງໃຊ້ຈ່າຍໄປກັບໜີ້ສິນທີ່ປອມຕົວເປັນຊັບສິນ ແລະ ຄົນຮັ່ງມີຊື້ຊັບສິນກ່ອນ ແລ້ວໃຊ້ລາຍໄດ້ຈາກຊັບສິນນັ້ນລ້ຽງຄວາມເປັນຢູ່.$$, $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີ່ຖືກໂຕ້ຖຽງທີ່ສຸດຂອງ Kiyosaki ຄື ເຮືອນຫຼັກສ່ວນຫຼາຍແມ່ນໜີ້ສິນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຊັບສິນ ຖ້າຕັດສິນຢ່າງເຂັ້ມງວດຕາມກະແສເງິນສົດ.$$, $$ໃສ່ໃຈທຸລະກິດຂອງຕົນເອງ: ຮັກສາວຽກໄວ້ ແຕ່ສ້າງຖັນຊັບສິນສ່ວນຕົວໄປພ້ອມກັນ — ວຽກຂອງທ່ານຂະຫຍາຍຖັນຊັບສິນຄົນອື່ນຕະຫຼອດວັນ.$$, $$ຄົນຮັ່ງມີມັກໃຊ້ບໍລິສັດ ເພື່ອປ່ຽນລຳດັບການຫາລາຍໄດ້, ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ແລະ ຈ່າຍພາສີ ພ້ອມທັງເຂົ້າເຖິງການປົກປ້ອງທາງກົດໝາຍ ທີ່ພະນັກງານທົ່ວໄປບໍ່ມີ.$$, $$ຄວາມສະຫຼາດທາງການເງິນ ຄືການປະສົມປະສານທັກສະການບັນຊີ, ການລົງທຶນ, ຕະຫຼາດ ແລະ ກົດໝາຍ ທີ່ຝຶກໄດ້ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນພອນສະຫວັນມາແຕ່ກຳເນີດ.$$, $$ເລືອກວຽກເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ ຕາມທັກສະທີ່ມັນບັງຄັບໃຫ້ສ້າງ ໂດຍສະເພາະການຂາຍ ແລະ ການສື່ສານ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ຕາມເງິນເດືອນ.$$, $$ອຸປະສັກພາຍໃນຫ້າອັນ — ຄວາມຢ້ານ, ຄວາມສົງໄສ, ຄວາມຂີ້ຄ້ານ, ນິໄສບໍ່ດີ ແລະ ຄວາມອວດດີ — ຂັດຂວາງການສ້າງຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຫຼາຍກວ່າການຂາດຂໍ້ມູນ.$$, $$ການຈ່າຍໃຫ້ຕົນເອງກ່ອນ ກ່ອນບິນອື່ນ ຕັ້ງໃຈສ້າງແຮງກົດດັນ ທີ່ຊ່ວຍໃຫ້ຄິດແກ້ບັນຫາການເງິນຢ່າງສ້າງສັນ ແທນທີ່ຈະປະໃຫ້ການລົງທຶນເປັນເງິນທີ່ບັງເອີນເຫຼືອ.$$]
where book_id = 'e9f97c27-8a81-439c-9756-925d0de2eabe';

-- Rich Dad's Cashflow Quadrant — Robert Kiyosaki
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$Explains four fundamentally different ways people earn income, and why moving toward ownership changes your relationship with time.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ອະທິບາຍສີ່ວິທີພື້ນຖານທີ່ຄົນຫາລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ເປັນຫຍັງການເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງລະບົບຈຶ່ງປ່ຽນຄວາມສຳພັນກັບເວລາຂອງທ່ານ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Robert Kiyosaki's core argument is that the number on your paycheck matters far less than the quadrant it comes from. Two people can earn exactly the same income and live completely different lives, because Employees and the Self-employed trade time directly for money, while Business owners and Investors build or buy systems that produce money whether or not they are personally present. This lesson works through what actually separates the four quadrants, why moving from the left side to the right side demands new skills rather than just more hours, and where the book's confident framework should be read with a more critical eye.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຫຼັກຂອງ Robert Kiyosaki ຄື ຕົວເລກລາຍໄດ້ຂອງທ່ານບໍ່ສຳຄັນເທົ່າກັບວ່າມັນມາຈາກ "ຈະຕຸລັດ" ໃດ. ສອງຄົນສາມາດມີລາຍໄດ້ເທົ່າກັນເປັກ ແຕ່ໃຊ້ຊີວິດແຕກຕ່າງກັນຄົນລະໂລກ ເພາະພະນັກງານ ແລະ ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ ແລກເວລາໂດຍກົງເປັນເງິນ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ ແລະ ນັກລົງທຶນ ສ້າງ ຫຼື ຊື້ລະບົບທີ່ຜະລິດເງິນ ບໍ່ວ່າຕົນເອງຈະຢູ່ໃນນັ້ນຫຼືບໍ່. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍວ່າຫຍັງແທ້ໆທີ່ແຍກສີ່ຈະຕຸລັດອອກຈາກກັນ, ເປັນຫຍັງການຍ້າຍຈາກຝັ່ງຊ້າຍໄປຝັ່ງຂວາຈຶ່ງຕ້ອງການທັກສະໃໝ່ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ຊົ່ວໂມງເຮັດວຽກເພີ່ມຂຶ້ນ ແລະ ຈຸດໃດຂອງກອບແນວຄິດອັນໜັກແໜ້ນນີ້ ທີ່ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍສາຍຕາທີ່ວິພາກວິຈານກວ່າ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The four quadrants: how people actually generate income$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki opens with a deceptively simple diagram: a square split into four boxes, labeled E, S, B, and I. The letters stand for Employee, Self-employed, Business owner, and Investor, and together they describe not four job titles but four fundamentally different relationships to how money enters your life. Almost everyone's income comes primarily from one quadrant, even if they touch the others at the margins, and Kiyosaki's claim is that which quadrant you sit in shapes your freedom, your risk, your tax treatment, and your daily experience of time far more than the size of your paycheck does.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The CASHFLOW Quadrant (ESBI)$$, 'body', $$A model dividing all income sources into four categories: E (Employee — you work for someone else's system), S (Self-employed — you own a job, and you are the system), B (Business owner — you own a system that other people run), and I (Investor — your money, not your labor, produces the income).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The dividing line that matters most runs down the middle of the square, not across it. On the left side, E and S, income depends on the person continuing to show up and work; stop working and the income stops with you. On the right side, B and I, income is produced by something that exists apart from you — a business system with its own staff and processes, or capital that earns a return on its own. Kiyosaki argues this left-right divide, far more than the specific job or industry, determines whether someone is trading their life for money or building something that eventually pays them regardless.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The letters describe four different relationships to money, not four job titles — and the divide that matters most is whether income stops when you do.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$E and S: trading time directly for money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Employee quadrant is the one most people are raised for. You work inside a system someone else built, in exchange for a paycheck, benefits, and — the core appeal, in Kiyosaki's telling — a measure of security. Someone else assumes the business risk, someone else worries about payroll and market conditions, and in exchange the E accepts limits on both the upside and the control: pay tends to rise slowly and predictably, and decisions about the business itself are made by someone above them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Self-employed (S)$$, 'body', $$Someone who owns their own job rather than a business. Doctors, lawyers, consultants, freelancers, and small shop owners who do the core work themselves fall here — they answer to no boss, but they are also personally the engine of the income.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Self-employed quadrant looks like freedom from the outside — no boss, your own hours, your own name on the door — and it does deliver real independence and control over quality that many E's never get. But Kiyosaki is blunt about its hidden cost: an S is the system. There is no separation between the worker and the income; skip a week of work and the income for that week is simply gone, with no backup and often no one else capable of doing the work at the same standard. Many S's put in longer hours than the employees they used to be, in exchange for autonomy rather than more free time.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Employees trade time for a safer paycheck; the self-employed trade time for control — but both stop earning the moment they stop working.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$B and I: owning systems instead of running them$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The right side of the quadrant is defined by a single test: does the income continue if the owner is not personally present? Kiyosaki's own version of the question is pointed — could you leave for a year, come back, and find the business not just intact but healthier? If yes, you own a B. If income quietly stops the moment you stop showing up, what you actually have is a job with employees, dressed up as a company.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Business owner (B)$$, 'body', $$Someone who owns a system — processes, staff, management structure — that produces income without requiring their constant personal labor. Size is not the defining feature; independence from the owner's presence is.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Investor (I)$$, 'body', $$Someone whose money, rather than their labor, produces income — through dividends, interest, rental cash flow, or ownership stakes bought rather than built. The core skill is allocating capital well, not operating anything day to day.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki treats I as the most powerful of the four quadrants precisely because it detaches income from labor entirely. A business owner still had to build or acquire the system; an investor simply directs capital toward systems other people have already built — a company's shares, a rental property with a manager in place, a fund. The skill shifts from operating to evaluating: judging where capital is likely to be put to productive use.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$On the right side, a system or a pool of capital does the earning; the owner's job is to build or choose well, not to keep showing up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Same paycheck, four different lives$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's favorite illustration is two people earning the identical income — say, the same annual total — from different quadrants. On paper their tax return might look similar. In practice almost nothing else about their lives matches.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Time.$$, 'body', $$The E and the S both trade hours for that income; take away the hours and the income stops. The B and the I built or bought something that keeps producing whether they work this week or not — their income and their calendar are no longer the same thing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Risk.$$, 'body', $$The E's risk is concentrated in one employer's decisions; the S's risk is concentrated in their own capacity to keep performing. The B and the I carry capital and systemic risk, but it is risk they can diversify, delegate, and manage deliberately, rather than risk that simply happens to them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Tax treatment.$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki argues tax codes are frequently written to reward the activity of the right side — building businesses that employ people, and investing capital that funds them — with more deductions and more control over timing, while the E's tax is withheld before they ever see the money, and the S often carries a heavier effective burden than either side, with fewer protections than an E and fewer deductions than a B.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$None of this means E and S income is inferior in absolute terms — plenty of E's and S's out-earn plenty of B's and I's. Kiyosaki's point is narrower: the same number on a pay stub can sit inside four very different structures, and the structure, not the number, determines how much of your life you get back.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Identical income can hide four completely different relationships to time, risk, and tax — the quadrant is the real variable.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The mindset behind each quadrant$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's more provocative claim is that each quadrant is not just an income structure but a value system — and that people tend to sort themselves into a quadrant that matches values they already hold, which is part of why simply changing jobs rarely changes quadrants.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$E values security.$$, 'body', $$Stability, a predictable paycheck, benefits, and clear rules matter more than upside or control. This is a rational preference, not a character flaw — it simply optimizes for certainty over ownership.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$S values independence and standards.$$, 'body', $$Being your own boss and controlling the quality of the work matters more than scale. The recurring S instinct — "if I want it done right, I have to do it myself" — protects quality and also caps growth.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$B values systems and people.$$, 'body', $$The defining skill is finding people who are better at parts of the job than the owner is, and building structure that coordinates them without the owner personally touching every task.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$I values calculated risk and patience.$$, 'body', $$Comfort putting capital at risk for a return, willingness to wait, and enough financial literacy to judge an opportunity rather than react emotionally to it.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's warning is that a values mismatch is why quadrant moves often fail: someone with an S mindset who technically owns a company will still work inside it doing everything personally, never quite becoming a B, because the underlying belief that only they can do it right never changed even though the org chart did.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Each quadrant runs on a different core value, and changing quadrants on paper without changing the underlying value rarely sticks.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Why the self-employed get stuck$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki spends real attention on the S quadrant because it is where highly capable, hard-working people frequently get trapped by their own competence. A great cook opens a restaurant; a great mechanic opens a garage; a great designer goes freelance. The technical skill that made them valuable as an employee becomes the very thing that keeps them personally load-bearing as an owner.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The pattern he describes is consistent: the S often works more hours than they did as an E, takes on more risk (their own capital, their own liability), and yet the business is worth little to anyone else, because it cannot function without them. There is no real vacation without lost income, no real sick day without a gap in service, and no exit without either shutting down or finding someone willing to buy a business that is really just a job wearing a company's name.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's suggested way out is not to abandon the S quadrant's skill but to deliberately start building B underneath it — documenting how the work gets done, training someone else to do pieces of it, hiring before it feels comfortable, and treating "can this run without me for a week" as a metric to improve rather than a threat to avoid.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The S quadrant rewards skill so directly that it can trap the most skilled people in their own irreplaceability.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$What actually makes a business a B, not just an S with employees$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Headcount is a common but misleading proxy for B status. A ten-person consulting firm can still be a pure S if every dollar of revenue flows through the owner's personal relationships and expertise — the staff support the owner rather than replacing them. Meanwhile a small franchise outlet with a trained manager and documented procedures can be a genuine B, even with only a handful of employees.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The absence test.$$, 'body', $$Could the business continue operating — ideally improving — for an extended stretch without the owner physically present or personally deciding everything? If yes, systems and people are carrying the load, not the owner.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Documented process over personal expertise.$$, 'body', $$A B relies on procedures that can be taught and repeated by someone else; an S relies on judgment or skill that lives only in the owner's head.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Franchising as a shortcut.$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki treats buying into a proven franchise system favorably for people making an early transition — it is a way to acquire an already-built B structure rather than having to design one from nothing, at the cost of paying for that structure and following its rules.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Size is not what makes a business a B — the test is whether it can run and improve without the owner in the room.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Crossing to the right side requires new skills, not just more effort$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki is direct about why moving right is hard: it is not primarily a matter of working harder in the same way, but of learning a different, largely unfamiliar set of skills that most schooling never covers.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Leadership and delegation.$$, 'body', $$Trusting other people to do work to a standard you did not personally supervise line by line — the opposite instinct from the S mindset that built many people's early careers.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Tolerance for uncertainty and failure.$$, 'body', $$E and S income is comparatively predictable; B and I income is lumpier, and setbacks along the way are normal rather than proof the approach is wrong.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Basic financial literacy.$$, 'body', $$Reading a balance sheet and a cash flow statement well enough to judge whether a business or an investment is actually healthy, rather than relying on someone else's summary.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Sales and communication.$$, 'body', $$Both B and I require persuading other people — customers, partners, lenders, tenants — which is a muscle E and S roles often never require building.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's sharper observation is that formal education trains people well for E, and sometimes adequately for S, but offers almost no training for B or I — which is why even highly credentialed, high-earning professionals can find the right side of the quadrant genuinely foreign, not just unfamiliar in practice but unfamiliar as a way of thinking.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The right side is not the left side with more hours added — it runs on a different, largely untaught skill set.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Turning surplus cash flow into an investor's income$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Whatever quadrant produces someone's active income — E, S, or B — Kiyosaki's mechanism for reaching the I quadrant is the same for everyone: stop spending all of it. The surplus, whatever is left after living expenses, is the raw material that becomes investor income, directed into assets that themselves produce ongoing cash flow rather than into consumption.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Cash flow (passive income)$$, 'body', $$Income produced by an asset on an ongoing basis — rent from property, dividends from shares, interest, royalties, or a share of a business's profit — as distinct from a one-time gain or active pay for active labor.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki's definition of financial freedom is specific and useful precisely because it does not depend on quitting anything: the point at which cash flow from the I quadrant covers ordinary living expenses, regardless of what quadrant the active paycheck still comes from. An E who methodically builds a portfolio of cash-flow assets can reach that point without ever leaving employment; a B owner who never routes surplus profit into I assets, by contrast, can remain financially fragile despite a large business, because all the value is trapped in one system.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Financial freedom, in this framework, is the day I-quadrant cash flow covers your expenses — regardless of which quadrant still produces your paycheck.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Financial education as the real first step$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Underneath all four quadrants sits Kiyosaki's largest claim: the main obstacle to moving right is not usually money or opportunity, it is financial education — and the conditioning, often absorbed early and reinforced by school, that equates a secure job with the only responsible path.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Financial IQ$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki's term for the practical financial literacy — reading financial statements, understanding assets versus liabilities, evaluating risk, understanding tax and legal structures — that traditional schooling largely does not teach.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His critique of formal education is pointed but narrow: schools are generally effective at producing capable E's, and sometimes S's, because that is what they are built to do, but they rarely teach how to build a system, read an investment, or manage capital. Kiyosaki's practical instruction, ahead of any specific tactic — buying property, starting a business, investing in shares — is to treat changing your own thinking and closing that financial-literacy gap as the actual first move, since tactics attempted without the underlying literacy tend to recreate E or S habits inside a B or I wrapper.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$No tactic on the right side of the quadrant works until the thinking and financial literacy behind it changes first.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Cashflow Quadrant is a mindset book built around one clean diagram, and that clarity is both its appeal and its limitation. Read it with the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The four boxes are a simplifying heuristic, not a rigorous economic model.$$, 'body', $$Real careers routinely blend quadrants — an employee who also owns rental property, a business owner who still personally does client work, a freelancer with one large retainer that functions like employment. Treat ESBI as a lens for examining your own income mix, not a strict classification every income source fits into cleanly.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The quadrant personality types can tip into stereotyping.$$, 'body', $$Painting E's as security-driven, S's as controlling, and B's and I's as the more evolved thinkers flatters a preferred narrative more than it describes real, varied people. Plenty of employees are entrepreneurial in spirit, and plenty of business owners are anxious and risk-averse day to day.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Moving to B or I in practice requires starting conditions the book underweights.$$, 'body', $$Capital, a financial cushion that can absorb failed attempts, access to credit, and social networks that open doors all shape how realistic a move to the right side is. Two readers with identical effort and identical reading of this book can face very different odds depending on where they start.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The tax and legal advantages described are largely US-specific.$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki's claims about deductions, entity structures, and the tax code's treatment of business owners and investors reflect the American system he operated in. They do not map directly onto Lao tax law, business registration requirements, or property and investment regulation, and should not be relied on as tax advice anywhere outside that context.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The book is far stronger on mindset than on execution.$$, 'body', $$It is persuasive about why the quadrants matter and light on the concrete, step-by-step mechanics of actually building a B or acquiring cash-flow assets — those details are left to other books, other resources, and considerable trial and error.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's real contribution is durable: a simple lens for noticing that where your income comes from shapes your life at least as much as how much of it there is, and a genuine nudge to ask whether your current quadrant reflects a deliberate choice or simply the path you were raised toward.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ສີ່ຈະຕຸລັດ: ຄົນຫາລາຍໄດ້ແທ້ໆແນວໃດ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Kiyosaki ເລີ່ມດ້ວຍແຜນວາດງ່າຍໆທີ່ເບິ່ງຄືບໍ່ສຳຄັນ: ຈະຕຸລັດໜຶ່ງອັນຖືກແບ່ງເປັນສີ່ກ່ອງ ຊື່ວ່າ E, S, B ແລະ I. ຕົວອັກສອນເຫຼົ່ານີ້ຫຍໍ້ມາຈາກ Employee (ພະນັກງານ), Self-employed (ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ), Business owner (ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ) ແລະ Investor (ນັກລົງທຶນ) — ແລະ ໂດຍລວມແລ້ວມັນບໍ່ໄດ້ອະທິບາຍຕຳແໜ່ງງານສີ່ຢ່າງ ແຕ່ອະທິບາຍສີ່ຄວາມສຳພັນທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຢ່າງພື້ນຖານ ວ່າເງິນເຂົ້າມາໃນຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານແນວໃດ. ເກືອບທຸກຄົນມີລາຍໄດ້ຫຼັກມາຈາກຈະຕຸລັດດຽວ ເຖິງແມ່ນຈະແຕະຕ້ອງກັບອັນອື່ນຢູ່ຂອບໆ ແລະ ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ Kiyosaki ຄື ຈະຕຸລັດທີ່ທ່ານຢູ່ ກຳນົດອິດສະຫຼະ, ຄວາມສ່ຽງ, ການເສຍພາສີ ແລະ ປະສົບການປະຈຳວັນກັບເວລາຂອງທ່ານ ຫຼາຍກວ່າຂະໜາດຂອງເງິນເດືອນຫຼາຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ຈະຕຸລັດ CASHFLOW (ESBI)$$, 'body', $$ໂມເດວທີ່ແບ່ງແຫຼ່ງລາຍໄດ້ທັງໝົດອອກເປັນສີ່ໝວດ: E (ພະນັກງານ — ທ່ານເຮັດວຽກໃນລະບົບຂອງຄົນອື່ນ), S (ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ — ທ່ານເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງວຽກ ແລະ ທ່ານກໍ່ຄືລະບົບເອງ), B (ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ — ທ່ານເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງລະບົບທີ່ຄົນອື່ນຂັບເຄື່ອນ) ແລະ I (ນັກລົງທຶນ — ເງິນຂອງທ່ານ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແຮງງານ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ຜະລິດລາຍໄດ້).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເສັ້ນແບ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນທີ່ສຸດແມ່ນຢູ່ຕາມແນວຕັ້ງກາງຈະຕຸລັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແນວນອນ. ຝັ່ງຊ້າຍ E ແລະ S ລາຍໄດ້ຂຶ້ນຢູ່ກັບຄົນຄົນນັ້ນຕ້ອງສືບຕໍ່ໄປເຮັດວຽກ; ຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ ລາຍໄດ້ກໍ່ຢຸດຕາມ. ຝັ່ງຂວາ B ແລະ I ລາຍໄດ້ຖືກຜະລິດຈາກສິ່ງທີ່ຢູ່ນອກຕົວທ່ານເອງ — ລະບົບທຸລະກິດທີ່ມີພະນັກງານ ແລະ ຂະບວນການຂອງມັນເອງ ຫຼື ທຶນທີ່ໃຫ້ຜົນຕອບແທນດ້ວຍຕົນເອງ. Kiyosaki ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ເສັ້ນແບ່ງຊ້າຍ-ຂວານີ້ ສຳຄັນກວ່າອາຊີບ ຫຼື ອຸດສາຫະກຳສະເພາະໃດໆ ໃນການຕັດສິນວ່າໃຜກຳລັງແລກຊີວິດເປັນເງິນ ຫຼື ກຳລັງສ້າງບາງສິ່ງທີ່ຈະຈ່າຍໃຫ້ຕົນເອງໃນທີ່ສຸດ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະໄປໃສ.$$)
