  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The CASHFLOW Quadrant (ESBI)$$, 'body', $$A model dividing income sources into four categories — Employee, Self-employed, Business owner, Investor — describing four different relationships to how money is earned.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Employee (E)$$, 'body', $$Someone who works inside a system built by someone else, trading time and labor for a paycheck and a degree of security.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Self-employed (S)$$, 'body', $$Someone who owns their own job rather than a business — they are personally the system, so income stops when they stop working.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Business owner (B)$$, 'body', $$Someone who owns a system of people and processes that produces income without requiring their constant personal presence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Investor (I)$$, 'body', $$Someone whose capital, rather than labor, produces income, through dividends, interest, rental cash flow, or ownership stakes.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Cash flow (passive income)$$, 'body', $$Ongoing income produced by an asset — rent, dividends, interest, royalties — distinct from a one-time gain or pay for active work.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Financial IQ$$, 'body', $$Kiyosaki's term for practical financial literacy — reading financial statements, understanding assets versus liabilities, evaluating risk — that traditional schooling rarely teaches.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈະຕຸລັດ CASHFLOW (ESBI)$$, 'body', $$ໂມເດວທີ່ແບ່ງແຫຼ່ງລາຍໄດ້ອອກເປັນສີ່ໝວດ — ພະນັກງານ, ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ, ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ, ນັກລົງທຶນ — ອະທິບາຍສີ່ຄວາມສຳພັນທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນກັບການຫາລາຍໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ພະນັກງານ (E)$$, 'body', $$ຄົນທີ່ເຮັດວຽກຢູ່ໃນລະບົບທີ່ຄົນອື່ນສ້າງໄວ້ ແລກເວລາ ແລະ ແຮງງານເປັນເງິນເດືອນ ແລະ ລະດັບຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງໜຶ່ງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ (S)$$, 'body', $$ຄົນທີ່ເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງວຽກຂອງຕົນເອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ — ພວກເຂົາເອງກໍ່ຄືລະບົບ ດັ່ງນັ້ນລາຍໄດ້ຢຸດເມື່ອພວກເຂົາຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ (B)$$, 'body', $$ຄົນທີ່ເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງລະບົບຄົນ ແລະ ຂະບວນການ ທີ່ຜະລິດລາຍໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຕ້ອງອາໄສການປະກົດຕົວສ່ວນຕົວຂອງເຂົາຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ນັກລົງທຶນ (I)$$, 'body', $$ຄົນທີ່ທຶນຂອງເຂົາ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແຮງງານ ຜະລິດລາຍໄດ້ ຜ່ານເງິນປັນຜົນ, ດອກເບ້ຍ, ກະແສເງິນສົດຈາກຄ່າເຊົ່າ ຫຼື ຫຸ້ນສ່ວນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກະແສເງິນສົດ (ລາຍໄດ້ passive)$$, 'body', $$ລາຍໄດ້ຕໍ່ເນື່ອງທີ່ຊັບສິນຜະລິດ — ຄ່າເຊົ່າ, ເງິນປັນຜົນ, ດອກເບ້ຍ, ຄ່າລິຂະສິດ — ແຕກຕ່າງຈາກກຳໄລເທື່ອດຽວ ຫຼື ຄ່າຈ້າງແຮງງານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ໄອຄິວທາງການເງິນ (Financial IQ)$$, 'body', $$ຄຳສັບຂອງ Kiyosaki ສຳລັບຄວາມຮູ້ດ້ານການເງິນໃນທາງປະຕິບັດ — ອ່ານໃບລາຍງານການເງິນ, ເຂົ້າໃຈຊັບສິນທຽບກັບໜີ້ສິນ, ປະເມີນຄວາມສ່ຽງ — ທີ່ໂຮງຮຽນທົ່ວໄປບໍ່ຄ່ອຍສອນ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Which quadrant does most of your current income actually come from — and is that the result of a deliberate choice, or simply the path your upbringing and education pointed you toward?$$, $$Kiyosaki argues the internal shift in values has to happen before a quadrant move sticks. Where in your own life do you have the tactics (savings, ambition, a side project) without yet having made that underlying mental shift?$$, $$The tax and legal advantages Kiyosaki describes for B and I are specific to the US system he worked in. How would Lao tax law, business registration, and property rules change the actual calculus of which quadrant is worth pursuing?$$, $$The book assumes a degree of capital and risk tolerance many readers do not start with. What would a realistic, low-capital first move toward B or I look like for someone starting with very little surplus?$$, $$Is it more realistic to deliberately straddle quadrants — staying an E for stability while slowly building I on the side — or does the book's four-box framing push toward a more binary choice than most people's real lives allow?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ລາຍໄດ້ສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຂອງທ່ານໃນປັດຈຸບັນ ມາຈາກຈະຕຸລັດໃດແທ້ໆ — ແລະ ນັ້ນແມ່ນຜົນຂອງທາງເລືອກທີ່ຕັ້ງໃຈ ຫຼືພຽງແຕ່ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ການລ້ຽງດູ ແລະ ການສຶກສາຊີ້ນຳທ່ານໄປ?$$, $$Kiyosaki ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ການປ່ຽນແປງຄຸນຄ່າພາຍໃນຕ້ອງເກີດຂຶ້ນກ່ອນ ການຍ້າຍຈະຕຸລັດຈຶ່ງຈະຕິດ. ຢູ່ໃສໃນຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານ ທີ່ທ່ານມີຍຸດທະສາດ (ເງິນຝາກ, ຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານ, ໂຄງການສ່ວນຕົວ) ແຕ່ຍັງບໍ່ທັນປ່ຽນແປງທາງຄວາມຄິດພື້ນຖານນັ້ນ?$$, $$ຂໍ້ໄດ້ປຽບດ້ານພາສີ ແລະ ກົດໝາຍທີ່ Kiyosaki ອະທິບາຍສຳລັບ B ແລະ I ແມ່ນສະເພາະລະບົບອາເມລິກາທີ່ລາວດຳເນີນທຸລະກິດຢູ່. ກົດໝາຍພາສີລາວ, ການຈົດທະບຽນທຸລະກິດ ແລະ ລະບຽບອະສັງຫາລິມະຊັບ ຈະປ່ຽນການຄິດໄລ່ວ່າຈະຕຸລັດໃດຄຸ້ມຄ່າໃນການໄລ່ຕາມແນວໃດ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ສົມມຸດລະດັບທຶນ ແລະ ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ທີ່ຜູ້ອ່ານຫຼາຍຄົນບໍ່ໄດ້ມີແຕ່ຕົ້ນ. ບາດກ້າວທຳອິດທີ່ເປັນຈິງ ດ້ວຍທຶນໜ້ອຍ ໄປສູ່ B ຫຼື I ຈະເປັນແນວໃດ ສຳລັບຄົນທີ່ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນດ້ວຍເງິນເກີນໜ້ອຍຫຼາຍ?$$, $$ການຢືນຄ້ອມຫຼາຍຈະຕຸລັດພ້ອມກັນ ຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ — ຄົງເປັນ E ເພື່ອຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ຄ່ອຍໆສ້າງ I ຄຽງຂ້າງ — ເປັນຈິງກວ່າບໍ ຫຼືກອບແນວຄິດສີ່ກ່ອງຂອງປຶ້ມ ດັນໃຫ້ເປັນທາງເລືອກສອງດ້ານຫຼາຍກວ່າຄວາມເປັນຈິງໃນຊີວິດຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Income comes from four fundamentally different quadrants — Employee, Self-employed, Business owner, Investor — not just four job types.$$, $$On the left side (E, S) income depends on the person continuing to work; on the right side (B, I) a system or capital produces income without constant presence.$$, $$The Self-employed quadrant offers independence and control, but the person is the system — stop working and the income stops immediately.$$, $$A business only qualifies as a true B if it can run and even improve for an extended period without the owner physically present.$$, $$Identical income can sit inside very different structures — time demands, risk exposure, and tax treatment all differ sharply by quadrant.$$, $$Each quadrant tends to reflect a different core value: security (E), independence (S), systems and people (B), calculated risk (I).$$, $$Moving from left to right requires new skills — delegation, tolerance for uncertainty, financial literacy, sales — not simply more effort.$$, $$Directing surplus cash flow into assets that produce ongoing income is how any quadrant's earner becomes an investor over time.$$, $$Financial freedom, in this framework, is the point where investor-quadrant cash flow covers living expenses, regardless of the active quadrant.$$, $$Kiyosaki treats financial education — changing how you think about money — as the real first step, before any specific investing or business tactic.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ລາຍໄດ້ມາຈາກສີ່ຈະຕຸລັດທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຢ່າງພື້ນຖານ — ພະນັກງານ, ຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະ, ເຈົ້າຂອງທຸລະກິດ, ນັກລົງທຶນ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ສີ່ປະເພດອາຊີບ.$$, $$ຝັ່ງຊ້າຍ (E, S) ລາຍໄດ້ຂຶ້ນຢູ່ກັບຄົນສືບຕໍ່ເຮັດວຽກ; ຝັ່ງຂວາ (B, I) ລະບົບ ຫຼື ທຶນຜະລິດລາຍໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຕ້ອງມີການປະກົດຕົວຢູ່ຕະຫຼອດ.$$, $$ຈະຕຸລັດຜູ້ປະກອບອາຊີບອິດສະຫຼະໃຫ້ອິດສະຫຼະ ແລະ ການຄວບຄຸມ ແຕ່ຄົນນັ້ນກໍ່ຄືລະບົບເອງ — ຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ ລາຍໄດ້ກໍ່ຢຸດທັນທີ.$$, $$ທຸລະກິດຈະນັບເປັນ B ແທ້ໆ ກໍ່ຕໍ່ເມື່ອມັນສາມາດດຳເນີນງານ ແລະ ດີຂຶ້ນໄດ້ ໃນໄລຍະຍາວ ໂດຍບໍ່ມີເຈົ້າຂອງຢູ່ນຳ.$$, $$ລາຍໄດ້ເທົ່າກັນ ອາດຢູ່ໃນໂຄງສ້າງທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຫຼາຍ — ຄວາມຕ້ອງການເວລາ, ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ແລະ ການເສຍພາສີ ລ້ວນແຕ່ຕ່າງກັນຢ່າງຊັດເຈນຕາມຈະຕຸລັດ.$$, $$ແຕ່ລະຈະຕຸລັດມັກສະທ້ອນຄຸນຄ່າຫຼັກທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນ: ຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງ (E), ອິດສະຫຼະ (S), ລະບົບ ແລະ ຄົນ (B), ຄວາມສ່ຽງທີ່ຄິດໄລ່ໄດ້ (I).$$, $$ການຍ້າຍຈາກຝັ່ງຊ້າຍໄປຝັ່ງຂວາ ຕ້ອງການທັກສະໃໝ່ — ການມອບໝາຍ, ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມບໍ່ແນ່ນອນ, ຄວາມຮູ້ດ້ານການເງິນ, ການຂາຍ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມເພີ່ມ.$$, $$ການນຳກະແສເງິນສົດສ່ວນເກີນໄປໃສ່ຊັບສິນທີ່ຜະລິດລາຍໄດ້ຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ ຄືວິທີທີ່ຄົນຈາກຈະຕຸລັດໃດກໍ່ຕາມ ຄ່ອຍໆກາຍເປັນນັກລົງທຶນ.$$, $$ຄວາມອິດສະຫຼະທາງການເງິນ ໃນກອບແນວຄິດນີ້ ຄືຈຸດທີ່ກະແສເງິນສົດຈາກຈະຕຸລັດນັກລົງທຶນ ພຽງພໍປົກຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະຕຸລັດປະຈຸບັນຈະເປັນຫຍັງ.$$, $$Kiyosaki ຖືວ່າການສຶກສາດ້ານການເງິນ — ການປ່ຽນວິທີຄິດກ່ຽວກັບເງິນ — ຄືບາດກ້າວທຳອິດແທ້ໆ ກ່ອນຍຸດທະສາດການລົງທຶນ ຫຼື ທຸລະກິດສະເພາະໃດໆ.$$]
where book_id = '51715700-a81d-48d9-8710-b10adf7ce8cd';

-- Think and Grow Rich — Napoleon Hill
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A foundational success classic built around definite purpose, persistence, and organized planning.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ປຶ້ມຄລາສສິກແຫ່ງຄວາມສຳເລັດ ທີ່ສ້າງຂຶ້ນຈາກເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຊັດເຈນ, ຄວາມພາກພຽນ ແລະ ແຜນທີ່ເປັນລະບົບ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Napoleon Hill's 1937 book is one of the most widely read success manuals ever published, built around a single claim repeated in a dozen forms: whatever the mind can conceive and believe, it can achieve, provided the desire is definite enough and the effort is organized enough. This lesson works through Hill's actual framework — definite purpose, autosuggestion, specialized knowledge, imagination, planning, persistence, the Master Mind principle, and the six fears that undermine all of it — in the measured, analytical language the claims deserve, since several of them rest on Hill's own testimony rather than verifiable evidence. It closes with a clear-eyed look at what the book gets right, what it cannot prove, and what era it was written in.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ປຶ້ມປີ 1937 ຂອງ Napoleon Hill ເປັນໜຶ່ງໃນຄູ່ມືຄວາມສຳເລັດທີ່ຖືກອ່ານກວ້າງຂວາງທີ່ສຸດເທົ່າທີ່ເຄີຍພິມ ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບຄຳກ່າວອ້າງດຽວທີ່ຖືກເວົ້າຊ້ຳໃນຫຼາຍຮູບແບບ: ສິ່ງໃດກໍ່ຕາມທີ່ຈິດໃຈສາມາດຄິດ ແລະ ເຊື່ອໄດ້ ຈິດໃຈກໍ່ສາມາດບັນລຸໄດ້ ຖ້າຄວາມປາຖະໜາຊັດເຈນພຽງພໍ ແລະ ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມເປັນລະບົບພຽງພໍ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍກອບແນວຄິດແທ້ຈິງຂອງ Hill — ເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຊັດເຈນ, ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ, ຄວາມຮູ້ສະເພາະທາງ, ຈິນຕະນາການ, ການວາງແຜນ, ຄວາມພາກພຽນ, ຫຼັກການ Master Mind ແລະ ຄວາມຢ້ານກົວຫົກຢ່າງທີ່ທຳລາຍທັງໝົດນັ້ນ — ດ້ວຍພາສາທີ່ວັດແທກໄດ້ ແລະ ວິເຄາະ ຊຶ່ງເໝາະສົມກັບຄຳກ່າວອ້າງເຫຼົ່ານັ້ນ ເພາະບາງອັນອີງໃສ່ຄຳໃຫ້ການຂອງ Hill ເອງ ຫຼາຍກວ່າຫຼັກຖານທີ່ພິສູດໄດ້. ບົດສະຫຼຸບຈະເບິ່ງຢ່າງຊັດເຈນວ່າປຶ້ມນີ້ຖືກຢູ່ບ່ອນໃດ, ພິສູດບໍ່ໄດ້ບ່ອນໃດ ແລະ ຖືກຂຽນຂຶ້ນໃນຍຸກໃດ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Thoughts become things: the premise of the book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill states his central claim early and repeats it throughout the book in slightly different phrasing: whatever the mind can conceive and believe, it can achieve. According to Hill's own account, the book grew out of roughly twenty years of research that began when steel magnate Andrew Carnegie allegedly commissioned him, as a young journalist, to study several hundred of the era's most successful people — among them Henry Ford, Thomas Edison, and John D. Rockefeller — and distill whatever they had in common into a philosophy any ordinary person could apply.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$That origin story matters for how the book should be read, because it is essentially unverifiable. There is no independent record of Carnegie commissioning the project, and historians who have looked into Hill's biography have found a pattern of exaggeration and invention in his other claims about his own life. None of that necessarily makes the underlying advice wrong, but it means the book's authority rests on Hill's narrative voice and his subjects' famous names, not on documented methodology.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Structurally, Hill organizes the material into what he calls thirteen principles or steps — desire, faith, autosuggestion, specialized knowledge, imagination, organized planning, decision, persistence, the Master Mind, and several chapters on the subconscious mind, the brain, a claimed "sixth sense," and the fears that block all of it. The chapters are cumulative: each principle is presented as a necessary but not sufficient link, so that skipping one, in Hill's telling, breaks the chain that turns a thought into a result.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's entire system rests on one wager: achievement follows a definite, teachable method rather than luck or circumstance — a claim he illustrates with, but cannot fully document from, the lives of famous industrialists.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Desire: the starting point of all achievement$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill opens the practical portion of the book by distinguishing an ordinary wish from what he calls a burning desire — a distinction the entire framework depends on. Most people, in his account, want more money or success in a vague, passive way that never survives the first setback. Achievement, he argues, requires a desire specific and intense enough that it becomes close to an obsession, backed by a plan rather than left as a hope.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Burning desire$$, 'body', $$In Hill's usage, a desire so definite, specific, and emotionally intense that it dominates a person's thinking and drives sustained action, as opposed to a passive wish that evaporates under the first obstacle.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill translates this into a concrete, six-step exercise, presented as the practical starting point for anyone using the book:$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fix in your mind the exact amount or object you desire.$$, 'body', $$A vague ambition such as "I want to be rich" is treated as functionally useless; the goal has to be specific enough to be written down as a fact.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Determine what you intend to give in return.$$, 'body', $$Hill is explicit that nothing is achieved without an equivalent — the plan must include what effort, service, or value you will exchange for the goal.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Set a definite date by which you intend to possess it.$$, 'body', $$A deadline, in his framework, is what turns a wish into a plan by forcing the goal out of the indefinite future.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Create a definite plan and begin carrying it out immediately, ready or not.$$, 'body', $$Hill's insistence is on starting before the plan feels complete, since waiting for full readiness is, in his account, a common way ambition quietly dies.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Write a clear, concise statement of the amount, the deadline, the plan, and what you will give in return.$$, 'body', $$Putting the goal in writing is treated as a distinct step from merely deciding on it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Read the statement aloud, twice daily, seeing and feeling yourself already in possession of the goal.$$, 'body', $$This step connects desire directly to the autosuggestion technique covered next — repetition is meant to impress the goal on the subconscious mind.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$A vague wish for success rarely survives the first setback; Hill's method converts desire into a written goal, a deadline, and a plan you begin immediately.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Faith and autosuggestion: impressing the subconscious$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill treats faith not as a religious sentiment but as a state of mind that can be deliberately manufactured — and the tool he offers for manufacturing it is autosuggestion, repeated self-communication aimed at the subconscious mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Autosuggestion$$, 'body', $$The process of repeatedly and deliberately impressing a chosen thought, image, or statement on one's own subconscious mind, typically through spoken or written repetition, on the theory that the subconscious eventually acts on whatever it is fed regardless of whether the input is true.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The mechanism Hill describes is that the subconscious does not distinguish between a vividly imagined thought and an actual experience, so repeating a written statement of desire aloud, with feeling, is presented as a way to gradually convert belief in a goal into the emotional certainty Hill calls faith. This is presented with the same confident, declarative tone as the book's more practical chapters on planning, which is worth noting: autosuggestion, the subconscious mind, and the related idea of a "sixth sense" later in the book are claims about how the mind works, not established findings, and the book offers no controlled evidence for them — only testimonial and analogy.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read more cautiously, the practice Hill describes overlaps with things later research has studied under different names — self-affirmation, visualization, goal priming — with a genuinely mixed evidence base; none of that research supports the stronger claim that repetition alone can summon money or opportunity into a person's life, which is closer to what Hill sometimes implies.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's faith is not a feeling that arrives on its own but a state he claims can be manufactured through repetition — a technique worth separating from the era's confident certainty about how it works.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Specialized knowledge vs. general knowledge$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill spends a chapter correcting what he treats as a popular misunderstanding: knowledge, on its own, is not power. General knowledge — the broad, unorganized information a person might pick up over a lifetime — has, in his framing, little practical value no matter how much of it a person accumulates.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Specialized knowledge$$, 'body', $$Knowledge that has been organized and directed toward a definite purpose through a definite plan. Hill's claim is that only this kind of knowledge, applied, constitutes usable power — general or scattered knowledge does not.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The practical corollary is that a person pursuing a definite goal does not need to personally master every relevant field. Hill points to industrialists who left school early and had comparatively little formal education, arguing that what mattered was their ability to acquire the specific knowledge a project required, often by employing or partnering with people who already had it — a thread that anticipates the Master Mind chapter later in the book.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is one of the more durable pieces of the framework: the idea that knowledge only becomes valuable when it is applied toward a specific end, rather than accumulated for its own sake, holds up reasonably well independent of Hill's more mystical claims elsewhere in the book.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Knowledge by itself is only potential power; Hill argues it becomes actual power only when organized, specialized, and applied to a definite plan.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Imagination: synthetic and creative$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill divides imagination into two working modes, arguing that both are trainable faculties rather than fixed talents a person either has or lacks.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Synthetic imagination$$, 'body', $$Combining existing ideas, concepts, or plans into new arrangements. Hill treats this as the more common and more trainable of the two — most inventions and plans, in his account, are recombinations of known elements rather than something from nothing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Creative imagination$$, 'body', $$In Hill's account, a faculty that operates when the conscious mind is stimulated by intense desire and puts a person in direct contact with sudden ideas, hunches, or flashes of insight — described elsewhere in the book as related to a claimed "sixth sense." This is presented as literal rather than metaphorical, and it is the least evidence-backed claim in the book.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The chapter's practical advice — using imagination deliberately to combine old ideas into new plans, rather than waiting passively for inspiration — is reasonable and consistent with how creativity researchers describe idea generation today. The claim that a sufficiently intense desire opens a channel to insight beyond ordinary reasoning is a separate, much stronger assertion that the book does not attempt to substantiate.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Most practical ideas are old elements recombined, not new elements invented — the disciplined half of Hill's imagination chapter; the claimed direct channel to sudden insight is the speculative half.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Organized planning and the qualities of leadership$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Desire and imagination, in Hill's structure, still need to be converted into a workable plan of action, and he is emphatic that the plan itself is disposable in a way the goal is not: if a plan fails, the failure is in the plan, not in the person, and the correct response is to build a new plan and continue, not to abandon the goal.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Where the plan requires skills or resources a person does not personally have, Hill directs readers toward alliances with others rather than solitary effort — a thread he develops fully in the Master Mind chapter later on. He also lists qualities he associates with effective leadership, framed as observations from the people he claims to have studied:$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Unwavering courage$$, 'body', $$Grounded in self-knowledge and confidence in one's own plan, not bravado.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Self-control$$, 'body', $$A leader who cannot control themselves cannot be trusted to control others.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$A keen sense of justice$$, 'body', $$Without a sense of fairness, no leader can command lasting respect.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Definiteness of decision$$, 'body', $$Hesitant leaders, in Hill's account, signal a lack of self-confidence that others quickly sense.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Definiteness of plans$$, 'body', $$A leader who works by guesswork rather than by plan is compared to a ship without a rudder.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The habit of doing more than paid for$$, 'body', $$Presented as an obligation leaders owe both themselves and those who follow them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$A pleasing personality$$, 'body', $$Hill argues sloppy or careless leadership rarely earns lasting cooperation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Sympathy and understanding$$, 'body', $$An effective leader must be able to understand followers and their problems.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Mastery of detail$$, 'body', $$Leadership demands mastery of the details of one's position.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Willingness to assume full responsibility$$, 'body', $$Including responsibility for the mistakes of subordinates.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$A failed plan is a failure of the plan, not of the goal — Hill's instruction is to keep revising the plan rather than lowering the desire.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Decision: the power of prompt, firm choices$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill claims, based on his research into the people he studied, that successful individuals share a habit of reaching decisions quickly and changing them slowly, if ever, once made — while people who fail to accumulate wealth tend to reach decisions slowly and change them frequently and easily. This specific comparison is presented as a finding from his interviews, but like the Carnegie commissioning story, it is not independently verifiable and should be read as Hill's characterization rather than documented research.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The underlying argument is more defensible than the specific numbers attached to it: indecision, in Hill's account, is closely tied to susceptibility to the opinions of others. People who have not done the work of forming a definite purpose are described as easily talked out of their plans by friends, relatives, and casual acquaintances — Hill's advice is to keep major goals private from anyone except the small group of people directly cooperating on them, precisely to avoid this erosion.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He links this directly to the fear of criticism, discussed later in the book as one of the six basic fears — indecisiveness, in his framing, is less a neutral trait than a symptom of an underlying fear of what other people will think.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's claim is less about speed for its own sake and more about immunity to other people's opinions once a decision, backed by a definite plan, has actually been made.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Persistence and the "three feet from gold" story$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill treats persistence as the quality that separates people who eventually convert desire into achievement from those who quit somewhere along the way, and he illustrates it with one of the book's best-known anecdotes.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$As Hill tells it, a man named R.U. Darby had an uncle who staked a gold claim, dug and found a promising vein, then hit a rock formation that seemed to cut the vein off entirely. After drilling further with no success, the uncle gave up, sold his equipment to a junk dealer for a small sum, and went home. The junk dealer, before scrapping anything, had an engineer examine the mine — the engineer calculated that the vein had not ended but merely shifted, and that it resumed just three feet from where drilling had stopped. The junk dealer resumed digging and made a fortune. Hill uses the story, and Darby's own account of learning from it, as the source of his recurring line that many people give up just before the breakthrough that would have rewarded their persistence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$As with the book's other anecdotes, this story cannot be independently verified and should be read as an illustration of Hill's point rather than proof of it. The point itself — that persistence is difficult to distinguish from stubbornness in the moment, and that setbacks do not reliably signal how close a goal actually is — is a genuine insight, but the story also invites survivorship bias: it is retold because the persistence paid off, not because persistence reliably pays off, and Hill does not address the far larger number of cases where continuing past a setback simply wastes further resources.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Definiteness of purpose$$, 'body', $$Knowing what you want is described as the first and most important step toward developing persistence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Desire$$, 'body', $$Intense desire makes persistence comparatively easy to sustain.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Self-reliance$$, 'body', $$Belief in one's ability to carry out a plan encourages following through on it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Definiteness of plans$$, 'body', $$Organized plans, even when they need revision, are described as easier to persist with than vague intentions.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Accurate knowledge$$, 'body', $$Knowing your plan is sound, based on experience or observation, supports persistence; guessing undermines it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Cooperation$$, 'body', $$Sympathy, understanding, and harmonious cooperation with others encourages persistence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Willpower$$, 'body', $$Habitually concentrating thought on building plans for a definite purpose, in Hill's account, leads to persistence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Habit$$, 'body', $$Persistence is presented as the direct result of habit — the mind absorbs and becomes part of the experiences on which it feeds, and courage born of repeated action gradually replaces fear.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's central warning is that most failure happens one step before success would have arrived — a genuinely useful caution about quitting, though the story that illustrates it proves nothing on its own.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The Master Mind principle$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill presents the Master Mind as one of the most important principles in the book — and, notably, as more than a metaphor for teamwork or networking.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The Master Mind$$, 'body', $$Coordination of knowledge and effort between two or more people, working toward a definite purpose in a spirit of genuine harmony. Hill's specific claim is that this alliance produces a third, intangible force — greater than the simple sum of the individuals' separate knowledge and effort — and that this added force is unavailable to anyone working alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill cites Andrew Carnegie's inner circle of roughly twenty associates as the alliance behind the creation of the steel empire, and points to Henry Ford's collaborations with Thomas Edison and Harvey Firestone as another example. The word he returns to repeatedly is harmony: a group assembled for mutual benefit but lacking genuine goodwill among its members, in his account, does not produce the Master Mind effect — it remains an ordinary working group, with the added force present only when the cooperation is real rather than transactional.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Stripped of the more mystical language Hill sometimes applies to it — describing the effect as something closer to a literal merging of minds — the practical version of this idea holds up well and anticipates later, better-evidenced observations about complementary skills, honest collaboration, and trusted advisory groups mattering more than raw individual talent.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's claim is not merely that teams get more done than individuals, but that genuine harmony between allied minds produces a distinct force unavailable to anyone working alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The six ghosts of fear$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill closes the practical framework by naming the obstacles he claims undermine every other principle in the book: six basic fears, which he argues are learned states of mind rather than anything inborn, and which can therefore be examined and removed through honest self-analysis.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of poverty$$, 'body', $$Described by Hill as the most destructive of the six, because it paralyzes reasoning and imagination and is closely tied to indecision.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of criticism$$, 'body', $$The fear of what others will think or say, which Hill links directly to the erosion of decisiveness covered earlier.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of ill health$$, 'body', $$A preoccupation with sickness that Hill claims can itself contribute to poor health and to timid, hesitant decision-making.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of loss of love$$, 'body', $$Rooted, in Hill's account, in jealousy and insecurity, and capable of distorting judgment in both personal and professional decisions.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of old age$$, 'body', $$Tied partly to fear of poverty and partly to loss of freedom, in Hill's telling.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fear of death$$, 'body', $$The most universal of the six, and one Hill argues is worsened by superstition and lack of a settled philosophy of life.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill also warns against a seventh, related state he treats as corrosive on its own: worry, indecision, and doubt feeding each other in a loop, gradually weakening a person's persistence and self-reliance even without a specific fear attached to them. As with autosuggestion and the sixth sense, this chapter is offered with the same confidence as the planning chapters, but it is closer to folk psychology dressed in analytical language than to a tested clinical model — useful as a prompt for self-examination, not as a diagnostic framework.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Hill's claim is that fear, not lack of ability, is the more common thing standing between a person and their definite purpose — and that, being learned, it can also be unlearned.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Think and Grow Rich has remained in print continuously since 1937 and has influenced a large share of the success and self-help literature that followed it. It is also a book that mixes genuinely useful, durable planning advice with claims that were never testable and have not been supported since. Read it with the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The core methodology claim is unverifiable.$$, 'body', $$Hill's account of a Carnegie-commissioned, twenty-year study involving interviews with hundreds of named industrialists has no independent documentation, and historians who have examined Hill's biography have found a pattern of embellishment elsewhere in his life story. Treat the framework as Hill's synthesis and the named tycoons as illustrations, not as a documented research study.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The mystical chapters are presented with the same confidence as the practical ones, but rest on essentially no evidence.$$, 'body', $$Autosuggestion, the subconscious mind, the claimed "sixth sense," and the idea of directly channeling a form of "infinite intelligence" are asserted in the same declarative tone as the advice on writing a definite plan. The planning advice is reasonable regardless of whether the mystical mechanism behind it is real; the book does not ask you to notice the difference.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Survivorship bias runs through the entire book.$$, 'body', $$Every principle is illustrated with people who became extremely wealthy. The book has essentially nothing to say about the far larger number of people who pursued a burning desire, wrote a definite plan, persisted past setbacks, and still did not succeed — often for reasons of circumstance, timing, discrimination, or luck that the framework does not account for.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It reflects early-20th-century American assumptions that do not universally translate.$$, 'body', $$Nearly every example is a man, most are white American industrialists operating in a specific economic and legal environment, and the book has little to say about readers facing structural barriers — limited capital, restricted markets, discrimination, unstable institutions — that no amount of definiteness of purpose removes on its own.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Some of the advice has aged better than the theory behind it.$$, 'body', $$Writing down a specific goal with a deadline, building a workable plan and revising it rather than abandoning the goal, seeking out complementary collaborators, and treating setbacks as information rather than verdicts are all reasonable practices independent of Hill's claims about the subconscious mind or a sixth sense. The practical skeleton of the book is more defensible than its stated mechanism.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read this way — as an influential, historically significant statement of a planning-and-persistence philosophy, not as documented psychology — the book's lasting contribution is real: definite purpose, a written plan with a deadline, organized knowledge, allied effort, and persistence past the point most people quit are a genuinely useful checklist. Its explanation for why they work, and its silence about who they fail, deserve the same scrutiny as any other unverified success story.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຄວາມຄິດກາຍເປັນສິ່ງທີ່ຈັບຕ້ອງໄດ້: ພື້ນຖານຄວາມຄິດຂອງປຶ້ມ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Hill ບອກຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຫຼັກຂອງລາວຕັ້ງແຕ່ຕົ້ນ ແລະ ເວົ້າຊ້ຳຕະຫຼອດປຶ້ມໃນຮູບແບບທີ່ຕ່າງກັນເລັກນ້ອຍ: ສິ່ງໃດກໍ່ຕາມທີ່ຈິດໃຈສາມາດຄິດ ແລະ ເຊື່ອໄດ້ ຈິດໃຈກໍ່ສາມາດບັນລຸໄດ້. ຕາມຄຳບອກເລົ່າຂອງ Hill ເອງ ປຶ້ມນີ້ເກີດຈາກການຄົ້ນຄວ້າປະມານ 20 ປີ ທີ່ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນເມື່ອນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳເຫຼັກກ້າ Andrew Carnegie ວ່າກັນວ່າໄດ້ວ່າຈ້າງລາວ ຊຶ່ງເປັນນັກຂ່າວໜຸ່ມໃນຂະນະນັ້ນ ໃຫ້ສຶກສາຄົນທີ່ປະສົບຄວາມສຳເລັດທີ່ສຸດຫຼາຍຮ້ອຍຄົນໃນຍຸກນັ້ນ — ລວມທັງ Henry Ford, Thomas Edison ແລະ John D. Rockefeller — ແລ້ວກັ່ນຕອງສິ່ງທີ່ພວກເຂົາມີຮ່ວມກັນ ໃຫ້ກາຍເປັນປັດຊະຍາທີ່ຄົນທົ່ວໄປສາມາດນຳໄປໃຊ້ໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເລື່ອງທີ່ມາຂອງປຶ້ມນີ້ ສຳຄັນຕໍ່ວິທີທີ່ຄວນອ່ານມັນ ເພາະໂດຍພື້ນຖານແລ້ວມັນພິສູດບໍ່ໄດ້. ບໍ່ມີບັນທຶກເອກະລາດໃດຢືນຢັນວ່າ Carnegie ວ່າຈ້າງໂຄງການນີ້ ແລະ ນັກປະຫວັດສາດທີ່ໄດ້ສຶກສາຊີວະປະຫວັດຂອງ Hill ພົບຮູບແບບການເວົ້າເກີນຄວາມຈິງ ແລະ ການປະດິດຂຶ້ນເອງ ໃນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງອື່ນໆກ່ຽວກັບຊີວິດຂອງລາວ. ນັ້ນບໍ່ໄດ້ໝາຍຄວາມວ່າຄຳແນະນຳພື້ນຖານຜິດແທ້ໆ ແຕ່ໝາຍຄວາມວ່າຄວາມໜ້າເຊື່ອຖືຂອງປຶ້ມ ອີງໃສ່ນ້ຳສຽງການເລົ່າເລື່ອງຂອງ Hill ແລະ ຊື່ສຽງຂອງບຸກຄົນທີ່ຖືກອ້າງອີງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນວິທີການຄົ້ນຄວ້າທີ່ມີເອກະສານຢັ້ງຢືນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ໃນດ້ານໂຄງສ້າງ Hill ຈັດລະບຽບເນື້ອຫາອອກເປັນສິ່ງທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນວ່າ 13 ຫຼັກການ ຫຼື ຂັ້ນຕອນ — ຄວາມປາຖະໜາ, ຄວາມເຊື່ອ, ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ, ຄວາມຮູ້ສະເພາະທາງ, ຈິນຕະນາການ, ການວາງແຜນເປັນລະບົບ, ການຕັດສິນໃຈ, ຄວາມພາກພຽນ, Master Mind ແລະ ບົດອື່ນໆອີກຫຼາຍບົດກ່ຽວກັບຈິດໃຕ້ສຳນຶກ, ສະໝອງ, "ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກທີ 6" ທີ່ອ້າງເຖິງ ແລະ ຄວາມຢ້ານກົວທີ່ຂັດຂວາງທັງໝົດນັ້ນ. ບົດຕ່າງໆເຊື່ອມໂຍງກັນ: ແຕ່ລະຫຼັກການຖືກນຳສະເໜີວ່າເປັນຂໍ້ຕໍ່ທີ່ຈຳເປັນແຕ່ບໍ່ພຽງພໍໃນຕົວມັນເອງ ດັ່ງນັ້ນການຂ້າມຂໍ້ໃດຂໍ້ໜຶ່ງ ຕາມການອະທິບາຍຂອງ Hill ຈະຕັດຕ່ອງໂສ້ທີ່ປ່ຽນຄວາມຄິດໃຫ້ກາຍເປັນຜົນລັບ.$$)
