    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງກ່ຽວກັບວິທີການຄົ້ນຄວ້າຫຼັກ ພິສູດບໍ່ໄດ້.$$, 'body', $$ຄຳບອກເລົ່າຂອງ Hill ກ່ຽວກັບການສຶກສາ 20 ປີ ທີ່ Carnegie ວ່າຈ້າງ ຮ່ວມກັບການສຳພາດນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳຫຼາຍຮ້ອຍຄົນທີ່ມີຊື່ ບໍ່ມີເອກະສານເອກະລາດຢືນຢັນ ແລະ ນັກປະຫວັດສາດທີ່ໄດ້ກວດເບິ່ງຊີວະປະຫວັດຂອງ Hill ພົບຮູບແບບການເວົ້າເກີນຄວາມຈິງ ໃນສ່ວນອື່ນຂອງເລື່ອງລາວຊີວິດຂອງລາວ. ຄວນຖືວ່າກອບແນວຄິດນີ້ ຄືການສັງເຄາະຂອງ Hill ແລະ ນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳທີ່ຖືກອ້າງອີງ ຄືຕົວຢ່າງປະກອບ ບໍ່ແມ່ນການສຶກສາຄົ້ນຄວ້າທີ່ມີເອກະສານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ບົດທາງລຶກລັບ ຖືກນຳສະເໜີດ້ວຍຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈເທົ່າກັບບົດປະຕິບັດ ແຕ່ໂດຍພື້ນຖານແລ້ວບໍ່ມີຫຼັກຖານຮອງຮັບ.$$, 'body', $$ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ, ຈິດໃຕ້ສຳນຶກ, "ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກທີ 6" ທີ່ອ້າງເຖິງ ແລະ ແນວຄິດການສຳພັດໂດຍກົງກັບ "ປັນຍາອັນໄຮ້ຂອບເຂດ" (infinite intelligence) ຖືກກ່າວອ້າງດ້ວຍນ້ຳສຽງໜັກແໜ້ນເທົ່າກັບຄຳແນະນຳການຂຽນແຜນທີ່ຊັດເຈນ. ຄຳແນະນຳການວາງແຜນ ມີເຫດຜົນ ບໍ່ວ່າກົນໄກທາງລຶກລັບເບື້ອງຫຼັງມັນຈະຈິງ ຫຼື ບໍ່; ປຶ້ມນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ຂໍໃຫ້ທ່ານສັງເກດຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງນັ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ອະຄະຕິຄົນລອດ (survivorship bias) ໄຫຼຢູ່ຕະຫຼອດປຶ້ມທັງເຫຼັ້ມ.$$, 'body', $$ທຸກຫຼັກການ ຖືກອະທິບາຍປະກອບດ້ວຍຄົນທີ່ກາຍເປັນເສດຖີໃຫຍ່. ປຶ້ມນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ເວົ້າຫຍັງເລີຍ ກ່ຽວກັບຄົນຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ທີ່ໄລ່ຕາມຄວາມປາຖະໜາທີ່ລຸກໄໝ້, ຂຽນແຜນທີ່ຊັດເຈນ, ພາກພຽນຜ່ານອຸປະສັກ ແລ້ວກໍ່ຍັງບໍ່ສຳເລັດ — ມັກເປັນຍ້ອນສະຖານະການ, ຈັງຫວະເວລາ, ການຈຳແນກ ຫຼື ຄວາມໂຊກ ທີ່ກອບແນວຄິດນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ຄິດເຖິງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ມັນສະທ້ອນສົມມຸດຖານແບບອາເມລິກາຕົ້ນສະຕະວັດທີ 20 ທີ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ນຳໃຊ້ໄດ້ທົ່ວໂລກ.$$, 'body', $$ເກືອບທຸກຕົວຢ່າງເປັນຜູ້ຊາຍ ສ່ວນຫຼາຍເປັນນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳຜິວຂາວອາເມລິກາ ທີ່ດຳເນີນງານໃນສະພາບແວດລ້ອມເສດຖະກິດ ແລະ ກົດໝາຍສະເພາະ ແລະ ປຶ້ມນີ້ເວົ້າໜ້ອຍຫຼາຍ ກ່ຽວກັບຜູ້ອ່ານທີ່ປະເຊີນອຸປະສັກທາງໂຄງສ້າງ — ທຶນຈຳກັດ, ຕະຫຼາດຈຳກັດ, ການຈຳແນກ, ສະຖາບັນທີ່ບໍ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ — ທີ່ບໍ່ວ່າຄວາມຊັດເຈນຂອງຈຸດປະສົງຫຼາຍປານໃດ ກໍ່ບໍ່ອາດແກ້ໄຂດ້ວຍຕົວມັນເອງໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄຳແນະນຳບາງຢ່າງ ຢູ່ໄດ້ດີກວ່າທິດສະດີເບື້ອງຫຼັງມັນ.$$, 'body', $$ການຂຽນເປົ້າໝາຍສະເພາະລົງພ້ອມກຳນົດເວລາ, ການສ້າງແຜນທີ່ໃຊ້ໄດ້ຈິງ ແລະ ແກ້ໄຂມັນແທນທີ່ຈະປະຖິ້ມເປົ້າໝາຍ, ການຊອກຫາຄູ່ຮ່ວມທີ່ເສີມກັນ ແລະ ການຖືວ່າອຸປະສັກເປັນຂໍ້ມູນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄຳຕັດສິນ ລ້ວນແຕ່ເປັນການປະຕິບັດທີ່ມີເຫດຜົນ ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຂອງ Hill ກ່ຽວກັບຈິດໃຕ້ສຳນຶກ ຫຼື ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກທີ 6. ໂຄງຮ່າງພາກປະຕິບັດຂອງປຶ້ມ ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖືກວ່າກົນໄກທີ່ລາວກ່າວອ້າງ.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອ່ານດ້ວຍວິທີນີ້ — ໃນຖານະຄຳແຖະງການທີ່ມີອິດທິພົນ ແລະ ສຳຄັນທາງປະຫວັດສາດ ຂອງປັດຊະຍາການວາງແຜນ ແລະ ຄວາມພາກພຽນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຈິດຕະວິທະຍາທີ່ມີເອກະສານພິສູດ — ຄຸນປະໂຫຍດທີ່ຄົງຢູ່ຂອງປຶ້ມ ຍັງເປັນຈິງ: ຈຸດປະສົງທີ່ຊັດເຈນ, ແຜນທີ່ຂຽນໄວ້ພ້ອມກຳນົດເວລາ, ຄວາມຮູ້ທີ່ຈັດລະບຽບ, ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມທີ່ເປັນພັນທະມິດ ແລະ ຄວາມພາກພຽນຜ່ານຈຸດທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນຫຼາຍເລີກລົ້ມ ຄືລາຍການກວດສອບທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດແທ້ໆ. ຄຳອະທິບາຍວ່າເປັນຫຍັງມັນຈຶ່ງໄດ້ຜົນ ແລະ ຄວາມງຽບຂອງມັນກ່ຽວກັບໃຜທີ່ລົ້ມເຫຼວ ສົມຄວນໄດ້ຮັບການກວດສອບຢ່າງລະມັດລະວັງເທົ່າກັບເລື່ອງຄວາມສຳເລັດອື່ນໆທີ່ພິສູດບໍ່ໄດ້.$$)
  ))
  ),
  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Definite Major Purpose$$, 'body', $$A single, specific, written goal — with an amount or outcome and a deadline — that Hill treats as the necessary foundation for every other principle in the book.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Burning Desire$$, 'body', $$A desire so definite and emotionally intense that it dominates a person's thinking, as opposed to a passive wish that fades under the first obstacle.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Autosuggestion$$, 'body', $$Deliberate, repeated self-communication aimed at impressing a chosen thought on the subconscious mind, typically through spoken or written repetition.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Specialized Knowledge$$, 'body', $$Knowledge organized and directed toward a definite purpose through a definite plan — the only kind of knowledge Hill treats as usable power.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Synthetic Imagination$$, 'body', $$Combining existing ideas, plans, or concepts into new arrangements; Hill's more practical and trainable form of imagination.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Creative Imagination$$, 'body', $$Hill's claimed faculty for direct, sudden insight stimulated by intense desire, linked elsewhere in the book to an asserted "sixth sense" — the least evidence-backed idea in the framework.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The Master Mind$$, 'body', $$Coordination of knowledge and effort between two or more people in genuine harmony, which Hill claims produces a distinct force beyond the sum of their individual efforts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Six Ghosts of Fear$$, 'body', $$Hill's list of learned fears — poverty, criticism, ill health, loss of love, old age, and death — presented as the most common obstacles standing between desire and achievement.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈຸດປະສົງຫຼັກທີ່ຊັດເຈນ (Definite Major Purpose)$$, 'body', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍດຽວ, ສະເພາະ, ຂຽນໄວ້ — ພ້ອມຈຳນວນ ຫຼື ຜົນລັບ ແລະ ກຳນົດເວລາ — ທີ່ Hill ຖືວ່າເປັນພື້ນຖານທີ່ຈຳເປັນສຳລັບທຸກຫຼັກການອື່ນໃນປຶ້ມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມປາຖະໜາທີ່ລຸກໄໝ້ (Burning Desire)$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມປາຖະໜາທີ່ຊັດເຈນ ແລະ ຮຸນແຮງທາງອາລົມ ຈົນຄອບງຳຄວາມຄິດຂອງຄົນຄົນໜຶ່ງ ກົງກັນຂ້າມກັບຄວາມຢາກແບບຮັບຮູ້ຢູ່ຫ່າງໆ ທີ່ຫາຍໄປເມື່ອພົບອຸປະສັກທຳອິດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ (Autosuggestion)$$, 'body', $$ການສື່ສານກັບຕົນເອງຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ແລະ ຊ້ຳໆ ເພື່ອສະຫຼັກຄວາມຄິດທີ່ເລືອກໄວ້ ໃສ່ຈິດໃຕ້ສຳນຶກ ໂດຍປົກກະຕິຜ່ານການເວົ້າ ຫຼື ຂຽນຊ້ຳ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມຮູ້ສະເພາະທາງ (Specialized Knowledge)$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມຮູ້ທີ່ຖືກຈັດລະບຽບ ແລະ ພຸ້ງໄປສູ່ຈຸດປະສົງທີ່ຊັດເຈນ ຜ່ານແຜນທີ່ຊັດເຈນ — ຄວາມຮູ້ແບບດຽວທີ່ Hill ຖືວ່າເປັນອຳນາດທີ່ໃຊ້ໄດ້ຈິງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈິນຕະນາການແບບປະສົມ (Synthetic Imagination)$$, 'body', $$ການລວມແນວຄິດ, ແຜນ ຫຼື ຄວາມຄິດທີ່ມີຢູ່ແລ້ວ ໃຫ້ເປັນການຈັດຮຽງໃໝ່; ຈິນຕະນາການແບບປະຕິບັດ ແລະ ຝຶກໄດ້ຫຼາຍກວ່າ ຂອງ Hill.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈິນຕະນາການແບບສ້າງສັນ (Creative Imagination)$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມສາມາດທີ່ Hill ອ້າງເຖິງ ສຳລັບຄວາມເຂົ້າໃຈໂດຍກົງ ແລະ ທັນທີ ທີ່ຖືກກະຕຸ້ນດ້ວຍຄວາມປາຖະໜາທີ່ຮຸນແຮງ ເຊື່ອມໂຍງໃນບ່ອນອື່ນຂອງປຶ້ມກັບ "ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກທີ 6" ທີ່ອ້າງເຖິງ — ແນວຄິດທີ່ມີຫຼັກຖານໜ້ອຍທີ່ສຸດໃນກອບແນວຄິດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Master Mind$$, 'body', $$ການປະສານຄວາມຮູ້ ແລະ ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມລະຫວ່າງຄົນສອງຄົນ ຫຼື ຫຼາຍກວ່າ ດ້ວຍຄວາມກົມກຽວແທ້ຈິງ ຊຶ່ງ Hill ອ້າງວ່າຜະລິດພະລັງທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງ ໃຫຍ່ກວ່າຜົນລວມຄວາມພະຍາຍາມສ່ວນຕົວຂອງພວກເຂົາ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມຢ້ານກົວຫົກຢ່າງ (Six Ghosts of Fear)$$, 'body', $$ລາຍການຄວາມຢ້ານກົວທີ່ຮຽນຮູ້ມາຂອງ Hill — ຄວາມທຸກຍາກ, ການວິຈານ, ການເຈັບປ່ວຍ, ການສູນເສຍຄວາມຮັກ, ຄວາມແກ່ຊະລາ ແລະ ຄວາມຕາຍ — ນຳສະເໜີວ່າເປັນອຸປະສັກທີ່ພົບເລື້ອຍທີ່ສຸດ ລະຫວ່າງຄວາມປາຖະໜາ ແລະ ຄວາມສຳເລັດ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Hill's methodology claim — a twenty-year, Carnegie-commissioned study of hundreds of named tycoons — is historically disputed. Does that undermine the practical advice in the book, or can a technique be useful even if its origin story is embellished?$$, $$Autosuggestion and the six-step desire exercise are presented with the same confidence as each other, yet one has some overlap with later, testable research on self-affirmation while the other is essentially untestable. How do you tell, while reading, which of Hill's claims deserve that confidence?$$, $$The "three feet from gold" story is memorable specifically because persistence paid off. What would the lesson be if you also had access to the stories of people who kept digging for years past the point where the gold genuinely was not there?$$, $$Hill's examples are drawn almost entirely from early-20th-century American industrialists with access to capital, markets, and legal protections not available to everyone. Which parts of the framework depend on that access, and which would hold up for someone starting with far less?$$, $$The Master Mind principle argues genuine harmony between allies produces something more than the sum of their effort. Where in your own experience has a collaboration clearly outperformed what the same people would have produced separately — and where has a similar-looking group failed to produce that effect?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງກ່ຽວກັບວິທີການຄົ້ນຄວ້າຂອງ Hill — ການສຶກສາ 20 ປີ ທີ່ Carnegie ວ່າຈ້າງ ກ່ຽວກັບນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳຫຼາຍຮ້ອຍຄົນທີ່ມີຊື່ — ຖືກໂຕ້ແຍ້ງທາງປະຫວັດສາດ. ນັ້ນທຳລາຍຄຳແນະນຳໃນທາງປະຕິບັດຂອງປຶ້ມແທ້ບໍ ຫຼື ເຕັກນິກໜຶ່ງຍັງເປັນປະໂຫຍດໄດ້ ເຖິງແມ່ນເລື່ອງທີ່ມາຂອງມັນຖືກເວົ້າເກີນຄວາມຈິງ?$$, $$ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ ແລະ ແບບຝຶກຫັດຄວາມປາຖະໜາຫົກຂັ້ນຕອນ ຖືກນຳສະເໜີດ້ວຍຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈເທົ່າກັນ ແຕ່ອັນໜຶ່ງມີຄວາມທັບຊ້ອນກັບງານຄົ້ນຄວ້າພາຍຫຼັງທີ່ທົດສອບໄດ້ກ່ຽວກັບການຢືນຢັນຕົນເອງ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ອີກອັນໜຶ່ງທົດສອບບໍ່ໄດ້ໂດຍພື້ນຖານ. ໃນຂະນະອ່ານ ທ່ານແຍກແຍະແນວໃດ ວ່າຄຳກ່າວອ້າງໃດຂອງ Hill ສົມຄວນໄດ້ຮັບຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈນັ້ນ?$$, $$ເລື່ອງ "ຫ່າງທອງພຽງສາມຟຸດ" ຈື່ໄດ້ງ່າຍ ເພາະຄວາມພາກພຽນນັ້ນໄດ້ຜົນ. ບົດຮຽນຈະເປັນແນວໃດ ຖ້າທ່ານມີໂອກາດເຫັນເລື່ອງຂອງຄົນທີ່ຂຸດຕໍ່ໄປອີກຫຼາຍປີ ຫຼັງຈາກຈຸດທີ່ທອງບໍ່ໄດ້ຢູ່ຢ່າງແທ້ຈິງ?$$, $$ຕົວຢ່າງຂອງ Hill ເກືອບທັງໝົດ ດຶງມາຈາກນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳອາເມລິກາຕົ້ນສະຕະວັດທີ 20 ທີ່ເຂົ້າເຖິງທຶນ, ຕະຫຼາດ ແລະ ການປົກປ້ອງທາງກົດໝາຍ ທີ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ມີໃຫ້ທຸກຄົນ. ສ່ວນໃດຂອງກອບແນວຄິດນີ້ ອີງໃສ່ການເຂົ້າເຖິງນັ້ນ ແລະ ສ່ວນໃດຈະຍັງໃຊ້ໄດ້ ສຳລັບຄົນທີ່ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນດ້ວຍໜ້ອຍກວ່າຫຼາຍ?$$, $$ຫຼັກການ Master Mind ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຄວາມກົມກຽວແທ້ຈິງລະຫວ່າງພັນທະມິດ ຜະລິດສິ່ງທີ່ຫຼາຍກວ່າຜົນລວມຄວາມພະຍາຍາມຂອງພວກເຂົາ. ໃນປະສົບການຂອງທ່ານເອງ ຢູ່ບ່ອນໃດ ທີ່ຄວາມຮ່ວມມືປະສົບຜົນສຳເລັດຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ ເໜືອກວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ຄົນກຸ່ມດຽວກັນຈະຜະລິດໄດ້ແຍກກັນ — ແລະ ບ່ອນໃດ ທີ່ກຸ່ມທີ່ເບິ່ງຄ້າຍຄືກັນ ບໍ່ໄດ້ຜະລິດຜົນນັ້ນ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Hill's central wager is that achievement follows a definite, learnable method, illustrated with — but not proven by — the lives of famous industrialists he claims to have studied.$$, $$A vague wish rarely survives a setback; Hill's six-step method converts desire into a specific written goal, a deadline, a plan, and daily repetition.$$, $$Autosuggestion is Hill's technique for repeatedly impressing a chosen goal on the subconscious mind — a claim asserted with confidence but never tested in the book itself.$$, $$Knowledge is only potential power; it becomes actual power, in Hill's framework, when organized, specialized, and applied to a definite plan.$$, $$Hill splits imagination into synthetic (recombining known ideas, well-supported as a practical technique) and creative (a claimed direct channel to insight, the book's most speculative idea).$$, $$A failed plan signals a flawed plan, not a flawed goal — Hill's instruction is to revise the plan and keep the desire intact.$$, $$Decisiveness, in Hill's account, is less about speed and more about resisting other people's opinions once a plan is set.$$, $$The "three feet from gold" story anchors Hill's central caution: many people quit immediately before a breakthrough — a real insight, though the anecdote alone proves nothing.$$, $$The Master Mind principle claims that genuine harmony between allied people produces a force greater than their combined individual effort.$$, $$Hill names six learned fears — poverty, criticism, ill health, loss of love, old age, death — as the more common obstacle to achievement, treatable through honest self-analysis rather than as an established clinical model.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຫຼັກຂອງ Hill ຄື ຄວາມສຳເລັດຕິດຕາມມາຈາກວິທີການທີ່ຊັດເຈນ ແລະ ຮຽນຮູ້ໄດ້ ອະທິບາຍດ້ວຍ — ແຕ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ພິສູດໂດຍ — ຊີວິດຂອງນັກອຸດສາຫະກຳທີ່ມີຊື່ສຽງ ທີ່ລາວອ້າງວ່າໄດ້ສຶກສາ.$$, $$ຄວາມຢາກທີ່ບໍ່ຊັດເຈນ ນ້ອຍທີ່ຈະຢູ່ລອດຜ່ານອຸປະສັກ; ວິທີການຫົກຂັ້ນຕອນຂອງ Hill ປ່ຽນຄວາມປາຖະໜາໃຫ້ກາຍເປັນເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຂຽນໄວ້ສະເພາະ, ກຳນົດເວລາ, ແຜນ ແລະ ການເຮັດຊ້ຳທຸກມື້.$$, $$ການສະກົດຈິດຕົນເອງ ຄືເຕັກນິກຂອງ Hill ສຳລັບການສະຫຼັກເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ເລືອກໄວ້ ໃສ່ຈິດໃຕ້ສຳນຶກຊ້ຳໆ — ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີ່ຖືກກ່າວດ້ວຍຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈ ແຕ່ບໍ່ເຄີຍຖືກທົດສອບໃນປຶ້ມເອງ.$$, $$ຄວາມຮູ້ ເປັນພຽງອຳນາດທີ່ຊ້ອນຢູ່; ມັນຈະກາຍເປັນອຳນາດແທ້ຈິງ ໃນກອບແນວຄິດຂອງ Hill ເມື່ອຖືກຈັດລະບຽບ, ສະເພາະທາງ ແລະ ນຳໄປໃຊ້ຕໍ່ແຜນທີ່ຊັດເຈນ.$$, $$Hill ແບ່ງຈິນຕະນາການອອກເປັນແບບປະສົມ (ການລວມແນວຄິດທີ່ຮູ້ຈັກແລ້ວ ຊຶ່ງໄດ້ຮັບການສະໜັບສະໜູນດີ ໃນຖານະເຕັກນິກປະຕິບັດ) ແລະ ແບບສ້າງສັນ (ຊ່ອງທາງໂດຍກົງທີ່ອ້າງເຖິງ ໄປສູ່ຄວາມເຂົ້າໃຈ ຄືແນວຄິດທີ່ຄາດເດົາທີ່ສຸດຂອງປຶ້ມ).$$, $$ແຜນທີ່ລົ້ມເຫຼວ ບົ່ງບອກແຜນທີ່ບົກຜ່ອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ບົກຜ່ອງ — ຄຳແນະນຳຂອງ Hill ຄືໃຫ້ແກ້ໄຂແຜນ ແລະ ຮັກສາຄວາມປາຖະໜາໄວ້.$$, $$ຄວາມຊັດເຈນໃນການຕັດສິນໃຈ ໃນຄຳອະທິບາຍຂອງ Hill ບໍ່ໄດ້ເນັ້ນທີ່ຄວາມໄວ ແຕ່ເນັ້ນທີ່ການຕ້ານທານຄວາມຄິດເຫັນຂອງຄົນອື່ນ ເມື່ອແຜນຖືກກຳນົດແລ້ວ.$$, $$ເລື່ອງ "ຫ່າງທອງພຽງສາມຟຸດ" ຄືພື້ນຖານຄຳເຕືອນຫຼັກຂອງ Hill: ຫຼາຍຄົນເລີກລົ້ມທັນທີກ່ອນຈຸດພິສະຫານ — ຄວາມເຂົ້າໃຈທີ່ແທ້ຈິງ ເຖິງແມ່ນເລື່ອງເລົ່ານັ້ນເອງບໍ່ໄດ້ພິສູດຫຍັງ.$$, $$ຫຼັກການ Master Mind ອ້າງວ່າ ຄວາມກົມກຽວແທ້ຈິງລະຫວ່າງຄົນທີ່ເປັນພັນທະມິດ ຜະລິດພະລັງທີ່ໃຫຍ່ກວ່າຜົນລວມຄວາມພະຍາຍາມສ່ວນຕົວຂອງພວກເຂົາ.$$, $$Hill ລະບຸຄວາມຢ້ານກົວທີ່ຮຽນຮູ້ມາຫົກຢ່າງ — ຄວາມທຸກຍາກ, ການວິຈານ, ການເຈັບປ່ວຍ, ການສູນເສຍຄວາມຮັກ, ຄວາມແກ່ຊະລາ, ຄວາມຕາຍ — ວ່າເປັນອຸປະສັກທີ່ພົບເລື້ອຍກວ່າຕໍ່ຄວາມສຳເລັດ ຮັກສາໄດ້ຜ່ານການວິເຄາະຕົນເອງຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນໂມເດວທາງຄລີນິກທີ່ຖືກຢັ້ງຢືນ.$$]
where book_id = '2fcc3349-17e4-4718-b840-84f96f1f36e0';

-- Sapiens — Yuval Noah Harari
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A sweeping argument that shared stories, not individual strength, are what let strangers cooperate and built the world humans live in today.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງກວ້າງໆທີ່ວ່າ ເລື່ອງເລົ່າຮ່ວມກັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມແຂງແຮງສ່ວນຕົວ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຄົນແປກໜ້າຮ່ວມມືກັນໄດ້ ແລະ ສ້າງໂລກທີ່ມະນຸດອາໄສຢູ່ທຸກມື້ນີ້.$$,
  deck_en = $$Yuval Noah Harari's argument is that Homo sapiens did not conquer the planet through brute strength or superior individual intelligence, but through a uniquely human talent: the ability to invent and collectively believe in stories that exist nowhere except in shared imagination — money, nations, corporations, religions, and human rights among them. This lesson traces that argument from the cognitive revolution through the agricultural revolution, the slow unification of humankind under money, empire, and religion, the alliance between science and capital, and Harari's pointed question about whether any of this progress actually made individual lives happier. It closes with a clear-eyed look at where this sweeping, single-narrative style should be read with real caution.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ Yuval Noah Harari ຄື Homo sapiens ບໍ່ໄດ້ຄອບຄອງໂລກຜ່ານຄວາມແຂງແຮງທາງກາຍ ຫຼື ສະຕິປັນຍາສ່ວນຕົວທີ່ເໜືອກວ່າ ແຕ່ຜ່ານພອນສະຫວັນທີ່ມີແຕ່ມະນຸດເທົ່ານັ້ນ: ຄວາມສາມາດປະດິດ ແລະ ເຊື່ອຮ່ວມກັນໃນເລື່ອງເລົ່າທີ່ບໍ່ມີຢູ່ຈິງນອກຈາກໃນຈິນຕະນາການຮ່ວມ — ເງິນ, ຊາດ, ບໍລິສັດ, ສາສະໜາ ແລະ ສິດທິມະນຸດ ລ້ວນແຕ່ຢູ່ໃນນັ້ນ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະລຳດັບຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງນັ້ນ ຕັ້ງແຕ່ການປະຕິວັດການຮັບຮູ້ ຜ່ານການປະຕິວັດການກະສິກຳ, ການລວມມະນຸດຊາດຢ່າງຄ່ອຍໆພາຍໃຕ້ເງິນ, ອານາຈັກ ແລະ ສາສະໜາ, ພັນທະມິດລະຫວ່າງວິທະຍາສາດ ແລະ ທຶນ, ແລະ ຄຳຖາມທີ່ Harari ຈິກແທງກ່ຽວກັບວ່າຄວາມກ້າວໜ້າທັງໝົດນີ້ ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດຄົນແຕ່ລະຄົນສຸກຂຶ້ນແທ້ບໍ. ປິດທ້າຍດ້ວຍການພິຈາລະນາຢ່າງກົງໄປກົງມາ ວ່າຮູບແບບການເລົ່າເລື່ອງດຽວແບບກວ້າງນີ້ ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍຄວາມລະມັດລະວັງໃນຈຸດໃດ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The cognitive revolution: how fiction created human cooperation$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari dates the true beginning of human history not to the appearance of our species, but to a change in how Homo sapiens' minds worked roughly seventy thousand years ago — a shift he calls the cognitive revolution. Before this point, sapiens was one of several human species walking the earth, sharing the landscape with Neanderthals and others, and not obviously superior to them in strength, tool use, or even brain size.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$What changed was language, but not language in the narrow sense of naming things. Harari argues the decisive leap was the ability to communicate about things that do not exist at all — myths, spirits, and later gods, nations, and legal entities. Other primates can warn each other about a real lion nearby; only sapiens could gather around a fire and agree that an unseen ancestor spirit protects the tribe, and act, hunt, and organize as if that belief were as solid as the lion.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This capacity solved a hard ceiling on cooperation. Chimpanzees and other social animals can only coordinate in small groups bound by direct personal familiarity and gossip-based trust, typically capped at roughly a hundred and fifty individuals — beyond that, nobody can keep track of who owes whom what. Sapiens broke through that ceiling because a shared fiction, not personal acquaintance, could bind together thousands or millions of strangers who would never meet, as long as they all believed the same story.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Sapiens did not out-muscle other species — it out-imagined them, using shared fictions to cooperate at a scale no other animal can reach.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Three layers of reality: objective, subjective, and intersubjective$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$To make sense of how a story like "money" or "a nation" can shape the real world, Harari draws a distinction between three kinds of reality that are easy to blur together.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Objective reality.$$, 'body', $$Things that exist independent of anyone's beliefs or feelings — gravity, radioactivity, the boiling point of water. They would exist exactly the same if every human vanished tomorrow.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Subjective reality.$$, 'body', $$Things that exist only inside one individual's mind — a private pain, a dream, a personal fear. It is entirely real to the person experiencing it, but it disappears the moment that one mind stops.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Intersubjective reality.$$, 'body', $$Things that exist only because a large number of people believe in and coordinate around them together — money, borders, corporations, laws, human rights. No single believer controls them, and they vanish only if the whole network of belief collapses at once.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Intersubjective reality$$, 'body', $$A category of things — money, nations, religions, corporations — that are not physically real in the way a rock is real, yet are far more powerful than most physical objects because enormous numbers of strangers coordinate their behavior around believing in them together.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari's point is not that intersubjective things are illusions to be dismissed. A corporation like a car manufacturer cannot be pointed to or touched, yet it can own factories, sign contracts, and be sued in court, because legal systems and enough people jointly agree it exists. To understand human history, Harari argues, these fictions must be taken as seriously as physical forces — because functionally, for the humans living inside them, they are just as real.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Money, nations, and corporations are not physically real, but they are intersubjectively real — and that makes them just as powerful.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The agricultural revolution: history's biggest trap?$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The conventional story of agriculture is one of unambiguous progress: humans learned to control their food supply and traded the uncertainty of foraging for the security of the farm. Harari deliberately inverts this narrative, framing farming as arguably history's biggest trap — a case where domesticating wheat may have domesticated the domesticator.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He points to evidence that early farmers were, by several measures, worse off than the foragers who came before them: skeletal remains show more spinal damage from repetitive labor, more signs of malnutrition, and shorter average stature than hunter-gatherer populations. Diets that had once drawn on dozens of wild plants and animals narrowed down to a handful of staple crops, which raised the risk of famine when a single harvest failed and increased exposure to new diseases that spread more easily in crowded, settled communities living close to domesticated animals.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The luxury trap$$, 'body', $$Harari's term for innovations that promise an easier life but instead create new dependencies and new obligations, leaving people working harder than before to sustain a standard they cannot easily walk away from once adopted.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Population did rise sharply after agriculture took hold, but Harari separates two different measures of success: the total number of bodies a food system can support is not the same as how well any individual within that system actually lives. More mouths fed adequately is a very different outcome from more mouths fed poorly, and he argues agriculture mostly delivered the latter — while also binding people permanently to fields, storable surplus, and the private property and hierarchy that surplus made possible, in ways that were nearly impossible to reverse once whole populations depended on them.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Farming fed more people, but by several measures it made each of those individual lives harder — a trade that nobody consciously chose or voted on.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Money, empire, and religion: three forces that unified strangers$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Once agriculture had produced larger, denser populations, a new problem emerged: the trust-based cooperation of the small band was no longer enough to organize thousands of people who did not know each other, spoke different dialects, or worshipped different spirits. Harari argues that history's next great leap was solved by three overlapping systems, each itself an intersubjective fiction, each capable of uniting total strangers around a shared order.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Money$$, 'body', $$Not gold or paper in itself, but a universal system of mutual trust — the most efficient one ever devised. A farmer who has never met a soldier, and shares no language or god with them, can still trade with them through the shared fiction of price, because both trust that a stranger down the road will accept the same currency in turn.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Empire is the second unifier. Harari uses the term without moral judgment, defining it simply as any political order that rules many distinct peoples and territories under a single system. Empires have historically been among the most durable and successful political structures precisely because they impose common law, currency, infrastructure, and often language across enormously diverse populations — erasing local particularity while manufacturing a shared elite culture that outlasts any single ruler.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Religion is the third. Universal religions — those claiming a truth meant for everyone, not just one tribe or bloodline — offered a super-human order that could justify cooperation among strangers by appeal to a shared cosmic or divine law rather than kinship, giving people with no personal relationship a reason to trust and cooperate with one another anyway.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Money, empire, and religion are three different intersubjective fictions that each solved the same problem: getting total strangers to trust and cooperate.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The unification of humankind$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari argues that human history, whatever else it is, has a directional bias — not necessarily a moral improvement, but a steady move from many small, isolated, independent cultures toward fewer and larger interconnected ones. Ten thousand years ago, thousands of separate human groups had no contact with each other at all; today virtually the entire species is folded into a single interacting economic and political system.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The mechanisms behind this were rarely gentle. Trade routes spread goods, technologies, and ideas across vast distances long before anyone involved understood the whole network they were part of. Conquest imposed common law, currency, and language across huge and previously unconnected territories. And shared belief systems gradually absorbed or replaced local ones, merging separate mythologies and customs into broader, shared worldviews.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari is explicit that this is not a story to romanticize. Unification proceeded largely through violence, subjugation, and the deliberate or incidental destruction of countless smaller cultures and languages along the way. He frames it as an amoral historical trend rather than a triumph — the number of independent cultural experiments on the planet has been shrinking for millennia, regardless of whether that shrinkage made any individual life better.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Humanity has been consolidating from thousands of separate cultures into one interconnected system for millennia — a trend driven as much by conquest as by trade.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The scientific revolution: the discovery of ignorance$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari locates the true novelty of the scientific revolution not in any single invention, but in a psychological and cultural shift roughly five hundred years ago: a new willingness to openly admit collective ignorance, rather than assuming that ancient texts and traditions already contained all of the important answers.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The discovery of ignorance$$, 'body', $$Harari's name for the founding insight of modern science — the admission that "we do not know" something important, which opens the door to deliberately investigating it, rather than assuming existing scripture or tradition has already settled the question.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$That admission mattered because it made knowledge open-ended and cumulative instead of fixed: observe, test, revise, repeat, rather than defer permanently to inherited authority. Crucially, this new science was rarely pursued for curiosity's sake alone — it was explicitly tied to power, aimed at producing useful knowledge for navigation, medicine, agriculture, and warfare, which is exactly why it began attracting funding and political backing at a scale earlier eras of scholarship never received.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Modern science began not with a new tool, but with a new admission — that huge, important questions had no answer yet, and were worth funding to find one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Science, capital, and empire: an alliance that remade the world$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari argues the scientific revolution and European imperial expansion were never two separate stories — they were tightly entangled from the start. Mapping, conquering, and administering distant lands required scientific tools: navigation, cartography, botany, and medicine among them. In turn, imperial ambition funded and motivated a great deal of the scientific inquiry of the period.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Capital was the connective tissue. Expensive, risky expeditions to unfamiliar continents needed financing well beyond what any single monarch's treasury could reliably supply, which is where an expanding system of credit and investment — joint-stock companies, loans issued against expected future returns — stepped in, willing to fund risky ventures in exchange for a share of whatever profit resulted.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The result, in Harari's telling, was a self-reinforcing loop: credit funded exploration and scientific inquiry, exploration and conquest produced new wealth and resources, and that new wealth repaid the credit and financed the next round of expeditions. He argues this particular alliance of curiosity, capital, and conquest — more than any single technological edge — is a large part of why certain powers overtook other advanced civilizations that, at earlier points, had comparable or superior technology but lacked this specific combination.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Science funded empire and empire funded science, with credit as the mechanism that let each one bankroll the other.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Capitalism as a belief system: credit, growth, and trust in the future$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari treats capitalism itself as another intersubjective fiction — one resting on a specific belief about time: that the total economic pie will keep growing, and so it is rational to lend money today against wealth that does not exist yet, but is trusted to be created tomorrow.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Credit$$, 'body', $$Money that exists now against wealth that does not exist yet, made possible only by a shared trust that the future economy will be larger than the present one. Harari treats the willingness to extend this trust broadly as one of the defining features of the modern age.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is a sharp break from eras when the total economic pie was widely treated as roughly fixed, which made one person's profit look like it necessarily came at another's loss, and made lending at interest suspect or outright forbidden in many traditions. Modern credit reversed that assumption, treating the future confidently as bigger than the present, and funding present enterprise on the promise of growth still to come.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari is careful to note this belief is not neutral or costless. Trust in perpetual growth tends to require and reinforce ongoing technological progress and expanding resource use, and it is fragile in a specific way: a crisis of confidence in future growth — a credit crunch, a market crash — is as much a crisis of shared belief as it is a crisis of physical resources.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Modern capitalism runs on a collective bet that tomorrow's economy will be bigger than today's — and that bet is a belief system, not a law of nature.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The industrial revolution: family and community give way to state and market$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari treats the industrial revolution as repeating the same pattern as the agricultural one: an enormous leap in raw productive power, paired with a genuine upheaval in how humans actually lived their daily lives — and, again, not an unambiguous improvement for everyone it touched.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Before industrialization, the extended family and the tight local community functioned as an almost complete social unit, providing welfare, healthcare, education, employment, and a sense of belonging and meaning all at once. Over the industrial era, Harari argues, the state and the market steadily absorbed each of these functions in turn — pensions and healthcare from the state, jobs and goods from the market — leaving the extended family and village community with a far smaller role than they had held for millennia.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He treats this as a genuine trade-off rather than a simple decline or a simple liberation. Individuals gained real independence from the sometimes oppressive and inescapable obligations of extended kin and village — state pensions and market-based jobs freed people to leave, move, and choose in ways earlier social structures rarely allowed. But they also lost the dense, unconditional web of belonging those older units provided, which Harari links to a rise in modern loneliness and alienation even as material comfort and life expectancy rose sharply.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The state and the market replaced the extended family and village as providers of welfare and belonging — trading unconditional community for individual independence.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Are we happier now? A critique of the progress narrative$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari poses a question most conventional histories skip past: after all of this — agriculture, empire, science, industry — has any of it actually made individual humans happier? He notes that historians usually chart material and political change in exhaustive detail while treating subjective well-being as an afterthought, if they address it directly at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He is candid that this question is extraordinarily hard to answer with any confidence. There is no way to directly access how a medieval peasant or a Paleolithic forager subjectively felt on an ordinary day, so any claim here is necessarily speculative, built on indirect proxies — health and mortality data, social structures, and modern happiness research projected backward onto eras that left no testimony about their inner lives.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$With that caveat firmly in place, Harari sketches reasons to doubt a simple story of ever-rising happiness: material wealth and technological capability have not obviously translated into a matching rise in reported well-being, and some modern happiness research suggests subjective well-being tracks expectations, social comparison, and the strength of community bonds more closely than it tracks absolute material conditions. Each revolution covered in the book produced clear winners and losers rather than a uniform improvement in lived experience, which is precisely why he treats "progress" and "happier" as two different questions that should not be casually collapsed into one.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$More food, more empire, and more science are historical facts; whether any of it made individual humans happier is a much harder, far more speculative question.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Looking ahead: biotechnology and an open question about the future (speculative)$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$In its closing chapters, the book turns forward-looking, speculating about a possible next transition as consequential as the cognitive or agricultural revolutions: the growing human capacity to deliberately redesign biology itself, through genetic engineering, cyborg augmentation, and potentially the creation of non-organic forms of life, rather than simply being shaped by natural selection over long stretches of time.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$It is worth being explicit about what this section is and is not. Harari raises it as an open, unsettled question meant to provoke reflection on what "human" might even mean once a species gains the tools to deliberately re-engineer itself — not as a forecast with a timeline, a confident prediction, or a settled scientific consensus. Read this material as a thought experiment intentionally designed to unsettle, not as a claim about what will actually happen.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari also ties this speculation back to the book's earlier arguments: which biological possibilities actually get pursued and funded is not a purely technical question, but one shaped by the same alliance of capital, state power, and belief systems that drove the scientific revolution and imperial expansion — meaning the future he sketches, if any part of it arrives, will likely be shaped as much by economics and politics as by biology itself.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The book's closing speculation about redesigning biology is explicitly a provocation to think with, not a prediction to take at face value.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Sapiens is written as a sweeping, single-narrative synthesis for a general audience, not a work of original archaeological or historical research, and that shapes both its power and its limits. Read it with the following firmly in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Harari is a synthesizer and popularizer, not a primary historian in most of the fields he covers.$$, 'body', $$The book draws together archaeology, anthropology, economic history, biology, and more, but Harari's own specialist training is in medieval military history. Many of the specific claims about prehistoric life, early agriculture, and ancient economics are contested, heavily simplified, or actively disputed by specialists working directly in those fields — the confident narrative voice can make settled-sounding claims feel more certain than the underlying research actually is.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The sweeping, single-narrative style tends to flatten regional variation and counter-examples.$$, 'body', $$Painting tens of thousands of years and every inhabited continent with one connected story is what makes the book readable, but it necessarily smooths over enormous regional and chronological diversity — the agricultural transition, for instance, unfolded differently across Mesopotamia, China, Mesoamerica, and elsewhere, with more variation and more exceptions than a single global narrative can hold.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The happiness and well-being claims are especially speculative and hard to verify historically.$$, 'body', $$As the "are we happier now" section makes clear, there is no direct evidence of how people in the deep past subjectively felt. Harari is himself careful to flag the speculative nature of this material, and it deserves to be read with at least as much caution as he applies to it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$"Agriculture was a trap" is a deliberately provocative thesis, not a settled consensus.$$, 'body', $$It is a genuinely useful corrective to an overly simple progress story, and it is grounded in real skeletal and demographic evidence — but many archaeologists and economic historians would frame the same evidence with more nuance, noting real regional variation in how farming affected health, diet, and quality of life rather than treating the "trap" framing as a settled verdict.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The book compresses enormous, still-debated academic arguments into short, confident chapters.$$, 'body', $$Topics that fill entire specialist careers — the causes of the agricultural transition, the mechanisms of early state formation, the drivers of the scientific revolution — are each handled in a matter of pages. That compression is what makes the book so readable, but it is worth remembering that a short, elegant explanation is not the same thing as a complete or uncontested one.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's real contribution is durable: a genuinely useful set of conceptual tools — shared fiction, intersubjective reality, the three unifiers, the discovery of ignorance — for thinking about why large-scale human cooperation works at all. Treat it as a provocative, big-picture framework to think with, not as a settled textbook account of prehistory and history.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ການປະຕິວັດການຮັບຮູ້: ເລື່ອງແຕ່ງສ້າງຄວາມຮ່ວມມືຂອງມະນຸດແນວໃດ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Harari ກຳນົດຈຸດເລີ່ມຕົ້ນທີ່ແທ້ຈິງຂອງປະຫວັດສາດມະນຸດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຢູ່ທີ່ການປາກົດຕົວຂອງສາຍພັນມະນຸດ ແຕ່ຢູ່ທີ່ການປ່ຽນແປງໃນວິທີການເຮັດວຽກຂອງສະໝອງ Homo sapiens ເມື່ອປະມານ 70,000 ປີກ່ອນ — ການປ່ຽນແປງທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນວ່າ "ການປະຕິວັດການຮັບຮູ້" (cognitive revolution). ກ່ອນຈຸດນີ້ sapiens ເປັນພຽງໜຶ່ງໃນຫຼາຍສາຍພັນມະນຸດທີ່ຍ່າງຢູ່ເທິງໂລກ ຢູ່ຮ່ວມພື້ນທີ່ກັບນີແອນເດີທັລ ແລະ ອື່ນໆ ໂດຍບໍ່ໄດ້ເໜືອກວ່າພວກເຂົາຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ ບໍ່ວ່າຈະເປັນຄວາມແຂງແຮງ, ການໃຊ້ເຄື່ອງມື ຫຼື ແມ່ນແຕ່ຂະໜາດສະໝອງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ປ່ຽນໄປ ຄືພາສາ ແຕ່ບໍ່ແມ່ນພາສາໃນຄວາມໝາຍແຄບໆເລື່ອງການຕັ້ງຊື່ສິ່ງຂອງ. Harari ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ການກ້າວກະໂດດທີ່ຕັດສິນໃຈ ຄືຄວາມສາມາດສື່ສານກ່ຽວກັບສິ່ງທີ່ບໍ່ມີຢູ່ຈິງເລີຍ — ນິທານ, ວິນຍານ ແລະ ຕໍ່ມາ ພະເຈົ້າ, ຊາດ ແລະ ນິຕິບຸກຄົນ. ໄພຣເມດອື່ນສາມາດເຕືອນກັນເລື່ອງເສືອທີ່ຢູ່ໃກ້ ຊຶ່ງເປັນຂອງຈິງ; ມີແຕ່ sapiens ເທົ່ານັ້ນທີ່ສາມາດລວມກັນອ້ອມກອງໄຟ ແລ້ວຕົກລົງກັນວ່າວິນຍານບັນພະບຸລຸດທີ່ເບິ່ງບໍ່ເຫັນ ປົກປ້ອງເຜົ່າຢູ່ ແລ້ວລົງມືປະຕິບັດ, ລ່າສັດ ແລະ ຈັດຕັ້ງ ຄືກັບວ່າຄວາມເຊື່ອນັ້ນແໜ້ນໜາເທົ່າກັບເສືອຕົວຈິງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄວາມສາມາດນີ້ ແກ້ບັນຫາເພດານທີ່ຍາກຂອງຄວາມຮ່ວມມື. ລິງຊິມແປນຊີ ແລະ ສັດສັງຄົມອື່ນໆ ສາມາດປະສານງານກັນໄດ້ພຽງໃນກຸ່ມນ້ອຍ ທີ່ຜູກມັດດ້ວຍຄວາມຄຸ້ນເຄີຍສ່ວນຕົວ ແລະ ຄວາມໄວ້ວາງໃຈຜ່ານການນິນທາ ໂດຍທົ່ວໄປຈຳກັດຢູ່ປະມານໜຶ່ງຮ້ອຍຫ້າສິບໂຕ — ເກີນກວ່ານັ້ນ ບໍ່ມີໃຜຕິດຕາມໄດ້ວ່າໃຜເປັນໜີ້ໃຜຫຍັງແດ່. Sapiens ທະລຸເພດານນັ້ນໄດ້ ເພາະເລື່ອງແຕ່ງຮ່ວມກັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຄຸ້ນເຄີຍສ່ວນຕົວ ສາມາດຜູກມັດຄົນແປກໜ້ານັບພັນ ຫຼື ນັບລ້ານທີ່ບໍ່ເຄີຍພົບກັນ ຕາບໃດທີ່ພວກເຂົາທັງໝົດເຊື່ອເລື່ອງດຽວກັນ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Sapiens ບໍ່ໄດ້ຊະນະດ້ວຍກຳລັງ ແຕ່ຊະນະດ້ວຍຈິນຕະນາການ — ໃຊ້ເລື່ອງແຕ່ງຮ່ວມກັນເພື່ອຮ່ວມມືກັນໃນຂະໜາດທີ່ບໍ່ມີສັດອື່ນເຮັດໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຄວາມເປັນຈິງສາມຊັ້ນ: ຈິງແທ້ (objective), ຈິງໃນໃຈ (subjective) ແລະ ຈິງຮ່ວມກັນ (intersubjective)$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເພື່ອເຂົ້າໃຈວ່າເລື່ອງແຕ່ງ ເຊັ່ນ "ເງິນ" ຫຼື "ຊາດ" ສາມາດປັ້ນແຕ່ງໂລກແທ້ໆໄດ້ແນວໃດ Harari ແຍກຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງລະຫວ່າງຄວາມເປັນຈິງສາມແບບ ທີ່ງ່າຍທີ່ຈະປົນເປກັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄວາມເປັນຈິງແທ້ (Objective reality).$$, 'body', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ມີຢູ່ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບຄວາມເຊື່ອ ຫຼື ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກຂອງໃຜ — ແຮງໂນ້ມຖ່ວງ, ການແຜ່ລັງສີ, ຈຸດເດືອດຂອງນ້ຳ. ພວກມັນຈະຄົງຢູ່ຄືເກົ່າ ແມ່ນວ່າມະນຸດທຸກຄົນຫາຍໄປໃນມື້ອື່ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄວາມເປັນຈິງໃນໃຈ (Subjective reality).$$, 'body', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ມີຢູ່ພຽງໃນຈິດໃຈຂອງຄົນຄົນດຽວ — ຄວາມເຈັບປວດສ່ວນຕົວ, ຄວາມຝັນ, ຄວາມຢ້ານສ່ວນຕົວ. ມັນເປັນຈິງຢ່າງສົມບູນສຳລັບຄົນທີ່ປະສົບມັນ ແຕ່ຫາຍໄປທັນທີທີ່ຈິດໃຈນັ້ນຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄວາມເປັນຈິງຮ່ວມກັນ (Intersubjective reality).$$, 'body', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ມີຢູ່ພຽງເພາະຄົນຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍເຊື່ອ ແລະ ປະສານງານກັນອ້ອມຮອບມັນ — ເງິນ, ພົມແດນ, ບໍລິສັດ, ກົດໝາຍ, ສິດທິມະນຸດ. ບໍ່ມີຜູ້ເຊື່ອຄົນດຽວທີ່ຄວບຄຸມມັນ ແລະ ມັນຈະຫາຍໄປກໍຕໍ່ເມື່ອເຄືອຂ່າຍຄວາມເຊື່ອທັງໝົດລົ້ມລົງພ້ອມກັນ.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ຄວາມເປັນຈິງຮ່ວມກັນ (Intersubjective reality)$$, 'body', $$ໝວດໝູ່ຂອງສິ່ງຕ່າງໆ — ເງິນ, ຊາດ, ສາສະໜາ, ບໍລິສັດ — ທີ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ມີຢູ່ຈິງທາງກາຍະພາບແບບກ້ອນຫີນ ແຕ່ມີພະລັງຫຼາຍກວ່າວັດຖຸທາງກາຍະພາບສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ເພາະຄົນແປກໜ້າຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ປະສານພຶດຕິກຳຂອງຕົນອ້ອມຮອບຄວາມເຊື່ອຮ່ວມກັນນັ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຈຸດຂອງ Harari ບໍ່ແມ່ນວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ຈິງຮ່ວມກັນ ເປັນພຽງພາບລວງທີ່ຄວນປະຕິເສດ. ບໍລິສັດຜະລິດລົດ ບໍ່ສາມາດຊີ້ ຫຼື ຈັບຕ້ອງໄດ້ ແຕ່ມັນສາມາດເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງໂຮງງານ, ເຊັນສັນຍາ ແລະ ຖືກຟ້ອງໃນສານໄດ້ ເພາະລະບົບກົດໝາຍ ແລະ ຄົນຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ຕົກລົງຮ່ວມກັນວ່າມັນມີຢູ່ຈິງ. ເພື່ອເຂົ້າໃຈປະຫວັດສາດມະນຸດ Harari ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຕ້ອງໃຫ້ຄວາມສຳຄັນກັບເລື່ອງແຕ່ງເຫຼົ່ານີ້ ເທົ່າກັບແຮງທາງກາຍະພາບ — ເພາະໃນທາງປະຕິບັດ ສຳລັບຄົນທີ່ອາໄສຢູ່ພາຍໃນມັນ ພວກມັນກໍ່ຈິງເທົ່າໆກັນ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ເງິນ, ຊາດ ແລະ ບໍລິສັດ ບໍ່ໄດ້ຈິງທາງກາຍະພາບ ແຕ່ຈິງຮ່ວມກັນ — ແລະ ນັ້ນເຮັດໃຫ້ພວກມັນມີພະລັງເທົ່າໆກັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ການປະຕິວັດການກະສິກຳ: ດັກທີ່ໃຫຍ່ທີ່ສຸດຂອງປະຫວັດສາດ?$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເລື່ອງເລົ່າທົ່ວໄປກ່ຽວກັບການກະສິກຳ ຄືເລື່ອງຄວາມກ້າວໜ້າທີ່ຊັດເຈນ: ມະນຸດຮຽນຮູ້ທີ່ຈະຄວບຄຸມແຫຼ່ງອາຫານຂອງຕົນ ແລະ ແລກຄວາມບໍ່ແນ່ນອນຂອງການຫາອາຫານປ່າ ດ້ວຍຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງຂອງໄຮ່ນາ. Harari ຕັ້ງໃຈກັບຫົວເລື່ອງເລົ່ານີ້ ໂດຍວາງກອບການກະສິກຳວ່າ ອາດເປັນດັກທີ່ໃຫຍ່ທີ່ສຸດຂອງປະຫວັດສາດ — ກໍລະນີທີ່ການເຮັດໃຫ້ເຂົ້າສາລີເປັນສັດລ້ຽງ ອາດເຮັດໃຫ້ຄົນທີ່ລ້ຽງມັນ ກາຍເປັນສັດລ້ຽງໄປນຳ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ລາວຊີ້ໃຫ້ເຫັນຫຼັກຖານທີ່ວ່າ ຊາວກະສິກອນຍຸກຕົ້ນ ຕາມການວັດແທກຫຼາຍຢ່າງ ຢູ່ໃນສະພາບແຍ່ກວ່າຄົນຫາອາຫານປ່າຮຸ່ນກ່ອນ: ໂຄງກະດູກທີ່ພົບ ສະແດງຄວາມເສຍຫາຍທີ່ກະດູກສັນຫຼັງຈາກແຮງງານຊ້ຳໆຫຼາຍກວ່າ, ອາການຂາດສານອາຫານຫຼາຍກວ່າ ແລະ ຄວາມສູງສະເລ່ຍໜ້ອຍກວ່າ ປະຊາກອນນັກລ່າ-ຫາອາຫານປ່າ. ອາຫານທີ່ເຄີຍໄດ້ຈາກພືດ ແລະ ສັດປ່າຫຼາຍສິບຊະນິດ ຫຼຸດລົງເຫຼືອພຽງພືດຫຼັກສອງສາມຊະນິດ ຊຶ່ງເພີ່ມຄວາມສ່ຽງຄວາມອຶດຢາກເມື່ອການເກັບກ່ຽວຄັ້ງດຽວລົ້ມເຫຼວ ແລະ ເພີ່ມການສຳຜັດກັບພະຍາດໃໝ່ໆ ທີ່ແຜ່ລາມງ່າຍຂຶ້ນ ໃນຊຸມຊົນທີ່ຕັ້ງຖິ່ນຖານແອອັດ ໃກ້ຊິດກັບສັດລ້ຽງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ດັກຄວາມສະດວກສະບາຍ (The luxury trap)$$, 'body', $$ຄຳສັບຂອງ Harari ສຳລັບນະວັດຕະກຳທີ່ສັນຍາຊີວິດທີ່ງ່າຍຂຶ້ນ ແຕ່ກັບສ້າງພາລະ ແລະ ຄວາມຜູກມັດໃໝ່ ຈົນຄົນຕ້ອງເຮັດວຽກໜັກກວ່າເກົ່າ ເພື່ອຮັກສາມາດຕະຖານທີ່ບໍ່ສາມາດຖອຍອອກໄດ້ງ່າຍໆ ເມື່ອຮັບເອົາມາແລ້ວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ປະຊາກອນເພີ່ມຂຶ້ນຢ່າງໄວ ຫຼັງການກະສິກຳຕັ້ງໝັ້ນ ແຕ່ Harari ແຍກການວັດແທກຄວາມສຳເລັດອອກເປັນສອງແບບ: ຈຳນວນຄົນທັງໝົດທີ່ລະບົບອາຫານໜຶ່ງລ້ຽງໄດ້ ບໍ່ແມ່ນສິ່ງດຽວກັນກັບຄົນແຕ່ລະຄົນໃນລະບົບນັ້ນ ອາໄສຢູ່ດີພຽງໃດ. ປາກຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນທີ່ຖືກລ້ຽງພຽງພໍ ແມ່ນຄົນລະເລື່ອງກັບປາກຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນທີ່ຖືກລ້ຽງແບບແຍ່ໆ ແລະ ລາວໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ການກະສິກຳສ່ວນຫຼາຍໃຫ້ຜົນແບບຫຼັງ — ພ້ອມທັງຜູກມະນຸດເຂົ້າກັບໄຮ່ນາຢ່າງຖາວອນ, ພືດຜົນທີ່ເກັບໄວ້ໄດ້ ແລະ ຊັບສິນສ່ວນຕົວ ພ້ອມກັບລະບົບຊົນຊັ້ນທີ່ພືດຜົນສ່ວນເກີນນັ້ນເຮັດໃຫ້ເປັນໄປໄດ້ — ໃນທາງທີ່ເກືອບເປັນໄປບໍ່ໄດ້ທີ່ຈະຫັນກັບຄືນ ເມື່ອປະຊາກອນທັງໝົດເພິ່ງພາມັນແລ້ວ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ການກະສິກຳລ້ຽງຄົນໄດ້ຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນ ແຕ່ຕາມການວັດແທກຫຼາຍຢ່າງ ມັນເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດແຕ່ລະຄົນຍາກຂຶ້ນ — ການແລກປ່ຽນທີ່ບໍ່ມີໃຜເລືອກ ຫຼື ລົງຄະແນນສຽງເລືອກຢ່າງມີສະຕິ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ເງິນ, ອານາຈັກ ແລະ ສາສະໜາ: ສາມພະລັງທີ່ລວມຄົນແປກໜ້າເຂົ້າກັນ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເມື່ອການກະສິກຳສ້າງປະຊາກອນທີ່ໃຫຍ່ ແລະ ໜາແໜ້ນຂຶ້ນ ບັນຫາໃໝ່ກໍ່ເກີດຂຶ້ນ: ຄວາມຮ່ວມມືທີ່ອີງໃສ່ຄວາມໄວ້ວາງໃຈຂອງກຸ່ມນ້ອຍ ບໍ່ພຽງພໍອີກຕໍ່ໄປ ໃນການຈັດຕັ້ງຄົນນັບພັນ ທີ່ບໍ່ຮູ້ຈັກກັນ, ເວົ້າພາສາຕ່າງກັນ ຫຼື ນັບຖືວິນຍານຕ່າງກັນ. Harari ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ການກ້າວກະໂດດຄັ້ງຕໍ່ໄປຂອງປະຫວັດສາດ ຖືກແກ້ໄຂດ້ວຍລະບົບສາມຢ່າງທີ່ຊ້ອນທັບກັນ ແຕ່ລະລະບົບເປັນເລື່ອງແຕ່ງຮ່ວມກັນ ແລະ ແຕ່ລະລະບົບສາມາດລວມຄົນແປກໜ້າອ້ອມຮອບລະບຽບຮ່ວມກັນໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ເງິນ (Money)$$, 'body', $$ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄຳ ຫຼື ເຈ້ຍໃນຕົວມັນເອງ ແຕ່ເປັນລະບົບຄວາມໄວ້ວາງໃຈຮ່ວມກັນທີ່ເປັນສາກົນ — ມີປະສິດທິພາບທີ່ສຸດເທົ່າທີ່ເຄີຍປະດິດຂຶ້ນ. ຊາວກະສິກອນທີ່ບໍ່ເຄີຍພົບທະຫານ ແລະ ບໍ່ມີພາສາ ຫຼື ພະເຈົ້າຮ່ວມກັນ ຍັງສາມາດຄ້າຂາຍນຳກັນໄດ້ ຜ່ານເລື່ອງແຕ່ງຮ່ວມກັນຂອງລາຄາ ເພາະທັງສອງເຊື່ອວ່າ ຄົນແປກໜ້າຢູ່ຖະໜົນຕໍ່ໄປ ຈະຮັບເອົາເງິນຕາດຽວກັນນັ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອານາຈັກ ຄືພະລັງທີສອງ. Harari ໃຊ້ຄຳນີ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຕັດສິນທາງສິນທຳ ໂດຍນິຍາມມັນງ່າຍໆວ່າ ລະບຽບການເມືອງໃດໜຶ່ງ ທີ່ປົກຄອງຫຼາຍຊົນຊາດ ແລະ ດິນແດນທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນ ພາຍໃຕ້ລະບົບດຽວ. ອານາຈັກເປັນໜຶ່ງໃນໂຄງສ້າງການເມືອງທີ່ຢືນຍົງ ແລະ ປະສົບຄວາມສຳເລັດທີ່ສຸດຕາມປະຫວັດສາດ ເພາະມັນບັງຄັບໃຊ້ກົດໝາຍ, ເງິນຕາ, ພື້ນຖານໂຄງລ່າງ ແລະ ຫຼາຍຄັ້ງ ພາສາ ໃຫ້ເປັນອັນດຽວກັນ ໃນປະຊາກອນທີ່ຫຼາກຫຼາຍຢ່າງມະຫາສານ — ລຶບລ້າງລັກສະນະສະເພາະທ້ອງຖິ່ນ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ສ້າງວັດທະນະທຳຊົນຊັ້ນສູງຮ່ວມກັນ ທີ່ຢືນຍົງກວ່າຜູ້ປົກຄອງຄົນໃດຄົນໜຶ່ງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ສາສະໜາ ຄືພະລັງທີສາມ. ສາສະໜາສາກົນ — ສາສະໜາທີ່ອ້າງຄວາມຈິງສຳລັບທຸກຄົນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນສະເພາະເຜົ່າ ຫຼື ສາຍເລືອດດຽວ — ໃຫ້ລະບຽບເໜືອມະນຸດ ທີ່ສາມາດຮັບຮອງຄວາມຮ່ວມມືລະຫວ່າງຄົນແປກໜ້າ ໂດຍອ້າງອິງກົດຈັກກະວານ ຫຼື ພະເຈົ້າຮ່ວມກັນ ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນສາຍເລືອດ ໃຫ້ຄົນທີ່ບໍ່ມີຄວາມສຳພັນສ່ວນຕົວ ມີເຫດຜົນທີ່ຈະໄວ້ວາງໃຈ ແລະ ຮ່ວມມືກັນຢູ່ດີ.$$)
