    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົບ (The frog)$$, 'body', $$ວຽກທີ່ໃຫຍ່ ແລະ ສຳຄັນທີ່ສຸດຂອງທ່ານ — ວຽກທີ່ມີໂອກາດສົ່ງຜົນກະທົບໃຫຍ່ທີ່ສຸດ ແລະ ວຽກທີ່ທ່ານຢາກຫຼີກລ່ຽງທີ່ສຸດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ວິທີ ABCDE$$, 'body', $$ລະບົບຈັດອັນດັບວຽກຕາມຜົນສະທ້ອນ: A (ຜົນສະທ້ອນຮ້າຍແຮງ), B (ຜົນສະທ້ອນເລັກນ້ອຍ), C (ບໍ່ມີຜົນສະທ້ອນ), D (ມອບໝາຍ), E (ຕັດອອກ).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຂົງເຂດຜົນລັບຫຼັກ (Key result areas)$$, 'body', $$ຜົນລັບສະເພາະຈຳນວນໜຶ່ງ ທີ່ຕັດສິນຄວາມສຳເລັດໃນວຽກ ຫຼື ບົດບາດຂອງທ່ານຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົດແຫ່ງສາມ (The law of three)$$, 'body', $$ຂໍ້ສັງເກດວ່າ ວຽກປະມານສາມອັນ ຄິດເປັນຄຸນຄ່າແທ້ຈິງສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ທີ່ຄົນຄົນໜຶ່ງປະກອບສ່ວນໃນວຽກຂອງຕົນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການຜັດວັນຢ່າງສ້າງສັນ (Creative procrastination)$$, 'body', $$ການເລືອກຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ວ່າວຽກຄຸນຄ່າຕ່ຳໃດຄວນເລື່ອນ, ມອບໝາຍ ຫຼື ຕັດອອກ ເພື່ອໃຫ້ເວລາໄປສູ່ສິ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນແທ້ໆ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການເຮັດວຽກທີລະອັນ (Single-handling)$$, 'body', $$ການເລີ່ມວຽກ ແລະ ຕິດຕາມມັນ ໂດຍບໍ່ປ່ຽນໄປວຽກອື່ນ ຈົນກວ່າຈະສຳເລັດຢ່າງສົມບູນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຂໍ້ຈຳກັດຫຼັກ (Key constraint)$$, 'body', $$ປັດໄຈອັນດຽວ ບໍ່ວ່າພາຍໃນ ຫຼື ພາຍນອກ ທີ່ຈຳກັດຄວາມໄວໃນການໄປສູ່ເປົ້າໝາຍສຳຄັນຂອງທ່ານຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Think of the frog you have been avoiding this week. What is it actually costing you to keep postponing it, versus what it would cost to eat it first thing tomorrow?$$, $$Tracy's advice assumes real control over your schedule. Where in your actual work or life is that assumption false, and how would you apply the underlying principle anyway?$$, $$Look at your own procrastination pattern honestly — is it mainly a prioritization problem, or is something more emotional (fear, perfectionism, unclear goals) driving it? What would that mean for which parts of this book actually help you?$$, $$The ABCDE method assumes you can accurately judge a task's consequences in advance. When has your own judgment about what mattered turned out to be wrong, and what did that cost?$$, $$Of the twenty-one tactics, which one or two would actually change your days if you applied them consistently for a month — and what has stopped you from doing that already?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ຄິດເຖິງກົບທີ່ທ່ານກຳລັງຫຼີກລ່ຽງຢູ່ໃນອາທິດນີ້. ການເລື່ອນມັນອອກໄປເລື້ອຍໆ ເສຍຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍແທ້ຈິງເທົ່າໃດ ທຽບກັບການກິນມັນກ່ອນໝູ່ໃນມື້ອື່ນ?$$, $$ຄຳແນະນຳຂອງ Tracy ສົມມຸດວ່າທ່ານຄວບຄຸມຕາຕະລາງເວລາຂອງຕົນເອງໄດ້ຈິງ. ໃນວຽກ ຫຼື ຊີວິດຕົວຈິງຂອງທ່ານ ຂໍ້ສົມມຸດນັ້ນຜິດຢູ່ບ່ອນໃດ ແລະ ທ່ານຈະນຳຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານໄປໃຊ້ໄດ້ແນວໃດຢູ່ດີ?$$, $$ພິຈາລະນາຮູບແບບການຜັດວັນຂອງທ່ານເອງຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ — ມັນເປັນບັນຫາການຈັດລຳດັບຄວາມສຳຄັນເປັນຫຼັກ ຫຼື ມີບາງຢ່າງທາງອາລົມ (ຄວາມຢ້ານ, ຄວາມສົມບູນແບບ, ເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ບໍ່ຊັດເຈນ) ທີ່ຂັບເຄື່ອນມັນຢູ່? ນັ້ນໝາຍຄວາມວ່າແນວໃດ ຕໍ່ວ່າສ່ວນໃດຂອງປຶ້ມນີ້ ຈະຊ່ວຍທ່ານໄດ້ແທ້ໆ?$$, $$ວິທີ ABCDE ສົມມຸດວ່າທ່ານສາມາດຕັດສິນຜົນສະທ້ອນຂອງວຽກລ່ວງໜ້າໄດ້ຢ່າງຖືກຕ້ອງ. ເມື່ອໃດທີ່ການຕັດສິນຂອງທ່ານເອງ ກ່ຽວກັບສິ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນ ກັບກາຍເປັນຜິດ ແລະ ມັນເສຍຫາຍຫຍັງແດ່?$$, $$ໃນ 21 ກົນລະຍຸດ ອັນໃດໜຶ່ງ ຫຼື ສອງອັນ ທີ່ຈະປ່ຽນແປງແຕ່ລະມື້ຂອງທ່ານໄດ້ແທ້ໆ ຫາກນຳໄປໃຊ້ຢ່າງສະໝ່ຳສະເໝີເປັນເວລາໜຶ່ງເດືອນ — ແລະ ຫຍັງທີ່ເຄີຍຢຸດທ່ານໄວ້ຈາກການເຮັດແນວນັ້ນ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Do your biggest, most important task first thing each morning, before email or small tasks get a chance to crowd it out.$$, $$About 20% of your tasks generate about 80% of your results — most of a long task list is close to irrelevant.$$, $$Rank tasks A through E by consequence, not urgency, and never touch a B task while an A task is undone.$$, $$Judge priority by the real long-term consequences of doing or not doing a task, not by how urgent it feels.$$, $$Get clear on your key result areas — the handful of outcomes that most determine your success in your role.$$, $$Apply the law of three: identify the roughly three tasks that account for most of your real contribution.$$, $$Plan every day, week, and month in advance — planning time pays for itself many times over in execution time.$$, $$Practice creative procrastination: deliberately choose what to delay or drop so you can focus on what matters.$$, $$Single-handle each task: start it, stay with it, and finish it completely before switching to anything else.$$, $$Identify your key constraint — the one bottleneck limiting your progress — and work on that before anything else.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ເຮັດວຽກທີ່ໃຫຍ່ ແລະ ສຳຄັນທີ່ສຸດຂອງທ່ານກ່ອນໝູ່ ໃນທຸກເຊົ້າ ກ່ອນອີເມວ ຫຼື ວຽກນ້ອຍໆຈະມີໂອກາດແກ່ງແຍ່ງເອົາໄປ.$$, $$ປະມານ 20% ຂອງວຽກ ສ້າງຜົນລັບໄດ້ປະມານ 80% — ລາຍການວຽກອັນຍາວສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ແທບບໍ່ມີຄວາມສຳຄັນ.$$, $$ຈັດອັນດັບວຽກ A ຫາ E ຕາມຜົນສະທ້ອນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮີບດ່ວນ ແລະ ຫ້າມແຕະຕ້ອງວຽກ B ໃນຂະນະທີ່ວຽກ A ຍັງບໍ່ສຳເລັດ.$$, $$ຕັດສິນຄວາມສຳຄັນ ຈາກຜົນສະທ້ອນໄລຍະຍາວທີ່ແທ້ຈິງຂອງການເຮັດ ຫຼື ບໍ່ເຮັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຈາກຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກຮີບດ່ວນ.$$, $$ໃຫ້ຊັດເຈນກ່ຽວກັບຂົງເຂດຜົນລັບຫຼັກຂອງທ່ານ — ຜົນລັບຈຳນວນໜຶ່ງ ທີ່ຕັດສິນຄວາມສຳເລັດໃນບົດບາດຂອງທ່ານຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດ.$$, $$ນຳໃຊ້ກົດແຫ່ງສາມ: ຄົ້ນຫາວຽກປະມານສາມອັນ ທີ່ຄິດເປັນຄຸນຄ່າແທ້ຈິງສ່ວນຫຼາຍທີ່ທ່ານປະກອບສ່ວນ.$$, $$ວາງແຜນລ່ວງໜ້າ ທຸກມື້, ທຸກອາທິດ, ທຸກເດືອນ — ເວລາທີ່ໃຊ້ວາງແຜນ ຄືນທຶນຫຼາຍເທົ່າໃນເວລາປະຕິບັດ.$$, $$ຝຶກຝົນການຜັດວັນຢ່າງສ້າງສັນ: ຕັ້ງໃຈເລືອກສິ່ງທີ່ຈະເລື່ອນ ຫຼື ຕັດອອກ ເພື່ອໃຫ້ຈົດຈໍ່ໃສ່ສິ່ງທີ່ສຳຄັນ.$$, $$ເຮັດວຽກທີລະອັນ: ເລີ່ມ, ຕິດຕາມ ແລະ ເຮັດໃຫ້ສຳເລັດຢ່າງສົມບູນ ກ່ອນປ່ຽນໄປວຽກອື່ນ.$$, $$ຄົ້ນຫາຂໍ້ຈຳກັດຫຼັກຂອງທ່ານ — ຄໍຂວດອັນດຽວທີ່ຈຳກັດຄວາມຄືບໜ້າ — ແລ້ວແກ້ໄຂມັນກ່ອນສິ່ງອື່ນ.$$]
where book_id = '3169593c-8fae-475c-ba39-88f8b8c44e60';

-- The Millionaire Fastlane — MJ DeMarco
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A challenge to the slow, job-and-retirement wealth script, in favor of building scalable systems.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ທ້າທາຍແນວຄິດຄວາມຮັ່ງມີແບບຊ້າໆຜ່ານວຽກປະຈຳ ດ້ວຍການສ້າງລະບົບທີ່ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້.$$,
  deck_en = $$M.J. DeMarco writes as a former limo-company founder turned combative critic of conventional financial advice, and his central claim is blunt: the "get a degree, get a job, save modestly for forty years" script is not a safe path to wealth — it is a mathematically slow one that trades your best, healthiest decades for a payoff deferred to old age. In its place he proposes the Fastlane, a roadmap built around owning a scalable business system rather than renting out your hours. This lesson works through his three roadmaps, his reframed wealth equation, the CENTS checklist he uses to evaluate any business idea, and where his combative certainty deserves a skeptical read.$$,
  deck_lo = $$M.J. DeMarco ຂຽນປຶ້ມນີ້ໃນຖານະອະດີດຜູ້ກໍ່ຕັ້ງບໍລິສັດລົດລິມູຊິນ ທີ່ກາຍມາເປັນນັກວິຈານຄຳແນະນຳການເງິນແບບດັ້ງເດີມຢ່າງແຮງ ແລະ ຂໍ້ອ້າງຫຼັກຂອງລາວກົງໄປກົງມາ: ສູດ "ຮຽນຈົບ, ຫາວຽກ, ເກັບເງິນທີລະໜ້ອຍເປັນເວລາ 40 ປີ" ບໍ່ແມ່ນເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ປອດໄພໄປສູ່ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ — ແຕ່ເປັນເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ຊ້າທາງຄະນິດສາດ ຊຶ່ງແລກໄລຍະທີ່ດີ ແລະ ແຂງແຮງທີ່ສຸດຂອງຊີວິດ ໄປກັບຜົນຕອບແທນທີ່ຖືກເລື່ອນໄປໄວ້ຕອນສູງອາຍຸ. ແທນທີ່ນັ້ນ ລາວສະເໜີ "Fastlane" ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບການເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງລະບົບທຸລະກິດທີ່ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້ ແທນທີ່ຈະໃຫ້ເຊົ່າຊົ່ວໂມງເວລາຂອງທ່ານ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍສາມເສັ້ນທາງຂອງລາວ, ສົມຜົນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີແບບໃໝ່, ບັນຊີກວດສອບ CENTS ທີ່ລາວໃຊ້ປະເມີນແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດ ແລະ ຈຸດໃດຂອງຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈແບບແຂງກ້າວຂອງລາວ ທີ່ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍຄວາມສົງໄສ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Three roadmaps, chosen mostly by accident$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco frames personal finance not as a series of isolated decisions but as a choice of roadmap — a fundamental financial trajectory that most people fall into without ever consciously selecting it, and that trajectory determines what your effort can actually produce far more than how hard you work within it. Working harder on the wrong roadmap, in his framing, still ends at the wrong destination.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The three roadmaps$$, 'body', $$The Sidewalk — spend now, plan never, financed by debt and consumerism, drifting toward poverty regardless of income. The Slowlane — defer gratification for decades: get educated, get a job, live modestly, save and invest a portion of every paycheck, and retire wealthy in your sixties. The Fastlane — build or acquire a business system that produces income and equity independent of your personal hours, compressing wealth accumulation into years rather than decades.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Sidewalk gets the least attention in the book because DeMarco assumes most readers have already rejected it; his real target, and the more interesting argument, is the Slowlane — precisely because it looks responsible, is widely endorsed by financial advisors, and is the roadmap most educated, disciplined people are quietly already on.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The roadmap you are on shapes your ceiling more than the effort you put in — DeMarco's argument targets people already working hard on the wrong one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The Slowlane's core flaw: trading your best years for a deferred payoff$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Slowlane plan is familiar: earn a credential, take a job, live below your means, and route a disciplined slice of every paycheck into retirement accounts and diversified investments for roughly forty years, trusting compound growth to eventually deliver a comfortable retirement. DeMarco does not dispute that this plan can work. His objection is to what it costs and what it depends on.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It spends your best decades to fund your worst ones.$$, 'body', $$The plan asks you to trade your twenties, thirties, forties, and fifties — the years you have the most energy, health, and time to actually enjoy wealth — for a payoff scheduled to arrive in your sixties or later, when your capacity to use it is often diminished.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It depends on someone else's system, not your own.$$, 'body', $$Your income depends on an employer continuing to want you; your eventual nest egg depends on market returns over which you have zero influence. The plan's success is largely outsourced to variables you do not control.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The math only works over a very long, uninterrupted horizon.$$, 'body', $$Compound growth genuinely does the heavy lifting eventually — but "eventually" for most realistic saving rates means decades, and the plan has little slack for a layoff, a health crisis, or a market downturn that lands at the wrong moment.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco's sharpest line of attack is less about the arithmetic and more about the trade itself: he argues the Slowlane implicitly asks you to accept a mediocre, deferred life in exchange for a promise of a good one later, and that the promise is conditional on decades of things going approximately right.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The Slowlane can work, but it spends your healthiest years to fund a retirement decades away, on a plan you don't control.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Wealth redefined: net worth, not salary$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A large part of DeMarco's argument rests on separating two things people routinely conflate: income and wealth. Income is what you get paid for your time — a wage, a salary, a fee — and it is capped by the number of hours you personally have and the rate anyone will pay for them. Wealth is different: it is net worth, the value of assets you actually own.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The wealth equation$$, 'body', $$Wealth is built through owned assets that produce cash flow and hold sale value on their own — a business, equity, intellectual property — not through the size of a paycheck. A high income that stops the moment you stop showing up is not wealth; it is well-paid labor.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is why DeMarco is skeptical of using income alone as a proxy for financial success. A surgeon or a partner at a law firm can earn an enormous income and still be, by his definition, on the Slowlane: the moment they stop trading hours, the income stops with them, because there is no asset underneath it that keeps producing value on its own. A Fastlane business, by contrast, is itself an asset — something that could in principle be sold, staffed, or scaled independent of its founder's ongoing labor.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A big paycheck that disappears the moment you stop working is income, not wealth — wealth is an asset that keeps producing value without you.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The Fastlane: business as a vehicle, not a lottery ticket$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco is careful to distinguish the Fastlane from get-rich-quick fantasies, even though critics often collapse the two. The Fastlane is not day-trading, not a lucky viral idea, not real-estate flipping as depicted on television, and not a lottery ticket. It is the deliberate construction of a business system — what he repeatedly calls a "vehicle" — that carries you toward wealth through ownership and scale, powered by unglamorous, often years-long effort, especially at the start.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The Fastlane$$, 'body', $$A roadmap built around owning or building a business that functions as a system: something that can generate income and equity value through processes, other people's effort, and reach beyond what your own two hands could produce alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The distinction he draws most sharply is between event thinking and process thinking. Sidewalkers and many aspiring entrepreneurs alike wait for a single lucky event — an inheritance, a jackpot, one viral idea — to change their trajectory. DeMarco insists wealth is instead a process: years of building, iterating, and reinvesting, where any individual day looks unremarkable and the outcome only becomes visible in hindsight.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The Fastlane is not a lucky break — it is the deliberate, often unglamorous construction of a business that functions as a system.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$CENTS: a due-diligence checklist, starting with Control$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Wanting to build a Fastlane business is not the same as having a good one, and DeMarco offers CENTS as a practical screening tool: five criteria a venture should satisfy before you commit years of effort to it. The letters stand for Control, Entry, Need, Time, and Scale.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$CENTS$$, 'body', $$A five-part checklist for evaluating any business idea: Control (do you actually run it), Entry (is it hard enough to copy), Need (does it serve a real, scalable demand), Time (does it free your time or consume it forever), and Scale (can it reach many customers without proportional personal effort).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Control$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The first question is simple but ruthless: do you actually control the business, or is your income dependent on someone else's decisions? DeMarco is skeptical of most franchises and multi-level-marketing structures for exactly this reason — the franchisor sets the rules, the upline captures the leverage, and the person doing the work is still, functionally, an employee of someone else's system, just without the paycheck stability a real job provides. A platform business built entirely on someone else's rules — a single retailer's marketplace, a single social platform's algorithm — carries the same risk: the terms can change without your consent, and your business changes with them.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Before anything else, ask who actually controls the business — if the answer is someone else, you have not left the Slowlane, you have just changed employers.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$CENTS continued: Entry and Need$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Entry$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A business with no real barrier to entry is a business anyone can copy over a weekend, and once they do, competition drives margins toward zero. DeMarco argues counterintuitively that ease of entry is a warning sign, not a comfort — an idea that requires no capital, no skill, and no meaningful setup rarely stays profitable for long, because nothing stops the next person from doing exactly the same thing. He pushes readers toward businesses that require real skill, a proprietary process, capital, or a system that is genuinely difficult to replicate quickly.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Need$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A business also has to serve a real market need — a problem enough people will actually pay to have solved — rather than simply being something the founder personally enjoys. DeMarco is pointed about this: a business built around a narrow personal hobby, serving a small enthusiast niche, can be satisfying, but it is rarely a path to significant wealth, because the underlying market is too small to scale into. The test is not "do I love this" but "will enough people pay for this at a scale that matters."$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A copyable idea gets competed away fast, and a niche too small to matter to anyone but you stays small no matter how hard you work it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$CENTS continued: Time and Scale$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Time$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Many businesses that look independent are, on closer inspection, just self-employment: a freelancer, a consultant, or a solo service provider who has simply become their own boss while still trading hours directly for dollars, with income capped by a personal calendar. DeMarco's test asks whether a business can eventually run, and earn, without your constant hourly presence — through systems, staff, or automation — or whether it will consume your time indefinitely by design.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Scale$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The final letter asks how many customers a business can reach without a proportional increase in your personal effort. A one-to-one service model — one client, one hour, one fee — is inherently capped by the number of hours in your week. A business built for scale can serve one customer or one million through largely the same underlying system, which is where DeMarco argues the real wealth-generating leverage actually lives.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A business that needs you every hour, or that hits a hard ceiling on customers, will pay you a living — not build you wealth.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Income follows the number of lives you affect$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Underneath CENTS sits a broader principle DeMarco returns to repeatedly: income is not a function of hours worked, it is a function of how many people you serve and how much value you actually deliver them. He sometimes calls this the law of effection — your financial reward scales with your reach and your usefulness to others, not with your effort measured in time.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Law of effection$$, 'body', $$The idea that income scales with the number of people a business serves multiplied by the value it delivers to each of them — not with the number of hours the founder personally works.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is also where DeMarco draws his sharpest line against what he sees as false Fastlane shortcuts: schemes marketed as passive income that, on closer inspection, fail the CENTS test entirely — low-entry side hustles anyone can replicate, narrow-audience content businesses, or recruitment-driven structures where most of the "income" actually comes from recruiting more participants rather than serving outside customers. His point is not that these are morally wrong so much as that they are usually poor businesses by his own criteria, dressed up in Fastlane language.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Wealth scales with how many people your business genuinely serves, not with how many hours you personally put in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Process, not event: wealth as sustained execution$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco is explicit that the Fastlane is not a shortcut in the sense most people want it to be. It typically demands an intense, often unglamorous period of building — long hours, reinvested profit instead of spent profit, and years where the business consumes far more than it returns — before it produces the kind of freedom the roadmap promises. He is dismissive of anyone looking for wealth without that upfront sacrifice, arguing that the search for effortless income is itself a Sidewalk habit wearing a business costume.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Process, not event$$, 'body', $$DeMarco's framing that wealth results from years of sustained execution, iteration, and reinvestment, rather than from a single lucky break, launch, or idea. The visible payoff, when it comes, is the output of a long invisible process rather than a discrete moment.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This reframing is deliberately uncomfortable: it removes the excuse of waiting for the right idea or the right break, and replaces it with a demand for sustained, often tedious action over years — closer in spirit to running a marathon of iteration than winning a single race.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The payoff DeMarco describes is the visible tip of years of invisible, unglamorous execution — not a lucky break dressed up as a business plan.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Producer over consumer: the mindset shift$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco frames the Fastlane less as a set of tactics and more as a wholesale identity shift: from consumer to producer. Sidewalkers and Slowlaners alike, in his framing, primarily consume — they spend what they earn, and their relationship to the economy is mostly as a buyer and an employee. Fastlaners orient themselves as producers — building the thing others buy, employ, or use, rather than only being the buyer or the employee themselves.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This reframing runs through his advice on money habits too: reinvesting profit into the business rather than upgrading a lifestyle, treating spare time as raw material for building rather than for consumption, and measuring progress by the growth of the underlying asset rather than by short-term lifestyle upgrades. The discipline he asks for is real, and it is one reason the book reads as much like a personal-conduct manifesto as a business-strategy guide.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The Fastlane asks you to become a producer of value for others, not just a more disciplined consumer of your own paycheck.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Millionaire Fastlane is written as a manifesto, not a balanced analysis, and its confident, combative tone is deliberate — it is part of what makes the book compelling and also exactly where a careful reader should slow down.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The tone can be dismissive of people who reasonably choose stability.$$, 'body', $$DeMarco writes about the Slowlane with real contempt at times, but many people choose steady employment deliberately and rationally — for health insurance, for predictable income to support a family, for lower risk tolerance, or because their circumstances leave little room to absorb a failed venture. The book has comparatively little sympathy for those trade-offs.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$"The Slowlane is a trap" oversimplifies a real, legitimate trade-off.$$, 'body', $$Framing steady work and disciplined investing as a mistake is a strong rhetorical move, but for most people it remains a genuinely sound, lower-risk path to a comfortable life. The book is most useful as a challenge to complacency, less useful as a literal claim that the conventional path is a failure.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Survivorship bias runs through the whole framework.$$, 'body', $$The book is written by, and largely illustrated with, people who succeeded at building a scalable business. It says comparatively little about the much larger number of attempts that fail outright, run out of capital, or never clear the CENTS bar despite years of honest effort — outcomes that are common and are not simply a matter of insufficient hustle.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$CENTS assumes a level of access the book does not fully acknowledge.$$, 'body', $$Evaluating and building a business against Control, Entry, Need, Time, and Scale assumes access to capital, credit, market reach, legal infrastructure, and a risk tolerance that not every reader has. Someone supporting dependents on a thin margin does not have the same room to gamble years of income on an unproven venture as the entrepreneurs profiled in the book.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The framework translates unevenly outside the US context DeMarco writes from.$$, 'body', $$Market size, access to capital, regulatory environments, payment infrastructure, and consumer purchasing power all differ substantially in a market like Laos compared to the US settings the book's examples are drawn from. The underlying logic of CENTS is still a useful lens, but the scale of opportunity and the practical path to satisfying it look different here.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read with those limits in mind, the book's real value survives its overstatement: a genuinely useful distinction between income and wealth, and a concrete checklist — CENTS — for stress-testing a business idea before spending years building it. Treat the certainty as motivational packaging around a real, useful set of questions, not as proof that the conventional path is a mistake for everyone who takes it.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ສາມເສັ້ນທາງ ທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ເລືອກໂດຍບໍ່ຮູ້ຕົວ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco ເບິ່ງເລື່ອງການເງິນສ່ວນຕົວ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຊຸດການຕັດສິນໃຈແຍກກັນ ແຕ່ເປັນການເລືອກ "ເສັ້ນທາງ" (roadmap) — ທິດທາງການເງິນພື້ນຖານທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຕົກເຂົ້າໄປໂດຍບໍ່ເຄີຍເລືອກຢ່າງມີສະຕິ ແລະ ເສັ້ນທາງນັ້ນຕ່າງຫາກທີ່ກຳນົດວ່າຄວາມພະຍາຍາມຂອງທ່ານຈະສ້າງຫຍັງໄດ້ແທ້ໆ ຫຼາຍກວ່າຄວາມພະຍາຍາມທີ່ໃສ່ລົງໄປໃນເສັ້ນທາງນັ້ນ. ພະຍາຍາມໜັກຂຶ້ນເທິງເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ຜິດ ໃນມຸມມອງຂອງລາວ ກໍ່ຍັງໄປສິ້ນສຸດຢູ່ຈຸດໝາຍທີ່ຜິດຢູ່ດີ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ສາມເສັ້ນທາງ$$, 'body', $$Sidewalk — ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍໃນປັດຈຸບັນ ບໍ່ມີແຜນການ ອາໄສໜີ້ສິນ ແລະ ການບໍລິໂພກ, ລອຍໄປສູ່ຄວາມທຸກຍາກບໍ່ວ່າຈະມີລາຍໄດ້ເທົ່າໃດ. Slowlane — ເລື່ອນຄວາມສຸກອອກໄປເປັນຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ: ຮຽນ, ຫາວຽກ, ໃຊ້ຊີວິດພໍປະມານ, ເກັບ ແລະ ລົງທຶນທຸກເດືອນ, ແລ້ວກາຍເປັນຄົນຮັ່ງມີຕອນອາຍຸຫົກສິບ. Fastlane — ສ້າງ ຫຼື ຊື້ລະບົບທຸລະກິດທີ່ສ້າງລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າຊັບສິນ ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບຊົ່ວໂມງແຮງງານສ່ວນຕົວ, ບີບອັດການສະສົມຄວາມຮັ່ງມີໃຫ້ເຫຼືອພຽງຫຼາຍປີ ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Sidewalk ໄດ້ຮັບຄວາມສົນໃຈໜ້ອຍທີ່ສຸດໃນປຶ້ມ ເພາະ DeMarco ຄາດວ່າຜູ້ອ່ານສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ປະຕິເສດມັນໄປແລ້ວ; ເປົ້າໝາຍແທ້ຂອງລາວ ແລະ ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງທີ່ໜ້າສົນໃຈກວ່າ ຄື Slowlane — ພຽງເພາະມັນເບິ່ງຄືມີຄວາມຮັບຜິດຊອບ, ຖືກແນະນຳຢ່າງກວ້າງຂວາງໂດຍທີ່ປຶກສາການເງິນ ແລະ ເປັນເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ຄົນມີການສຶກສາ ແລະ ມີວິໄນສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ກຳລັງຢູ່ໃນນັ້ນຢ່າງງຽບໆຢູ່ແລ້ວ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ທ່ານຢູ່ ກຳນົດເພດານຂອງທ່ານ ຫຼາຍກວ່າຄວາມພະຍາຍາມທີ່ໃສ່ລົງໄປ — ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ DeMarco ພຸ້ງໃສ່ຄົນທີ່ພະຍາຍາມໜັກຢູ່ແລ້ວ ແຕ່ຢູ່ຜິດເສັ້ນທາງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຈຸດອ່ອນຫຼັກຂອງ Slowlane: ແລກໄລຍະທີ່ດີທີ່ສຸດຂອງຊີວິດ ໄປກັບຜົນຕອບແທນທີ່ຖືກເລື່ອນອອກໄປ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ແຜນ Slowlane ຄຸ້ນເຄີຍດີ: ຮຽນຈົບເອົາໃບປະກາດ, ຫາວຽກ, ໃຊ້ຊີວິດຕ່ຳກວ່າລາຍໄດ້, ແລ້ວແບ່ງເງິນສ່ວນໜຶ່ງຈາກທຸກເດືອນໄປລົງທຶນໃນບັນຊີກຽມກະສຽນ ແລະ ການລົງທຶນຫຼາກຫຼາຍເປັນເວລາປະມານ 40 ປີ ໂດຍໄວ້ໃຈວ່າດອກເບ້ຍທົບຕົ້ນຈະໃຫ້ຜົນຕອບແທນທີ່ດີໃນທີ່ສຸດ. DeMarco ບໍ່ໄດ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າແຜນນີ້ໃຊ້ບໍ່ໄດ້. ຂໍ້ຄັດຄ້ານຂອງລາວ ຢູ່ທີ່ຄ່າໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ແລະ ສິ່ງທີ່ມັນຂຶ້ນກັບ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ມັນໃຊ້ໄລຍະທີ່ດີທີ່ສຸດ ໄປສະໜອງໄລຍະທີ່ແຍ່ທີ່ສຸດ.$$, 'body', $$ແຜນນີ້ຂໍໃຫ້ທ່ານແລກຊ່ວງອາຍຸ 20, 30, 40 ແລະ 50 ປີ — ຊ່ວງທີ່ທ່ານມີພະລັງ, ສຸຂະພາບ ແລະ ເວລາທີ່ຈະໃຊ້ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີໄດ້ຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດ — ໄປກັບຜົນຕອບແທນທີ່ກຳນົດໃຫ້ມາຮອດຕອນອາຍຸຫົກສິບ ຫຼືຫຼັງນັ້ນ, ຊ່ວງທີ່ຄວາມສາມາດໃນການໃຊ້ມັນມັກຫຼຸດລົງແລ້ວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ມັນຂຶ້ນກັບລະບົບຂອງຄົນອື່ນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂອງທ່ານເອງ.$$, 'body', $$ລາຍໄດ້ຂອງທ່ານຂຶ້ນກັບວ່ານາຍຈ້າງຈະຢາກຈ້າງທ່ານຕໍ່ໄປບໍ; ເງິນກຽມກະສຽນສຸດທ້າຍຂອງທ່ານຂຶ້ນກັບຜົນຕອບແທນຕະຫຼາດ ຊຶ່ງທ່ານບໍ່ມີອິດທິພົນຫຍັງເລີຍ. ຄວາມສຳເລັດຂອງແຜນນີ້ ຖືກມອບໝາຍໃຫ້ຕົວແປທີ່ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ຄວບຄຸມເປັນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄະນິດສາດຈະໃຊ້ໄດ້ ກໍ່ຕໍ່ເມື່ອໄລຍະເວລາຍາວ ແລະ ບໍ່ຂາດຕອນ.$$, 'body', $$ດອກເບ້ຍທົບຕົ້ນເຮັດວຽກໜັກໃນທີ່ສຸດແທ້ໆ — ແຕ່ "ໃນທີ່ສຸດ" ສຳລັບອັດຕາການເກັບເງິນທົ່ວໄປ ໝາຍເຖິງຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ ແລະ ແຜນນີ້ບໍ່ມີຊ່ອງຫວ່າງຫຼາຍ ຖ້າຖືກໄລ່ອອກ, ເກີດວິກິດສຸຂະພາບ ຫຼື ຕະຫຼາດຕົກຢູ່ຈັງຫວະບໍ່ດີ.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຈຸດໂຈມຕີທີ່ແຫຼມຄົມທີ່ສຸດຂອງ DeMarco ບໍ່ແມ່ນເລກຄະນິດສາດ ແຕ່ຢູ່ທີ່ຕົວການແລກປ່ຽນເອງ: ລາວໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ Slowlane ໂດຍທາງອ້ອມຂໍໃຫ້ທ່ານຍອມຮັບຊີວິດທີ່ທຳມະດາ ແລະ ຖືກເລື່ອນອອກໄປ ເພື່ອແລກກັບຄຳສັນຍາຂອງຊີວິດທີ່ດີໃນອະນາຄົດ ແລະ ຄຳສັນຍານັ້ນມີເງື່ອນໄຂ ຄືສິ່ງຕ່າງໆຕ້ອງດຳເນີນໄປໃນທາງທີ່ຖືກຕ້ອງເປັນເວລາຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Slowlane ອາດໃຊ້ໄດ້ ແຕ່ມັນໃຊ້ໄລຍະທີ່ແຂງແຮງທີ່ສຸດຂອງທ່ານ ໄປສະໜອງເງິນກຽມກະສຽນທີ່ຢູ່ໄກອອກໄປຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ ໃນແຜນທີ່ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ຄວບຄຸມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ນິຍາມຄວາມຮັ່ງມີໃໝ່: ມູນຄ່າສຸດທິ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເງິນເດືອນ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຂອງຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ DeMarco ອີງໃສ່ການແຍກສອງສິ່ງທີ່ຄົນມັກປົນເປກັນ: ລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ. ລາຍໄດ້ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານໄດ້ຮັບແທນເວລາ — ຄ່າຈ້າງ, ເງິນເດືອນ, ຄ່າທຳນຽມ — ແລະ ມັນຖືກຈຳກັດດ້ວຍຈຳນວນຊົ່ວໂມງທີ່ທ່ານມີ ແລະ ອັດຕາທີ່ໃຜຈະຈ່າຍໃຫ້. ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີແມ່ນຄົນລະເລື່ອງ: ມັນຄືມູນຄ່າສຸດທິ, ມູນຄ່າຂອງຊັບສິນທີ່ທ່ານເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງແທ້ໆ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ສົມຜົນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຖືກສ້າງຂຶ້ນຈາກຊັບສິນທີ່ເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ຊຶ່ງສ້າງກະແສເງິນສົດ ແລະ ມີມູນຄ່າຂາຍໄດ້ດ້ວຍຕົນເອງ — ທຸລະກິດ, ຫຸ້ນ, ຊັບສິນທາງປັນຍາ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂະໜາດຂອງເງິນເດືອນ. ລາຍໄດ້ສູງທີ່ຢຸດທັນທີເມື່ອທ່ານຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ — ມັນຄືແຮງງານທີ່ໄດ້ຄ່າຈ້າງດີເທົ່ານັ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ຄືເຫດຜົນທີ່ DeMarco ສົງໄສການໃຊ້ລາຍໄດ້ຢ່າງດຽວເປັນຕົວວັດແທນຄວາມສຳເລັດທາງການເງິນ. ໝໍຜ່າຕັດ ຫຼື ຫຸ້ນສ່ວນບໍລິສັດກົດໝາຍ ອາດມີລາຍໄດ້ມະຫາສານ ແຕ່ຕາມນິຍາມຂອງລາວ ກໍ່ຍັງຢູ່ Slowlane: ທັນທີທີ່ພວກເຂົາຢຸດແລກເວລາ ລາຍໄດ້ກໍ່ຢຸດຕາມ ເພາະບໍ່ມີຊັບສິນຢູ່ຂ້າງລຸ່ມທີ່ຍັງສ້າງມູນຄ່າດ້ວຍຕົນເອງ. ທຸລະກິດແບບ Fastlane ກົງກັນຂ້າມ — ມັນເປັນຊັບສິນເອງ, ບາງສິ່ງທີ່ໃນທາງທິດສະດີສາມາດຂາຍ, ຈ້າງພະນັກງານມາເຮັດ ຫຼື ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້ ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບແຮງງານຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງຂອງຜູ້ກໍ່ຕັ້ງ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ເງິນເດືອນສູງທີ່ຫາຍໄປທັນທີທີ່ທ່ານຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ ຄືລາຍໄດ້ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ — ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຄືຊັບສິນທີ່ຍັງສ້າງມູນຄ່າໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ຕ້ອງມີທ່ານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Fastlane: ພາຫະນະ ບໍ່ແມ່ນປີ້ຫວຍ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$DeMarco ລະມັດລະວັງແຍກ Fastlane ອອກຈາກຄວາມຝັນຮັ່ງມີໄວ ເຖິງແມ່ນຜູ້ວິຈານມັກເອົາສອງອັນນີ້ມາປົນກັນ. Fastlane ບໍ່ແມ່ນການເທຣດຫຸ້ນລາຍວັນ, ບໍ່ແມ່ນແນວຄິດທີ່ດັງຢ່າງໂຊກດີ, ບໍ່ແມ່ນການຊື້ຂາຍອະສັງຫາລິມະຊັບແບບໃນລາຍການໂທລະທັດ ແລະ ບໍ່ແມ່ນປີ້ຫວຍ. ມັນຄືການສ້າງລະບົບທຸລະກິດຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ — ສິ່ງທີ່ລາວເອີ້ນຊ້ຳໆວ່າ "ພາຫະນະ" (vehicle) — ທີ່ພາທ່ານໄປສູ່ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຜ່ານການເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ແລະ ການຂະຫຍາຍຕົວ, ຂັບເຄື່ອນດ້ວຍຄວາມພະຍາຍາມທີ່ບໍ່ໜ້າຕື່ນເຕັ້ນ ແລະ ມັກໃຊ້ເວລາຫຼາຍປີ ໂດຍສະເພາະໃນຊ່ວງເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Fastlane$$, 'body', $$ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບການເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ຫຼື ການສ້າງທຸລະກິດທີ່ເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນລະບົບ: ບາງສິ່ງທີ່ສາມາດສ້າງລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າຊັບສິນ ຜ່ານຂະບວນການ, ແຮງງານຂອງຄົນອື່ນ ແລະ ການເຂົ້າເຖິງທີ່ກວ້າງກວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ມືສອງຂ້າງຂອງທ່ານເອງຈະສ້າງໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງທີ່ລາວແຍກຢ່າງຊັດເຈນທີ່ສຸດ ຄືລະຫວ່າງ "ແນວຄິດແບບເຫດການ" ແລະ "ແນວຄິດແບບຂະບວນການ". ທັງຄົນຢູ່ Sidewalk ແລະ ຜູ້ຢາກເປັນຜູ້ປະກອບການຫຼາຍຄົນ ລໍຖ້າເຫດການໂຊກດີຄັ້ງດຽວ — ມູນມໍລະດົກ, ຫວຍລາງວັນໃຫຍ່, ແນວຄິດທີ່ດັງຄັ້ງດຽວ — ເພື່ອປ່ຽນເສັ້ນທາງຊີວິດ. DeMarco ຢືນຢັນວ່າຄວາມຮັ່ງມີກົງກັນຂ້າມ ຄືຂະບວນການ: ຫຼາຍປີຂອງການສ້າງ, ປັບປຸງ ແລະ ລົງທຶນຄືນ ຊຶ່ງແຕ່ລະມື້ເບິ່ງທຳມະດາ ແລະ ຜົນລັບຈະເຫັນຊັດກໍ່ຕໍ່ເມື່ອຫຼັງຈາກນັ້ນ.$$)
