    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Millionaire Fastlane ຖືກຂຽນຄືປະກາດແນວທາງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນການວິເຄາະທີ່ສົມດຸນ ແລະ ນ້ຳສຽງທີ່ໝັ້ນໃຈ ແລະ ແຂງກ້າວຂອງມັນ ຕັ້ງໃຈ — ນັ້ນຄືສ່ວນໜຶ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ປຶ້ມນີ້ໜ້າສົນໃຈ ແລະ ກໍ່ເປັນຈຸດພໍດີທີ່ຜູ້ອ່ານທີ່ລະມັດລະວັງຄວນຊ້າລົງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ນ້ຳສຽງອາດປະໝາດຄົນທີ່ເລືອກຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງຢ່າງມີເຫດຜົນ.$$, 'body', $$DeMarco ຂຽນກ່ຽວກັບ Slowlane ດ້ວຍຄວາມດູຖູກແທ້ໆບາງຄັ້ງ ແຕ່ຫຼາຍຄົນເລືອກວຽກປະຈຳຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ແລະ ມີເຫດຜົນ — ເພື່ອປະກັນສຸຂະພາບ, ເພື່ອລາຍໄດ້ທີ່ຄາດເດົາໄດ້ໃນການລ້ຽງດູຄອບຄົວ, ຍ້ອນຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງຕ່ຳກວ່າ ຫຼື ຍ້ອນສະຖານະການຊີວິດບໍ່ມີພື້ນທີ່ຮັບຄວາມສ່ຽງຈາກທຸລະກິດທີ່ອາດລົ້ມເຫຼວ. ປຶ້ມນີ້ມີຄວາມເຂົ້າໃຈໜ້ອຍຕໍ່ການແລກປ່ຽນເຫຼົ່ານີ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$"Slowlane ຄືກັບດັກ" ເປັນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີ່ແຮງແຕ່ງ່າຍເກີນໄປ.$$, 'body', $$ການວາງກອບວ່າວຽກທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ ແລະ ການລົງທຶນຢ່າງມີວິໄນ ຄືຄວາມຜິດພາດ ເປັນທ່າທີໂຕ້ວາທີທີ່ແຮງ ແຕ່ສຳລັບຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ ມັນຍັງເປັນເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງຕ່ຳໄປສູ່ຊີວິດທີ່ດີ. ປຶ້ມນີ້ເປັນປະໂຫຍດທີ່ສຸດ ໃນຖານະການທ້າທາຍຄວາມພໍໃຈ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຕົງໆວ່າເສັ້ນທາງທົ່ວໄປຄືຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ອະຄະຕິຈາກຄົນທີ່ລອດຢູ່ ແລ່ນຜ່ານທັງກອບຄວາມຄິດ.$$, 'body', $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ຂຽນໂດຍ ແລະ ຍົກຕົວຢ່າງໂດຍຄົນທີ່ປະສົບຄວາມສຳເລັດໃນການສ້າງທຸລະກິດທີ່ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້ເປັນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່. ມັນເວົ້າໜ້ອຍກ່ຽວກັບຄວາມພະຍາຍາມຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍທີ່ລົ້ມເຫຼວທັງໝົດ, ໝົດທຶນ ຫຼື ບໍ່ເຄີຍຜ່ານເກນ CENTS ເລີຍ ເຖິງແມ່ນພະຍາຍາມຢ່າງຈິງໃຈຫຼາຍປີ — ຜົນລັບເຫຼົ່ານີ້ພົບເລື້ອຍ ແລະ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງເລື່ອງຄວາມພະຍາຍາມບໍ່ພຽງພໍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$CENTS ສົມມຸດການເຂົ້າເຖິງ ທີ່ປຶ້ມບໍ່ໄດ້ຍອມຮັບຢ່າງເຕັມທີ່.$$, 'body', $$ການປະເມີນ ແລະ ສ້າງທຸລະກິດຕາມ Control, Entry, Need, Time ແລະ Scale ສົມມຸດວ່າມີການເຂົ້າເຖິງທຶນ, ສິນເຊື່ອ, ຕະຫຼາດ, ໂຄງສ້າງທາງກົດໝາຍ ແລະ ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງ ທີ່ບໍ່ແມ່ນຜູ້ອ່ານທຸກຄົນມີ. ຄົນທີ່ລ້ຽງດູຄອບຄົວດ້ວຍລາຍໄດ້ຈຳກັດ ບໍ່ມີພື້ນທີ່ພຽງພໍທີ່ຈະສ່ຽງລາຍໄດ້ຫຼາຍປີ ໃສ່ທຸລະກິດທີ່ຍັງບໍ່ໄດ້ພິສູດ ຄືກັບຜູ້ປະກອບການທີ່ຖືກຍົກຕົວຢ່າງໃນປຶ້ມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ກອບຄວາມຄິດນີ້ ໃຊ້ໄດ້ບໍ່ເທົ່າກັນນອກສະພາບແວດລ້ອມສະຫະລັດອາເມຣິກາທີ່ DeMarco ຂຽນມາ.$$, 'body', $$ຂະໜາດຕະຫຼາດ, ການເຂົ້າເຖິງທຶນ, ສະພາບແວດລ້ອມກົດລະບຽບ, ໂຄງລ່າງການຊຳລະເງິນ ແລະ ກຳລັງຊື້ຂອງຜູ້ບໍລິໂພກ ລ້ວນແຕ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຫຼາຍ ໃນຕະຫຼາດແບບລາວ ທຽບກັບສະພາບແວດລ້ອມສະຫະລັດອາເມຣິກາທີ່ຕົວຢ່າງໃນປຶ້ມມາຈາກ. ຫຼັກເຫດຜົນພື້ນຖານຂອງ CENTS ຍັງເປັນເລນທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດ ແຕ່ຂະໜາດຂອງໂອກາດ ແລະ ເສັ້ນທາງພາກປະຕິບັດເພື່ອບັນລຸມັນ ເບິ່ງແຕກຕ່າງອອກໄປຢູ່ບ່ອນນີ້.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອ່ານໂດຍຄຳນຶງເຖິງຂອບເຂດເຫຼົ່ານີ້ ຄຸນຄ່າແທ້ຂອງປຶ້ມນີ້ຍັງຄົງຢູ່ ເຖິງແມ່ນມີການເວົ້າເກີນຈິງ: ຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດແທ້ລະຫວ່າງລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ ແລະ ບັນຊີກວດສອບທີ່ຈັບຕ້ອງໄດ້ — CENTS — ສຳລັບທົດສອບແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດກ່ອນຈະໃຊ້ເວລາຫຼາຍປີສ້າງມັນ. ຖືເອົາຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈຂອງລາວ ເປັນຫີບຫໍ່ແຮງຈູງໃຈອ້ອມຮອບຄຳຖາມທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດແທ້ໆ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຫຼັກຖານວ່າເສັ້ນທາງທົ່ວໄປຄືຄວາມຜິດພາດສຳລັບທຸກຄົນທີ່ເລືອກມັນ.$$)
  ))
  ),
  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The Sidewalk$$, 'body', $$The roadmap of spending now with no financial plan, financed by debt and consumerism, drifting toward poverty regardless of income.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The Slowlane$$, 'body', $$The conventional roadmap of education, steady employment, modest saving, and decades of investing toward a retirement payoff in old age.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The Fastlane$$, 'body', $$A roadmap built around owning or building a scalable business system that produces income and equity independent of your personal hours.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$CENTS$$, 'body', $$DeMarco's five-part checklist for evaluating a business idea: Control, Entry, Need, Time, Scale.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The wealth equation$$, 'body', $$DeMarco's reframing that wealth is built through owned, cash-flow-producing assets, not through the size of a salary.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Law of effection$$, 'body', $$The idea that income scales with the number of people a business serves and the value delivered to each, not with hours worked.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Process, not event$$, 'body', $$DeMarco's framing that wealth results from years of sustained execution and reinvestment, not from a single lucky break or idea.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Sidewalk$$, 'body', $$ເສັ້ນທາງໃຊ້ຈ່າຍປັດຈຸບັນ ໂດຍບໍ່ມີແຜນການເງິນ ອາໄສໜີ້ສິນ ແລະ ການບໍລິໂພກ ລອຍໄປສູ່ຄວາມທຸກຍາກບໍ່ວ່າຈະມີລາຍໄດ້ເທົ່າໃດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Slowlane$$, 'body', $$ເສັ້ນທາງທົ່ວໄປຂອງການສຶກສາ, ວຽກປະຈຳ, ການເກັບເງິນພໍປະມານ ແລະ ການລົງທຶນຫຼາຍທົດສະວັດ ໄປສູ່ຜົນຕອບແທນຕອນເກສຽນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Fastlane$$, 'body', $$ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບການເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ຫຼື ການສ້າງລະບົບທຸລະກິດທີ່ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້ ຊຶ່ງສ້າງລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າຊັບສິນ ໂດຍບໍ່ຂຶ້ນກັບຊົ່ວໂມງແຮງງານສ່ວນຕົວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$CENTS$$, 'body', $$ບັນຊີກວດສອບຫ້າສ່ວນຂອງ DeMarco ສຳລັບປະເມີນແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດ: Control, Entry, Need, Time, Scale.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ສົມຜົນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ$$, 'body', $$ການປັບກອບຄວາມຄິດຂອງ DeMarco ວ່າ ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຖືກສ້າງຈາກຊັບສິນທີ່ເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ແລະ ສ້າງກະແສເງິນສົດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂະໜາດເງິນເດືອນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົດແຫ່ງອິດທິພົນ (Law of effection)$$, 'body', $$ແນວຄິດທີ່ວ່າ ລາຍໄດ້ຂະຫຍາຍຕາມຈຳນວນຄົນທີ່ທຸລະກິດຮັບໃຊ້ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າທີ່ມອບໃຫ້ແຕ່ລະຄົນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຊົ່ວໂມງເຮັດວຽກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຂະບວນການ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຫດການ (Process, not event)$$, 'body', $$ກອບຄວາມຄິດຂອງ DeMarco ວ່າ ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີເປັນຜົນຈາກຫຼາຍປີແຫ່ງການລົງມືເຮັດ ແລະ ລົງທຶນຄືນຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຫດການໂຊກດີ ຫຼື ແນວຄິດຄັ້ງດຽວ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Where in your own life are you unconsciously living Sidewalk, Slowlane, or Fastlane assumptions — and did you choose that path deliberately, or drift into it?$$, $$DeMarco argues the Slowlane depends on someone else's system. Where in your current income does that dependency actually sit, and how much real control do you have over it?$$, $$Run a business idea you've considered through CENTS. Which letter is it weakest on — and would that actually change your mind about pursuing it, or would you pursue it anyway?$$, $$The book treats stable employment as a trap to escape. For someone who values health insurance, predictable income, or family stability, is a disciplined Slowlane plan a mistake — or a legitimate rational choice the book undervalues?$$, $$DeMarco's examples assume US-level access to capital, credit, and market scale. What specifically would change if you tried to apply the CENTS framework to a business idea in Laos?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ບ່ອນໃດໃນຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານ ທີ່ທ່ານກຳລັງດຳລົງຊີວິດຕາມແນວຄິດ Sidewalk, Slowlane ຫຼື Fastlane ໂດຍບໍ່ຮູ້ຕົວ — ແລະ ທ່ານເລືອກເສັ້ນທາງນັ້ນຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ຫຼື ຫຼົງໄຫຼເຂົ້າໄປ?$$, $$DeMarco ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ Slowlane ຂຶ້ນກັບລະບົບຂອງຄົນອື່ນ. ບ່ອນໃດໃນລາຍໄດ້ປັດຈຸບັນຂອງທ່ານ ທີ່ຄວາມເພິ່ງພານັ້ນຢູ່ແທ້ໆ ແລະ ທ່ານມີການຄວບຄຸມແທ້ຈິງຫຼາຍປານໃດ?$$, $$ລອງເອົາແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດທີ່ທ່ານເຄີຍຄິດໄວ້ ໄປທົດສອບຜ່ານ CENTS. ຂໍ້ໃດອ່ອນທີ່ສຸດ — ແລະ ນັ້ນຈະປ່ຽນໃຈທ່ານແທ້ບໍ ຫຼື ທ່ານຈະເຮັດຕໍ່ໄປຢູ່ດີ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ເບິ່ງວຽກປະຈຳທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງເປັນກັບດັກທີ່ຄວນຫຼີກໜີ. ສຳລັບຄົນທີ່ໃຫ້ຄຸນຄ່າກັບປະກັນສຸຂະພາບ, ລາຍໄດ້ທີ່ຄາດເດົາໄດ້ ຫຼື ຄວາມໝັ້ນຄົງຄອບຄົວ ແຜນ Slowlane ທີ່ມີວິໄນ ຄືຄວາມຜິດພາດ ຫຼື ຄືທາງເລືອກທີ່ມີເຫດຜົນ ທີ່ປຶ້ມນີ້ໃຫ້ຄຸນຄ່າໜ້ອຍໄປ?$$, $$ຕົວຢ່າງຂອງ DeMarco ສົມມຸດການເຂົ້າເຖິງທຶນ, ສິນເຊື່ອ ແລະ ຂະໜາດຕະຫຼາດແບບສະຫະລັດອາເມຣິກາ. ຫຍັງຈະປ່ຽນແປງໂດຍສະເພາະ ຖ້າທ່ານລອງນຳກອບ CENTS ໄປໃຊ້ກັບແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດໃນລາວ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$DeMarco frames financial life as a choice of roadmap — Sidewalk, Slowlane, or Fastlane — that shapes your ceiling more than effort within it.$$, $$The Slowlane (degree, job, decades of modest saving) can work, but it spends your healthiest years funding a retirement decades away, on a plan you don't control.$$, $$Wealth is net worth built from owned assets, not the size of a paycheck — income that stops the moment you stop working is not wealth.$$, $$The Fastlane means building or acquiring a business that functions as a system, generating income and equity beyond your personal hours.$$, $$CENTS is a five-part checklist for screening a business idea: Control, Entry, Need, Time, Scale.$$, $$Control asks who actually runs the business; Entry and Need ask whether it can be easily copied and whether it serves real, scalable demand.$$, $$Time and Scale ask whether the business frees you from constant hourly presence and can reach many customers without proportional personal effort.$$, $$Income scales with how many people you genuinely serve and the value delivered to them, not with hours worked.$$, $$Wealth is a process of sustained, often unglamorous execution and reinvestment over years, not a single lucky event.$$, $$The book's confident, combative tone oversimplifies a real trade-off — for many people, the conventional path is a legitimate, lower-risk choice, not a mistake.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$DeMarco ວາງກອບຊີວິດການເງິນ ເປັນການເລືອກເສັ້ນທາງ — Sidewalk, Slowlane ຫຼື Fastlane — ຊຶ່ງກຳນົດເພດານຂອງທ່ານ ຫຼາຍກວ່າຄວາມພະຍາຍາມທີ່ໃສ່ລົງໄປ.$$, $$Slowlane (ຮຽນ, ຫາວຽກ, ເກັບເງິນທົ່ວທົດສະວັດ) ອາດໃຊ້ໄດ້ ແຕ່ໃຊ້ໄລຍະທີ່ແຂງແຮງທີ່ສຸດ ໄປສະໜອງເງິນກຽມກະສຽນທີ່ຢູ່ໄກອອກໄປ ໃນແຜນທີ່ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ຄວບຄຸມ.$$, $$ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຄືມູນຄ່າສຸດທິທີ່ສ້າງຈາກຊັບສິນທີ່ເປັນເຈົ້າຂອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂະໜາດເງິນເດືອນ — ລາຍໄດ້ທີ່ຢຸດທັນທີທີ່ທ່ານຢຸດເຮັດວຽກ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ.$$, $$Fastlane ໝາຍເຖິງການສ້າງ ຫຼື ຊື້ທຸລະກິດທີ່ເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນລະບົບ ສ້າງລາຍໄດ້ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າຊັບສິນ ນອກເໜືອຈາກຊົ່ວໂມງແຮງງານສ່ວນຕົວ.$$, $$CENTS ຄືບັນຊີກວດສອບຫ້າສ່ວນສຳລັບກັ່ນຕອງແນວຄິດທຸລະກິດ: Control, Entry, Need, Time, Scale.$$, $$Control ຖາມວ່າໃຜຄວບຄຸມທຸລະກິດແທ້ໆ; Entry ແລະ Need ຖາມວ່າມັນລອກແບບໄດ້ງ່າຍບໍ ແລະ ຕອບສະໜອງຄວາມຕ້ອງການທີ່ຂະຫຍາຍໄດ້ແທ້ບໍ.$$, $$Time ແລະ Scale ຖາມວ່າທຸລະກິດປົດປ່ອຍທ່ານຈາກການຢູ່ຕະຫຼອດເວລາບໍ ແລະ ສາມາດເຂົ້າເຖິງລູກຄ້າຫຼາຍໄດ້ ໂດຍບໍ່ຕ້ອງເພີ່ມຄວາມພະຍາຍາມສ່ວນຕົວຕາມສັດສ່ວນ.$$, $$ລາຍໄດ້ຂະຫຍາຍຕາມຈຳນວນຄົນທີ່ທ່ານຮັບໃຊ້ແທ້ໆ ແລະ ມູນຄ່າທີ່ມອບໃຫ້ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຊົ່ວໂມງທີ່ເຮັດວຽກ.$$, $$ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີຄືຂະບວນການລົງມືເຮັດ ແລະ ລົງທຶນຄືນຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງຫຼາຍປີ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຫດການໂຊກດີຄັ້ງດຽວ.$$, $$ນ້ຳສຽງທີ່ໝັ້ນໃຈ ແລະ ແຂງກ້າວຂອງປຶ້ມ ເຮັດໃຫ້ການແລກປ່ຽນທີ່ແທ້ຈິງງ່າຍເກີນໄປ — ສຳລັບຫຼາຍຄົນ ເສັ້ນທາງທົ່ວໄປຍັງເປັນທາງເລືອກທີ່ຖືກຕ້ອງ ແລະ ຄວາມສ່ຽງຕ່ຳ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຜິດພາດ.$$]
where book_id = '1944ac9b-5ab6-4108-87bd-7422b0feac79';

-- The Little Book of Ikigai — Ken Mogi
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 14,
  summary_en = $$Five everyday pillars, drawn from Japanese culture, for finding a personal reason for being.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ຫ້າຫຼັກປະຈຳວັນ ຈາກວັດທະນະທຳຍີ່ປຸ່ນ ສຳລັບການຄົ້ນຫາເຫດຜົນໃນການມີຊີວິດຂອງຕົນເອງ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Neuroscientist Ken Mogi argues that ikigai — a Japanese word roughly meaning a reason for getting up in the morning — is not a grand career discovery or a single dream job waiting to be found. Drawing on sushi apprenticeships, tea ceremony, subculture fan communities, and the quiet rituals of daily Japanese life, he distills it into five interlocking habits of mind: starting small, accepting yourself as you are, valuing harmony over disruption, savoring little pleasures, and staying present in whatever you are doing. This lesson works through each pillar in turn, then flags where the book's warm, essayistic style should be read with a critical eye — including the popular online ikigai diagram that did not actually come from this book or from Japan.$$,
  deck_lo = $$Ken Mogi ນັກວິທະຍາສາດດ້ານສະໝອງ ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ikigai — ຄຳສັບຍີ່ປຸ່ນທີ່ມີຄວາມໝາຍປະມານ "ເຫດຜົນທີ່ລຸກຂຶ້ນຕອນເຊົ້າ" — ບໍ່ແມ່ນການຄົ້ນພົບອາຊີບອັນຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່ ຫຼື ວຽກໃນຝັນອັນດຽວທີ່ລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ຄົ້ນຫາ. ໂດຍອີງໃສ່ການເປັນລູກສິດຮ້ານຊູຊິ, ພິທີຊາ, ກຸ່ມແຟນຄລັບຍ່ອຍ ແລະ ພິທີກຳງຽບໆໃນຊີວິດປະຈຳວັນຂອງຄົນຍີ່ປຸ່ນ, ລາວກັ່ນຄວາມຄິດອອກມາເປັນນິໄສທາງຄວາມຄິດຫ້າຢ່າງທີ່ພົວພັນກັນ: ເລີ່ມຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ, ຍອມຮັບຕົນເອງໃນສະພາບປັດຈຸບັນ, ໃຫ້ຄຸນຄ່າກັບຄວາມກົມກຽວຫຼາຍກວ່າການແຫກແຫນວ, ຊື່ນຊົມຄວາມສຸກນ້ອຍໆ ແລະ ຢູ່ກັບປັດຈຸບັນໃນທຸກສິ່ງທີ່ກຳລັງເຮັດ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍແຕ່ລະຫຼັກເທື່ອລະຂໍ້ ແລ້ວຈຶ່ງຊີ້ໃຫ້ເຫັນຈຸດທີ່ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍສາຍຕາທີ່ວິພາກວິຈານ — ລວມທັງແຜນວົງມົນ ikigai ທີ່ດັງທາງອອນລາຍ ຊຶ່ງແທ້ຈິງແລ້ວບໍ່ໄດ້ມາຈາກປຶ້ມຫົວນີ້ ຫຼື ຈາກຍີ່ປຸ່ນເລີຍ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$What ikigai actually means$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The word ikigai combines iki (to live) and gai (a reason, or value derived from an action) into something like "a reason for being" or "that which makes life worth living." Mogi is careful to note that in ordinary Japanese usage, the word carries none of the grandeur it has picked up abroad. Someone can say their ikigai is their grandchildren, their morning coffee, their vegetable garden, or a weekly volleyball game with coworkers, and no listener would find that a strange or small answer.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Ikigai$$, 'body', $$A reason for being, or a source of value that makes daily life worth living. In Japan it is applied casually to routines, relationships, and hobbies, not reserved for a single grand life purpose.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This matters because a specific image of ikigai has spread widely outside Japan: a Venn diagram of four overlapping circles — what you love, what you are good at, what the world needs, what you can be paid for — with ikigai sitting in the middle where all four meet. Mogi's own account is different and considerably broader. He treats ikigai as something that can be found in a single afternoon spent well, not only at the intersection of career, skill, and market demand.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Rather than a checklist to satisfy, Mogi frames ikigai as a way of living that shows up through five recurring habits of mind, drawn from observing craftsmen, tea masters, sushi chefs, and ordinary people going about unremarkable days. The rest of the book, and this lesson, works through those five in turn.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Ikigai is closer to a daily reason for living well than a single career discovery waiting to be found.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Pillar one — starting small$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Mogi's first pillar is that mastery and satisfaction almost always begin with humble, unglamorous first steps, and that skipping or resenting those steps is a common way people cut themselves off from ikigai before they ever get close to it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His central illustration is the traditional path to becoming a sushi chef in Japan. A young apprentice does not touch fish for years. The early work is washing rice, wringing towels, cleaning the workspace, and watching — tasks so far removed from the eventual skill that an impatient outsider might call them a waste of time. Mogi's reading is the opposite: those years build attention, discipline, and a feel for the environment of the kitchen that no amount of shortcut instruction could substitute for. The small task is not a barrier in front of the craft; it is already the craft, in an early and unglamorous form.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Craft apprenticeships.$$, 'body', $$Traditional training in sushi, pottery, carpentry, and other trades in Japan is built on long, repetitive foundational stages before a student is trusted with the more visible or skilled parts of the work.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Kaizen, continuous small improvement.$$, 'body', $$The idea, familiar from Japanese manufacturing, that steady incremental refinement of small details compounds into quality that a single dramatic overhaul rarely matches.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The practical takeaway is not patience for its own sake, but a reframing: the small, tedious first step is where attention and skill are actually built, so avoiding it in favor of jumping straight to the interesting part usually undermines the very outcome being sought.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The unglamorous first steps are not an obstacle before the craft begins — they already are the craft.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Pillar two — releasing yourself (accepting who you are)$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The second pillar is self-acceptance: honestly acknowledging your current abilities, circumstances, and limitations, rather than resisting them or measuring yourself constantly against other people. Mogi presents this not as resignation but as the actual starting point from which real progress becomes possible.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The reasoning runs like this: energy spent denying where you currently stand, or spent in anxious comparison with someone further along, is energy not spent improving. A beginner potter who is embarrassed by an early lopsided bowl and hides it away learns less than one who examines it honestly, accepts that this is what their hands could do today, and lets that plain assessment guide the next attempt. Mogi frames self-acceptance as a kind of release — letting go of the story you wish were true about your current level, so you can see the actual one clearly enough to work with it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He also connects this to a broader cultural comfort, in the traditions he draws on, with quiet self-improvement that does not require public comparison to register as real. You do not need to be better than a rival for the effort to count; you only need an honest picture of where you are now and the next small step from there.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Progress starts with an honest look at where you actually are, not with resisting it or measuring yourself against someone else.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Pillar three — harmony and sustainability$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The third pillar shifts from the individual to the surrounding community and environment: ikigai, in Mogi's account, is sustained by fitting well with the people and systems around you, not by disrupting them for personal advancement.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He draws a contrast with narratives of success more common in individualistic cultures, where standing out, self-promotion, and disruption are often treated as the direct route to achievement. In the Japanese traditions he describes, a craftsman's or a worker's long-term satisfaction is bound up with the health of the surrounding relationships — the trust of a team, the reputation of a shop, the rhythm of a community — and a person willing to sacrifice those relationships for individual gain is often seen as undermining the very foundation their ikigai rests on.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Wa (harmony)$$, 'body', $$A cultural value placing weight on cooperative, long-term relationships within a group over individual self-promotion or disruption. Mogi treats it as one of the conditions that make a sustainable ikigai possible.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is not presented as an argument against ambition altogether, but as a claim about durability: achievement built on cooperation and mutual trust tends to last and to keep generating meaning, while achievement built by working against the people around you tends to erode the very relationships that would have sustained it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Ikigai that depends on undermining the people around you rarely lasts as long as ikigai built through cooperation with them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Pillar four — the joy of little things$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The fourth pillar is that ordinary, small pleasures are a legitimate and sustainable source of ikigai — not a lesser consolation prize compared to grand achievement, but one of its most reliable sources.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Mogi points to the disproportionate care many Japanese traditions put into small, easily overlooked things: the precise folding of a wrapping cloth, the seasonal detail in a simple meal, the exact texture of a well-made bowl, a short walk noticed rather than merely completed. None of these require wealth, fame, or a major life event to enjoy, and none of them wait for some future milestone before they can be appreciated.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The functional argument is about reliability. Grand achievements are rare, effortful, and often outside your control — a promotion, an award, a breakthrough may or may not arrive regardless of how hard you work. Small pleasures are available constantly, nearly every day, to anyone willing to actually notice them. A person whose sense of ikigai depends entirely on major milestones has built a fragile, intermittent source of meaning; a person who has also learned to enjoy a good cup of tea or a well-swept doorway has a much steadier one running underneath it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Small pleasures are not a consolation prize next to grand achievement — they are a steadier and more available source of ikigai.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Pillar five — being in the here and now$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The fifth pillar is mindful absorption in whatever you are currently doing, treated as valuable in itself rather than only as a means to some future payoff.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Mogi draws a deliberate contrast with the Western psychological concept of flow, the state of total absorption in a challenging task, usually studied in terms of optimal performance and productivity. He treats presence in the Japanese sense he is describing as related but not identical: it is less about hitting a peak-performance zone and more about a quality of attention brought to ordinary moments, including ones with no particular challenge or stakes attached — sweeping a floor, preparing tea, walking a familiar route. The value is not what the presence produces afterward; it is the quality of the moment itself while it is happening.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He connects this to the traditional Japanese tea ceremony, where an entire elaborate ritual is built around fully inhabiting a sequence of small, deliberate actions, and to craftsmanship generally, where a worker absorbed in the immediate task tends to produce better and more honest work than one whose attention is elsewhere, rehearsing the outcome instead of attending to the process.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Presence is valuable in the moment itself, not only as a means toward whatever the moment eventually produces.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Ikigai without a dream job$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A recurring argument across the book is that ikigai does not require a singular, identity-defining career, external validation, fame, or wealth. Mogi is explicit that this is where the popular Western reading of the concept — often built around finding the perfect intersection of passion, skill, market, and pay — pulls away from how the word is actually used and lived in Japan.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Ordinary work can carry it.$$, 'body', $$A shopkeeper, a train conductor, or an office employee can hold as strong a sense of ikigai in their daily routine as anyone in a celebrated creative career, if the five pillars are present in how the work is approached.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Hobbies count fully.$$, 'body', $$A pastime pursued for its own sake, with no ambition to monetize or professionalize it, is a complete and valid source of ikigai, not a lesser substitute for a "real" one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Relationships and routines count too.$$, 'body', $$Caring for family, tending a garden, a regular walk, a standing arrangement with friends — none of these need to resemble achievement to function as ikigai.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The underlying claim is that ikigai is abundant rather than scarce: most people already have several sources of it scattered through an ordinary week, and the more urgent task is usually noticing and tending them, not searching for one grand, missing purpose.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Ikigai is usually already present in ordinary routines and relationships — the task is noticing it, not discovering one grand missing purpose.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Hospitality, craft, and community — where these habits come from$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Mogi grounds all five pillars in specific strands of Japanese culture rather than presenting them as abstract principles, and two of these strands recur throughout the book: hospitality and community.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Omotenashi$$, 'body', $$A Japanese approach to hospitality emphasizing anticipating a guest's needs quietly and thoroughly, without expectation of recognition or reward. Mogi treats it as harmony and attentiveness to the present moment applied outward, toward another person.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He also draws on subculture and fan communities — enthusiasts of anime, trains, particular foods, or crafts who organize their free time and social lives around a shared, often unglamorous interest. These communities, in his account, generate real ikigai precisely because they combine several of the pillars at once: small, repeated engagement with a niche interest; acceptance of that interest without needing outside validation; a cooperative community rather than a competitive one; and enjoyment of small details only insiders would notice.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The throughline is that ikigai, in this account, is rarely manufactured by an individual acting alone. It tends to grow out of a craft, a practice, or a community with its own history and standards — something a person joins and is shaped by, rather than invents from nothing.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Ikigai typically grows out of joining an existing craft, practice, or community, rather than being invented alone from nothing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How the five pillars work together$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Mogi does not present the five pillars — starting small, releasing yourself, harmony and sustainability, the joy of little things, being in the here and now — as sequential steps to complete in order. They function more as five lenses that reinforce each other, often visible together in the same small moment.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Consider the sushi apprentice again: the humble first tasks (starting small) are only bearable and productive if the apprentice honestly accepts their current beginner status rather than resenting it (releasing yourself); the apprentice's progress depends on the trust and rhythm of the whole kitchen, not on outshining fellow apprentices (harmony); the daily texture of the work — the smell of vinegar rice, the feel of a good knife — offers real satisfaction long before mastery arrives (the joy of little things); and none of it works if the apprentice's mind is elsewhere, rehearsing a future promotion instead of attending to the rice in front of them (the here and now).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The five pillars, taken together, describe less a technique for acquiring ikigai and more a description of what ikigai already looks like when it is present: a person doing something small and real, accepted honestly, embedded in a cooperative context, enjoyed in its details, attended to fully in the moment.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The five pillars are not steps in sequence — they are five things usually present together in any moment that actually has ikigai in it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Little Book of Ikigai is a short, warm collection of reflections illustrated with cultural examples, not a research-backed or rigorously structured framework, and it is worth being clear-eyed about what that means for how much weight to put on it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The popular online ikigai diagram is not from this book, and not traditionally Japanese.$$, 'body', $$The four-circle Venn diagram — what you love, what you're good at, what the world needs, what you can be paid for — circulates widely online and is often mistakenly attributed to Japanese culture or to books like this one. It did not originate in Japan and is not how the word is used there. Mogi's actual account is broader, less career-focused, and not built around a formula for choosing a job. Readers who arrive expecting the diagram should be aware the two versions get conflated, and that this book is closer to the original, looser sense of the word.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Cultural illustration risks becoming cultural generalization.$$, 'body', $$Mogi draws vivid, specific examples from Japanese craftsmanship, hospitality, and community life to illustrate universal-feeling claims about meaning and satisfaction. The examples are genuinely illuminating, but a short book built on selective cultural vignettes can flatten a complex, internally diverse culture into a tidy set of lessons, and readers should hold the generalizations more loosely than the individual stories.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Harmony and acceptance, pushed too far, can discourage legitimate ambition or resistance.$$, 'body', $$The book does not spend much time on the line between healthy self-acceptance and settling for unfair or genuinely changeable circumstances, or between valuing harmony and tolerating a situation that should actually be challenged or left. Readers applying these ideas should supply their own judgment about when acceptance is wisdom and when it is avoidance.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It is essayistic, not evidence-driven.$$, 'body', $$The book is built from cultural observation, personal reflection, and illustrative anecdote rather than citation-heavy behavioral research. That makes it a source of vocabulary and perspective more than a validated method, and its claims are best treated as thoughtful proposals rather than established findings.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read with those caveats in mind, the book's real value holds up well: a compact, memorable vocabulary — starting small, releasing yourself, harmony, little pleasures, the here and now — for noticing sources of meaning that are usually already present in an ordinary life, rather than a grand purpose still waiting to be discovered.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ikigai ໝາຍຄວາມວ່າຫຍັງແທ້ໆ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄຳວ່າ ikigai ປະກອບດ້ວຍ iki (ການມີຊີວິດ) ແລະ gai (ເຫດຜົນ ຫຼື ຄຸນຄ່າທີ່ໄດ້ຈາກການກະທຳ) ລວມກັນເປັນບາງສິ່ງຄື "ເຫດຜົນໃນການມີຊີວິດ" ຫຼື "ສິ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດມີຄ່າຄວນຢູ່". Mogi ລະມັດລະວັງທີ່ຈະຊີ້ໃຫ້ເຫັນວ່າ ໃນການໃຊ້ພາສາຍີ່ປຸ່ນປະຈຳວັນ ຄຳນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ມີຄວາມຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່ຄືທີ່ມັນໄດ້ຮັບໃນຕ່າງປະເທດ. ຄົນໜຶ່ງສາມາດເວົ້າວ່າ ikigai ຂອງຕົນຄື ຫຼານ, ກາເຟຕອນເຊົ້າ, ສວນຜັກ ຫຼື ການຫຼີ້ນວອນເລບານກັບເພື່ອນຮ່ວມງານທຸກອາທິດ ໂດຍບໍ່ມີໃຜເຫັນວ່າຄຳຕອບນັ້ນແປກ ຫຼື ນ້ອຍໆເລີຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Ikigai$$, 'body', $$ເຫດຜົນໃນການມີຊີວິດ ຫຼື ແຫຼ່ງທີ່ມາຂອງຄຸນຄ່າທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດປະຈຳວັນມີຄ່າຄວນຢູ່. ໃນຍີ່ປຸ່ນ ຄຳນີ້ຖືກໃຊ້ຢ່າງທຳມະດາກັບກິດຈະວັດ, ຄວາມສຳພັນ ແລະ ງານອະດິເລກ ບໍ່ໄດ້ສະຫງວນໄວ້ສະເພາະຈຸດປະສົງໃຫຍ່ໃນຊີວິດພຽງອັນດຽວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ສຳຄັນ ເພາະມີພາບສະເພາະຂອງ ikigai ທີ່ແຜ່ຫຼາຍນອກປະເທດຍີ່ປຸ່ນ: ແຜນວົງມົນສີ່ວົງທີ່ຊ້ອນກັນ — ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານຮັກ, ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເກັ່ງ, ສິ່ງທີ່ໂລກຕ້ອງການ, ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານໄດ້ຮັບຄ່າຈ້າງ — ໂດຍມີ ikigai ຢູ່ຈຸດກາງທີ່ທັງສີ່ວົງມາພົບກັນ. ຄຳອະທິບາຍຂອງ Mogi ເອງ ແຕກຕ່າງກັນ ແລະ ກວ້າງກວ່າຫຼາຍ. ລາວເບິ່ງ ikigai ວ່າສາມາດພົບໄດ້ໃນຕອນບ່າຍໜຶ່ງທີ່ໃຊ້ໄປຢ່າງດີ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ຢູ່ຈຸດຕັດກັນລະຫວ່າງອາຊີບ, ທັກສະ ແລະ ຄວາມຕ້ອງການຂອງຕະຫຼາດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນລາຍການກວດສອບທີ່ຕ້ອງເຮັດໃຫ້ຄົບ Mogi ອະທິບາຍ ikigai ວ່າເປັນວິທີການດຳລົງຊີວິດ ທີ່ປະກົດອອກມາຜ່ານນິໄສທາງຄວາມຄິດຫ້າຢ່າງທີ່ເກີດຂຶ້ນຊ້ຳໆ ຊຶ່ງໄດ້ມາຈາກການສັງເກດຊ່າງຝີມື, ອາຈານພິທີຊາ, ພໍ່ຄົວຊູຊິ ແລະ ຄົນທຳມະດາທີ່ໃຊ້ຊີວິດປະຈຳວັນທີ່ບໍ່ໂດດເດັ່ນ. ສ່ວນທີ່ເຫຼືອຂອງປຶ້ມ ແລະ ບົດຮຽນນີ້ ຈະອະທິບາຍທັງຫ້າຢ່າງນັ້ນເທື່ອລະຂໍ້.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ikigai ໃກ້ຄຽງກັບເຫດຜົນປະຈຳວັນໃນການດຳລົງຊີວິດຢ່າງດີ ຫຼາຍກວ່າການຄົ້ນພົບອາຊີບອັນດຽວທີ່ລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ຄົ້ນຫາ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຫຼັກທີໜຶ່ງ — ເລີ່ມຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຫຼັກທຳອິດຂອງ Mogi ຄື ຄວາມຊຳນານ ແລະ ຄວາມພໍໃຈ ເກືອບຈະເລີ່ມຕົ້ນຈາກບາດກ້າວທຳອິດທີ່ຖ່ອມຕົວ ແລະ ບໍ່ໜ້າຕື່ນເຕັ້ນສະເໝີ ແລະ ການຂ້າມ ຫຼື ຄຽດແຄ້ນຕໍ່ບາດກ້າວເຫຼົ່ານັ້ນ ເປັນວິທີທົ່ວໄປທີ່ຄົນຕັດຂາດຕົນເອງອອກຈາກ ikigai ກ່ອນທີ່ຈະໄດ້ເຂົ້າໃກ້ມັນເລີຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຕົວຢ່າງຫຼັກຂອງລາວ ຄືເສັ້ນທາງດັ້ງເດີມໃນການເປັນພໍ່ຄົວຊູຊິໃນຍີ່ປຸ່ນ. ລູກສິດໜຸ່ມຈະບໍ່ໄດ້ຈັບປາເປັນເວລາຫຼາຍປີ. ວຽກໃນຕົ້ນໆ ຄືການລ້າງເຂົ້າ, ບິດຜ້າ, ທຳຄວາມສະອາດພື້ນທີ່ເຮັດວຽກ ແລະ ການສັງເກດເບິ່ງ — ວຽກທີ່ຫ່າງໄກຈາກທັກສະສຸດທ້າຍ ຈົນຄົນທີ່ໃຈຮ້ອນອາດເອີ້ນວ່າເສຍເວລາ. ການອ່ານຂອງ Mogi ກົງກັນຂ້າມ: ຫຼາຍປີເຫຼົ່ານັ້ນສ້າງຄວາມສົນໃຈ, ລະບຽບວິໄນ ແລະ ຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກຕໍ່ບັນຍາກາດຄົວ ທີ່ບໍ່ມີການສອນແບບຫຍໍ້ໃດຈະທົດແທນໄດ້. ວຽກນ້ອຍໆນັ້ນບໍ່ແມ່ນອຸປະສັກຢູ່ໜ້າຫັດຖະກຳ — ມັນຄືຫັດຖະກຳຢູ່ແລ້ວ ໃນຮູບແບບຕົ້ນໆ ແລະ ບໍ່ໂດດເດັ່ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ການເປັນລູກສິດຝຶກຫັດຖະກຳ.$$, 'body', $$ການຝຶກແບບດັ້ງເດີມໃນຊູຊິ, ເຄື່ອງປັ້ນດິນເຜົາ, ວຽກໄມ້ ແລະ ອາຊີບອື່ນໆໃນຍີ່ປຸ່ນ ສ້າງຂຶ້ນຈາກຂັ້ນຕອນພື້ນຖານທີ່ຍາວນານ ແລະ ຊ້ຳໆ ກ່ອນທີ່ນັກຮຽນຈະຖືກໄວ້ວາງໃຈໃຫ້ເຮັດສ່ວນທີ່ໂດດເດັ່ນ ຫຼື ຕ້ອງໃຊ້ທັກສະສູງກວ່າ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Kaizen, ການປັບປຸງນ້ອຍໆຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ.$$, 'body', $$ແນວຄິດທີ່ຄຸ້ນເຄີຍຈາກອຸດສາຫະກຳຍີ່ປຸ່ນ ວ່າການປັບປຸງລາຍລະອຽດນ້ອຍໆຢ່າງສະໝ່ຳສະເໝີ ສະສົມກາຍເປັນຄຸນນະພາບ ທີ່ການປັບປຸງໃຫຍ່ຄັ້ງດຽວ ຍາກຈະທຽບໄດ້.$$)
