  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Ikigai$$, 'body', $$A reason for being, or a source of value that makes daily life worth living. Applied in everyday Japanese to routines, relationships, and hobbies, not reserved for one grand purpose.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Starting small$$, 'body', $$The first pillar: mastery and satisfaction begin with humble, repetitive foundational tasks that are already part of the craft, not an obstacle before it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Releasing yourself$$, 'body', $$The second pillar: honestly accepting your current abilities and circumstances as the real starting point for progress, instead of resisting them or comparing yourself to others.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Wa (harmony)$$, 'body', $$A cultural value placing weight on cooperative, long-term relationships within a group over individual self-promotion or disruption; the third pillar's foundation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The joy of little things$$, 'body', $$The fourth pillar: small, ordinary pleasures as a steady, reliable, always-available source of meaning, not a lesser consolation next to grand achievement.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Being in the here and now$$, 'body', $$The fifth pillar: mindful absorption in the present task, valued for its own quality in the moment rather than only for what it produces later.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Omotenashi$$, 'body', $$A Japanese approach to hospitality: anticipating a guest's needs quietly and thoroughly, with no expectation of recognition or reward.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Ikigai$$, 'body', $$ເຫດຜົນໃນການມີຊີວິດ ຫຼື ແຫຼ່ງທີ່ມາຂອງຄຸນຄ່າທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊີວິດປະຈຳວັນມີຄ່າຄວນຢູ່. ໃນພາສາຍີ່ປຸ່ນປະຈຳວັນ ໃຊ້ກັບກິດຈະວັດ, ຄວາມສຳພັນ ແລະ ງານອະດິເລກ ບໍ່ໄດ້ສະຫງວນໄວ້ສະເພາະຈຸດປະສົງໃຫຍ່ອັນດຽວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ເລີ່ມຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ$$, 'body', $$ຫຼັກທຳອິດ: ຄວາມຊຳນານ ແລະ ຄວາມພໍໃຈ ເລີ່ມຈາກວຽກພື້ນຖານທີ່ຖ່ອມຕົວ ແລະ ຊ້ຳໆ ຊຶ່ງເປັນສ່ວນໜຶ່ງຂອງຫັດຖະກຳຢູ່ແລ້ວ ບໍ່ແມ່ນອຸປະສັກກ່ອນມັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ປ່ອຍວາງຕົນເອງ$$, 'body', $$ຫຼັກທີສອງ: ການຍອມຮັບຄວາມສາມາດ ແລະ ສະຖານະການປັດຈຸບັນຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ ວ່າເປັນຈຸດເລີ່ມຕົ້ນແທ້ຈິງຂອງຄວາມຄືບໜ້າ ແທນທີ່ຈະຕໍ່ຕ້ານມັນ ຫຼື ປຽບທຽບກັບຄົນອື່ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Wa (ຄວາມກົມກຽວ)$$, 'body', $$ຄຸນຄ່າທາງວັດທະນະທຳທີ່ໃຫ້ນ້ຳໜັກກັບຄວາມສຳພັນແບບຮ່ວມມືກັນໄລຍະຍາວພາຍໃນກຸ່ມ ຫຼາຍກວ່າການໂປຣໂມດຕົນເອງ ຫຼື ການແຫກແຫນວແບບບຸກຄົນ; ພື້ນຖານຂອງຫຼັກທີສາມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມສຸກຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ$$, 'body', $$ຫຼັກທີສີ່: ຄວາມສຸກນ້ອຍໆປະຈຳວັນ ເປັນແຫຼ່ງຄວາມໝາຍທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ, ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖື ແລະ ຫາໄດ້ຕະຫຼອດເວລາ ບໍ່ແມ່ນລາງວັນປອບໃຈທີ່ດ້ອຍກວ່າຄຽງຄູ່ຄວາມສຳເລັດອັນຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການຢູ່ກັບປັດຈຸບັນ$$, 'body', $$ຫຼັກທີຫ້າ: ຄວາມເອົາໃຈໃສ່ຢ່າງເຕັມທີ່ຕໍ່ວຽກປັດຈຸບັນ ຖືກໃຫ້ຄຸນຄ່າໃນຄຸນນະພາບຂອງມັນເອງໃນຊ່ວງເວລານັ້ນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ສິ່ງທີ່ມັນຜະລິດອອກມາພາຍຫຼັງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Omotenashi$$, 'body', $$ວິທີການຕ້ອນຮັບແບບຍີ່ປຸ່ນ: ການຄາດຄະເນຄວາມຕ້ອງການຂອງແຂກຢ່າງງຽບໆ ແລະ ຄົບຖ້ວນ ໂດຍບໍ່ຄາດຫວັງການຮັບຮູ້ ຫຼື ລາງວັນ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Mogi says ikigai does not require a singular dream career. Where in your own ordinary week — a routine, a relationship, a small habit — is a source of ikigai you may be overlooking because it isn't a big achievement?$$, $$The popular online ikigai diagram frames it as an intersection of passion, skill, market need, and pay. Now that you've seen Mogi's broader, less career-bound version, which one better describes how you actually find meaning in daily life?$$, $$Think of something you're a beginner at. Are you resisting your current skill level in a way that's slowing you down, or have you genuinely accepted it as pillar two describes?$$, $$The book values harmony and cooperation over individual self-promotion. Can you think of a situation where that ideal, taken too far, would mean tolerating something that should actually be challenged or changed?$$, $$Mogi distinguishes being in the here and now from the Western idea of flow. Think of a recent moment of full attention — was it tied to performing well at something, or was it valuable simply as an experience in itself?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$Mogi ບອກວ່າ ikigai ບໍ່ຈຳເປັນຕ້ອງມີອາຊີບໃນຝັນອັນດຽວ. ໃນອາທິດປົກກະຕິຂອງທ່ານເອງ — ກິດຈະວັດ, ຄວາມສຳພັນ, ນິໄສນ້ອຍໆ — ມີແຫຼ່ງ ikigai ໃດແດ່ທີ່ທ່ານອາດຈະມອງຂ້າມ ເພາະມັນບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມສຳເລັດອັນໃຫຍ່ໂຕ?$$, $$ແຜນວົງມົນ ikigai ທີ່ດັງທາງອອນລາຍ ອະທິບາຍມັນວ່າເປັນຈຸດຕັດລະຫວ່າງຄວາມມັກ, ທັກສະ, ຄວາມຕ້ອງການຂອງຕະຫຼາດ ແລະ ຄ່າຈ້າງ. ຫຼັງຈາກໄດ້ເຫັນຄຳອະທິບາຍທີ່ກວ້າງກວ່າ ແລະ ບໍ່ຜູກມັດກັບອາຊີບຂອງ Mogi ແລ້ວ ອັນໃດອະທິບາຍໄດ້ດີກວ່າກ່ຽວກັບວິທີທີ່ທ່ານຄົ້ນຫາຄວາມໝາຍໃນຊີວິດປະຈຳວັນແທ້ໆ?$$, $$ລອງນຶກເຖິງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານຍັງເປັນມືໃໝ່ຢູ່. ທ່ານກຳລັງຕໍ່ຕ້ານລະດັບທັກສະປັດຈຸບັນຂອງທ່ານ ໃນທາງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຊ້າລົງບໍ ຫຼື ວ່າທ່ານໄດ້ຍອມຮັບມັນຢ່າງແທ້ຈິງ ດັ່ງທີ່ຫຼັກທີສອງອະທິບາຍ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ໃຫ້ຄຸນຄ່າກັບຄວາມກົມກຽວ ແລະ ຄວາມຮ່ວມມື ຫຼາຍກວ່າການໂປຣໂມດຕົນເອງ. ທ່ານນຶກອອກສະຖານະການໃດແດ່ ທີ່ອຸດົມການນັ້ນ ຖ້າໄປໄກເກີນໄປ ຈະໝາຍຄວາມວ່າຕ້ອງທົນຕໍ່ບາງສິ່ງທີ່ຄວນຖືກທ້າທາຍ ຫຼື ປ່ຽນແປງແທ້ໆ?$$, $$Mogi ແຍກການຢູ່ກັບປັດຈຸບັນ ອອກຈາກແນວຄິດ flow ຂອງຕາເວັນຕົກ. ລອງນຶກເຖິງຊ່ວງເວລາທີ່ທ່ານເອົາໃຈໃສ່ເຕັມທີ່ເມື່ອບໍ່ດົນມານີ້ — ມັນຜູກພັນກັບການເຮັດບາງສິ່ງໃຫ້ດີ ຫຼື ວ່າມັນມີຄ່າພຽງແຕ່ໃນຖານະປະສົບການໃນຕົວມັນເອງ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Ikigai roughly means a reason for being, and in everyday Japanese usage it applies to small routines and relationships, not only to a career or a grand purpose.$$, $$The popular four-circle Venn diagram of ikigai found online did not come from this book and is not a traditional Japanese framework — Mogi's own account is broader and less career-focused.$$, $$Starting small: mastery is built through humble, repetitive first steps, illustrated by the years a sushi apprentice spends on tasks far removed from making sushi.$$, $$Releasing yourself: honestly accepting your current abilities and circumstances, rather than resisting them or comparing yourself to others, is the real starting point for progress.$$, $$Harmony and sustainability: ikigai that depends on disrupting or outshining the people around you tends to erode; ikigai built through cooperation tends to last.$$, $$The joy of little things: small, everyday pleasures are a steady and reliable source of meaning, not a lesser substitute for grand achievement.$$, $$Being in the here and now: full attention to the present task matters in itself, not only as a means to a future result — related to, but not identical with, the Western idea of flow.$$, $$Ikigai does not require fame, wealth, or a singular dream career; ordinary work, hobbies, and relationships can carry it fully.$$, $$The five pillars are not sequential steps but interlocking qualities usually present together in any moment that actually has ikigai in it.$$, $$The book is a short, essayistic collection of cultural reflections rather than a research-backed framework, so its claims are best read as thoughtful proposals, not proven findings.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$Ikigai ໝາຍຄວາມປະມານວ່າ ເຫດຜົນໃນການມີຊີວິດ ແລະ ໃນການໃຊ້ພາສາຍີ່ປຸ່ນປະຈຳວັນ ຄຳນີ້ຖືກໃຊ້ກັບກິດຈະວັດ ແລະ ຄວາມສຳພັນນ້ອຍໆ ບໍ່ໄດ້ສະເພາະອາຊີບ ຫຼື ຈຸດປະສົງອັນຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່ເທົ່ານັ້ນ.$$, $$ແຜນວົງມົນສີ່ວົງຂອງ ikigai ທີ່ດັງທາງອອນລາຍ ບໍ່ໄດ້ມາຈາກປຶ້ມຫົວນີ້ ແລະ ບໍ່ແມ່ນກອບແນວຄິດແບບດັ້ງເດີມຂອງຍີ່ປຸ່ນ — ຄຳອະທິບາຍຂອງ Mogi ເອງກວ້າງກວ່າ ແລະ ບໍ່ເນັ້ນອາຊີບ.$$, $$ເລີ່ມຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ: ຄວາມຊຳນານຖືກສ້າງຂຶ້ນຜ່ານບາດກ້າວທຳອິດທີ່ຖ່ອມຕົວ ແລະ ຊ້ຳໆ ດັ່ງທີ່ເຫັນຈາກຫຼາຍປີທີ່ລູກສິດຊູຊິໃຊ້ໄປກັບວຽກທີ່ຫ່າງໄກຈາກການເຮັດຊູຊິ.$$, $$ປ່ອຍວາງຕົນເອງ: ການຍອມຮັບຄວາມສາມາດ ແລະ ສະຖານະການປັດຈຸບັນຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ ແທນທີ່ຈະຕໍ່ຕ້ານມັນ ຫຼື ປຽບທຽບກັບຄົນອື່ນ ຄືຈຸດເລີ່ມຕົ້ນແທ້ຈິງຂອງຄວາມຄືບໜ້າ.$$, $$ຄວາມກົມກຽວ ແລະ ຄວາມຍືນຍົງ: ikigai ທີ່ອາໄສການທຳລາຍ ຫຼື ເອົາຊະນະຄົນອ້ອມຂ້າງ ມັກຈະເສື່ອມ; ikigai ທີ່ສ້າງຂຶ້ນຜ່ານຄວາມຮ່ວມມື ມັກຈະຄົງຢູ່.$$, $$ຄວາມສຸກຈາກສິ່ງນ້ອຍໆ: ຄວາມສຸກປະຈຳວັນນ້ອຍໆ ຄືແຫຼ່ງຄວາມໝາຍທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ ແລະ ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖື ບໍ່ແມ່ນສິ່ງທົດແທນທີ່ດ້ອຍກວ່າຄວາມສຳເລັດອັນຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່.$$, $$ການຢູ່ກັບປັດຈຸບັນ: ຄວາມເອົາໃຈໃສ່ເຕັມທີ່ຕໍ່ວຽກປັດຈຸບັນ ມີຄ່າໃນຕົວມັນເອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງແຕ່ເປັນວິທີໄປສູ່ຜົນລັບອະນາຄົດ — ກ່ຽວຂ້ອງແຕ່ບໍ່ຄືກັບແນວຄິດ flow ຂອງຕາເວັນຕົກ.$$, $$Ikigai ບໍ່ຈຳເປັນຕ້ອງມີຊື່ສຽງ, ຄວາມຮັ່ງມີ ຫຼື ອາຊີບໃນຝັນອັນດຽວ; ວຽກທຳມະດາ, ງານອະດິເລກ ແລະ ຄວາມສຳພັນ ສາມາດເປັນແຫຼ່ງນັ້ນໄດ້ຢ່າງສົມບູນ.$$, $$ຫຼັກທັງຫ້າ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂັ້ນຕອນຕາມລຳດັບ ແຕ່ເປັນຄຸນລັກສະນະທີ່ພົວພັນກັນ ມັກປະກົດຮ່ວມກັນ ໃນທຸກຊ່ວງເວລາທີ່ມີ ikigai ຢູ່ແທ້ໆ.$$, $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ເປັນການລວບລວມການສະທ້ອນຄິດທາງວັດທະນະທຳສັ້ນໆ ບໍ່ແມ່ນກອບແນວຄິດທີ່ອີງໃສ່ການຄົ້ນຄວ້າ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຂອງມັນ ຄວນອ່ານເປັນຂໍ້ສະເໜີທີ່ຄິດຮອບຄອບ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂໍ້ຄົ້ນພົບທີ່ພິສູດແລ້ວ.$$]
where book_id = '6bd0c325-bfd3-4678-976f-7b2a6d36f4b4';

-- The Magic of Thinking Big — David Schwartz
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A classic argument that belief, not just ability, sets the ceiling on what you attempt and achieve.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ປຶ້ມຄລາສສິກທີ່ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຄວາມເຊື່ອ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແຕ່ຄວາມສາມາດ ເປັນຕົວກຳນົດເພດານຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານກ້າລອງ ແລະ ບັນລຸໄດ້.$$,
  deck_en = $$David J. Schwartz's central claim is disarmingly simple: you rarely accomplish more than you believe is possible, so the size of your thinking — not the size of your talent, your starting resources, or your luck — is the first thing worth working on. This lesson walks through his diagnosis of excusitis, the habitual disease of excuse-making that keeps ambition small, and the practical habits he offers instead: thinking in solutions, building confidence through action, engineering your environment, and using goals as a long-range compass. It closes with a candid look at where a book written in 1959 needs to be read with today's eyes.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຫຼັກຂອງ David J. Schwartz ແມ່ນງ່າຍຢ່າງໜ້າແປກໃຈ: ທ່ານແທບຈະບໍ່ບັນລຸຫຼາຍກວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຂະໜາດຂອງການຄິດຂອງທ່ານ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນຂະໜາດຂອງພອນສະຫວັນ, ຊັບພະຍາກອນເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ ຫຼື ຄວາມໂຊກດີ — ຄືສິ່ງທຳອິດທີ່ຄວນລົງມືປັບປຸງ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະພາທ່ານຜ່ານການວິນິດໄສ "excusitis" ຂອງ Schwartz ຊຶ່ງເປັນພະຍາດແຫ່ງການອ້າງເຫດຜົນທີ່ຮັກສາຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານໃຫ້ນ້ອຍ ແລະ ນິໄສທີ່ລາວສະເໜີແທນ — ຄິດແບບຫາທາງແກ້, ສ້າງຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈຜ່ານການລົງມືເຮັດ, ອອກແບບສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມຂອງທ່ານ ແລະ ໃຊ້ເປົ້າໝາຍເປັນເຂັມທິດໄລຍະຍາວ. ບົດຮຽນປິດທ້າຍດ້ວຍການເບິ່ງຢ່າງກົງໄປກົງມາວ່າ ປຶ້ມທີ່ຂຽນໃນປີ 1959 ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍສາຍຕາຂອງຍຸກປັດຈຸບັນຢ່າງໃດ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The core claim: belief sets the ceiling$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz opens with a claim almost too simple to notice on first read: how much you believe you can accomplish is the actual determinant of how much you attempt, and how much you attempt is the actual determinant of how much you achieve. Not raw ability first, not resources first, not luck first — belief first. He is careful to separate this from wishful thinking. Belief, in his usage, is not a warm feeling you generate and then passively wait to see confirmed. It is a working assumption you adopt on purpose because it changes the range of actions you are willing to consider trying.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The mechanism is more mundane than mystical. People do not attempt what they have already decided is beyond them, which means the attempt gets filtered out before it can even be tested — the "failure" never happens because the try never happens, so the belief that blocked it is never actually disproven, only reinforced by the absence of evidence against it. Raise the belief even slightly, Schwartz argues, and a wider set of actions becomes available to try, which is exactly why he treats belief as a lever to pull deliberately rather than a pleasant attitude to hope for.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Belief ceiling$$, 'body', $$The idea that the size of what you accomplish is capped by the size of what you believe is possible for you — so the ceiling is raised by deliberately expanding belief first, not by waiting for evidence to expand it for you.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This reframes the practical question from "how do I get better results" to "how do I believe bigger before I have proof I should" — a genuinely uncomfortable instruction, since it asks for belief in advance of evidence rather than after it. Most of the rest of the book is an argument for why that discomfort is worth tolerating, plus a set of concrete habits for tolerating it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$You rarely accomplish more than you believe is possible, so the practical first move is raising the belief, not waiting to earn it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Excusitis: the disease of habitual excuse-making$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz names the biggest obstacle to bigger thinking not as stupidity or bad luck but as a habit he calls excusitis — the chronic, often unconscious manufacture of a legitimate-sounding reason not to try, not to persist, or not to succeed. He deliberately frames it as a disease rather than a character flaw: something a person can catch without noticing, something that can run for years unexamined, and something that needs an ongoing, active cure rather than a single moment of willpower.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Excusitis$$, 'body', $$Schwartz's term for the habitual manufacturing of excuses that let a person avoid the discomfort of trying, without ever directly admitting that avoidance is the actual goal.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Health excusitis.$$, 'body', $$Leaning on a real or exaggerated health complaint to explain a stalled ambition. The tell, Schwartz notes, is that the same complaint rarely stops the person from doing what they genuinely want to do — it only shows up in front of the things they would rather avoid.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Intelligence excusitis.$$, 'body', $$Treating "I'm not smart enough" as a settled verdict, while overlooking that follow-through, judgment, and applied effort matter more day to day than raw measured intelligence — and that plenty of people rated highly intelligent underachieve for lack of exactly those.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Age excusitis.$$, 'body', $$"I'm too young" and "I'm too old" are both available excuses at almost any age simultaneously, which is itself the giveaway: age is being used as a stand-in reason rather than examined as a real constraint.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Luck excusitis.$$, 'body', $$Attributing someone else's success entirely to good fortune conveniently removes the need to examine what they actually did differently — and quietly excuses not doing it yourself.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The point is not that every self-assessment is an excuse — some constraints are entirely real, and Schwartz does not ask readers to pretend otherwise. The point is that excusitis specifically works by dressing avoidance up as realism. His practical diagnostic is simple: does the "reason" change your behavior toward improving the situation, or does it just explain away the lack of improvement? Only the second is excusitis.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Excusitis rarely feels like an excuse from the inside — it feels like an honest explanation, which is exactly what makes it worth interrogating.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Think in solutions, not obstacles$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz observes that most people's default response to a hard problem is to list the reasons it cannot be done — a habit that feels like careful thinking but actually forecloses the search before it starts. He proposes reversing the default question: instead of "why won't this work," ask "how can this be made to work." The two questions can be aimed at the identical problem and produce entirely different mental output, because one searches for confirmation of a stopping point and the other searches for a path around it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Ask how, not whether.$$, 'body', $$"Whether" invites a yes-or-no verdict that can end the thinking immediately. "How" presumes a path exists and puts your attention on finding it, which keeps the problem open rather than closed.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Treat "impossible" as under-investigated, not proven.$$, 'body', $$Schwartz's habitual move when he hears "it can't be done" is to ask what, specifically, has actually been tried — often the honest answer is "not much," which means the impossibility is an assumption wearing the costume of a conclusion.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Borrow solutions from adjacent situations.$$, 'body', $$Someone, somewhere, has usually already solved a structurally similar problem in a different context. Looking for that precedent and adapting it is faster than re-deriving a solution from first principles alone.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is not a call for blind optimism — Schwartz is not arguing every obstacle dissolves under a positive attitude. It is a call to postpone the obstacle-inventory until after the solution-search, because doing it in the reverse order quietly guarantees the search never really happens.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Listing reasons something can't be done feels like careful thinking, but it forecloses the search for a way through before it starts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Build confidence through action, not before it$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz treats confidence as a product of evidence rather than a precondition for acting, which reverses the order most people assume. Waiting to feel confident before trying gets the causality backward: confidence is manufactured by a track record of having tried, not the other way around, so a person waiting to feel ready is often waiting for something that only action itself can generate.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Act-first principle$$, 'body', $$The claim that action produces confidence more reliably than confidence produces action — so the practical move when self-doubt is loudest is to act anyway, in small ways, and let the evidence from that action do the persuading.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Destroy self-doubt-producing thoughts as they arise.$$, 'body', $$Left unchallenged, small doubts accumulate into a settled belief about your own limits. Schwartz argues for actively interrupting that pattern in the moment rather than letting it run unexamined.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Remind yourself, deliberately, that you're better than you think.$$, 'body', $$Most people carry a private assessment of themselves that is more critical than the assessment anyone else actually holds of them — a gap worth noticing and correcting on purpose rather than accepting as accurate.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Act the way you want to feel.$$, 'body', $$Enthusiasm, engagement, and confidence can be produced by behaving as though you already have them, not only felt first and expressed second — behavior leads feeling about as often as feeling leads behavior.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Confidence is built from a record of having acted, not felt first and then acted on — so act while still unsure, and let the evidence catch up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Think right toward other people$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz argues that how you regard other people quietly changes what they show you. Treating someone only as they currently present — at their most tired, most defensive, or most junior — tends to freeze the relationship at that showing, while treating them as capable of more tends to actually draw more out of them. He is explicit that this is not merely a nicety; he presents it as a practical advantage, because people respond, often unconsciously, to the level of respect and expectation directed at them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$See people as they can become, not only as they are.$$, 'body', $$A junior colleague treated as a future expert tends to grow toward that expectation faster than one treated as permanently junior — expectation functions as a quiet, ongoing input into how people see themselves.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Make genuine respect and warmth a habit, not an occasional gesture.$$, 'body', $$Remembering names and details, greeting people (including those in lower-status roles) with real attention rather than performance, compounds into a reputation that opens doors — which Schwartz frames as self-interested and generous at the same time.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Assume reasonable motives before assuming bad ones.$$, 'body', $$Defaulting to a charitable interpretation of someone's behavior keeps a relationship workable long enough to actually find out what is really going on, instead of souring it on a guess.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$People tend to grow toward the expectation you actually hold of them, so treating someone as capable is a practical lever, not just a courtesy.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Manage your environment deliberately$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz treats your physical and social surroundings as a constant, low-level suggestion running in the background of your thinking, whether or not you notice it operating. Being regularly around people who expect little — of themselves or of you — quietly lowers what feels normal to attempt, in the same way that being around people who assume bigger outcomes are routine quietly raises it. Neither effect requires anyone to say anything directly; the expectation is absorbed by proximity alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Choose your associations on purpose.$$, 'body', $$Deliberately spending time with people who think in bigger terms than you currently do — mentors, peers a step ahead — functions less like inspiration and more like a slow recalibration of what counts as a normal ambition.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Audit the conversations you're regularly part of.$$, 'body', $$A social circle that habitually discusses limitations, complaints, and reasons things don't work reinforces small thinking simply through repetition, independent of anyone's individual intent.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Shape your physical surroundings to match the scale you're aiming for.$$, 'body', $$Schwartz treats visible reminders of the target you're working toward, and the removal of clutter and cues that reinforce smallness, as a legitimate lever — not decoration, but a form of self-management.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$Environment operates as a constant, wordless suggestion about what's normal to attempt — so managing it deliberately is not optional if you're managing your thinking.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Use goals as a compass, not a to-do list$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz argues that a goal only functions as a real compass when it is set far enough out — he points readers toward something like a ten-year horizon — because a target that distant is not threatened by any single bad week, a missed opportunity, or a setback that would otherwise feel decisive. A short-term-only goal makes every stumble look like proof of failure; a long-range one gives you a fixed reference point to measure a bad stretch against, so it reads as a deviation rather than a verdict.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Write the long-range goal down in specific terms.$$, 'body', $$A vague direction is easy to quietly abandon under pressure; a written, specific target is harder to rationalize away, and gives later decisions something concrete to be checked against.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Break it into shorter checkpoints.$$, 'body', $$A ten-year target is too distant to act on directly day to day, so Schwartz has readers decompose it into nearer milestones that can actually guide this month's decisions while still pointing at the same distant target.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Revisit and revise on a schedule, not only in a crisis.$$, 'body', $$Treating the goal as fixed forever is as much a trap as never setting one — periodic, calm review keeps it honest without letting every setback trigger a full rewrite.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$A goal set far enough out survives a bad week; a goal that only reaches next month treats every setback as a verdict.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Turn setbacks and criticism into data$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz draws a sharp line between two ways of responding to failure: using it as information that tells you what to adjust, versus using it as evidence that tells you to stop. The event itself — a deal falling through, a rejected proposal — does not dictate which interpretation follows; the interpretation is a choice, made habitually enough that it starts to feel automatic rather than chosen.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He applies the same logic specifically to criticism, treating it as a mixed signal that usually contains both a genuinely useful observation and something that is simply hostile, envious, or beside the point. The practical skill he asks for is separating the two rather than reacting to the tone of the whole package — taking the useful fraction as a free correction to the plan, and declining to let the rest determine your next move.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is where big thinking and excusitis are closest to each other and easiest to confuse: both can look, from the outside, like "not giving up." The difference Schwartz draws is whether the setback actually changes what you do next. Persisting on an unchanged plan out of stubbornness is not the same as persisting on a revised plan informed by what the setback taught you — and only the second is the habit worth building.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A setback tells you almost nothing on its own — what matters is whether you use it to adjust the plan or as an excuse to stop.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Bigness is a habit of mind, not a personality trait$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz's closing thesis pulls the earlier chapters together into a single claim: "thinking big" is not a fixed trait some people are born with and others were not, but a set of habitual responses — to problems, to opportunities, and to your own self-assessment — that can be deliberately practiced until they become the default. Nothing in the book asks for a single dramatic transformation; the argument throughout is cumulative, built from many small, repeated choices about which question to ask, which explanation to accept, and which environment to keep yourself in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He is also explicit that this applies at whatever scale a reader is currently operating — the habits are the same whether the "bigness" in question is a modest promotion, a new business, or an entirely reoriented career, because the mechanism (belief shaping attempt, attempt shaping outcome) does not change with the size of the ambition, only the specific application of it does.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Big thinking is not a personality some people have — it's a set of habitual responses to problems, opportunities, and yourself, practiced until they're automatic.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Magic of Thinking Big is a work of practical persuasion, not a research study, and its confident, anecdote-driven style is exactly what makes it easy to read and exactly what should be read with some caution. Keep the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It was published in 1959, and it shows.$$, 'body', $$Many of the examples and social assumptions are dated — a near-exclusively male cast of businessmen and executives, secretaries and wives in supporting roles, and an entirely American, mid-century corporate-ladder context that does not map cleanly onto today's varied career paths, economies, or gender roles. Read the examples as period illustrations of a general mechanism, not as a template to imitate literally.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The central claim understates structural constraints.$$, 'body', $$Belief shaping what you attempt is real, but the book has comparatively little to say about capital, access, discrimination, health, and starting resources — all of which shape what is actually achievable regardless of belief. Two equally confident people starting from very different positions do not face the same odds, and the book barely engages with that gap.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Excusitis is a genuinely useful diagnostic — and a genuinely risky one.$$, 'body', $$Naming excuse-making as a habit worth interrupting is valuable. But the same framing, applied carelessly, can dismiss real, legitimate constraints as mere excuses — a standard and fair critique of positive-thinking literature generally. The diagnostic only works if it is applied honestly, not used to wave away constraints that are actually structural.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The evidence is almost entirely anecdotal.$$, 'body', $$Schwartz supports his claims with stories of named businesspeople and their turnarounds rather than controlled research or data. The stories are vivid and memorable as illustrations of a mechanism; they are much weaker as proof that the mechanism is the dominant explanation for the outcomes described.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's durable contribution survives its era: belief genuinely does gate what you attempt, excuse-making genuinely is a habit worth interrogating in yourself, and deliberately managing your thinking, your environment, and your goals genuinely is more within your control than most people act as though it is. Read it as a set of practical habits to test against your own life, not as a complete or dated-proof theory of how success works.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຫຼັກ: ຄວາມເຊື່ອກຳນົດເພດານ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Schwartz ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນດ້ວຍຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີ່ງ່າຍຈົນເກືອບບໍ່ທັນສັງເກດໃນເທື່ອທຳອິດທີ່ອ່ານ: ຄວາມເຊື່ອຂອງທ່ານວ່າຈະບັນລຸໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດ ຄືຕົວກຳນົດຕົວຈິງວ່າທ່ານຈະລອງເຮັດຫຼາຍປານໃດ ແລະ ຈຳນວນທີ່ທ່ານລອງເຮັດ ຄືຕົວກຳນົດຕົວຈິງວ່າທ່ານຈະບັນລຸໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດ. ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມສາມາດແທ້ໆກ່ອນ, ບໍ່ແມ່ນຊັບພະຍາກອນກ່ອນ, ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມໂຊກດີກ່ອນ — ແຕ່ຄວາມເຊື່ອກ່ອນ. ລາວລະມັດລະວັງທີ່ຈະແຍກແນວຄິດນີ້ອອກຈາກການຄິດຝັນເອົາເອງ. ຄວາມເຊື່ອ ຕາມທີ່ Schwartz ໃຊ້ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຮູ້ສຶກອົບອຸ່ນທີ່ທ່ານສ້າງຂຶ້ນແລ້ວລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ມັນເປັນຈິງແບບຕັ້ງຮັບ. ມັນຄືສົມມຸດຖານທີ່ທ່ານຮັບເອົາຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ເພາະມັນປ່ຽນຂອບເຂດຂອງການກະທຳທີ່ທ່ານຍອມພິຈາລະນາລອງເຮັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ກົນໄກນີ້ຮຽບງ່າຍກວ່າທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງ. ຄົນເຮົາບໍ່ລອງເຮັດສິ່ງທີ່ຕົນເອງໄດ້ຕັດສິນໃຈໄປແລ້ວວ່າເກີນຄວາມສາມາດ ຊຶ່ງໝາຍຄວາມວ່າຄວາມພະຍາຍາມນັ້ນຖືກກັ່ນຕອງອອກກ່ອນທີ່ຈະໄດ້ຮັບການທົດສອບແທ້ໆ — "ຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວ" ບໍ່ເຄີຍເກີດຂຶ້ນ ເພາະການລອງບໍ່ເຄີຍເກີດຂຶ້ນ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຄວາມເຊື່ອທີ່ຂັດຂວາງມັນ ຈຶ່ງບໍ່ເຄີຍຖືກພິສູດວ່າຜິດ ມີແຕ່ຖືກເສີມສ້າງໃຫ້ໜັກແໜ້ນຂຶ້ນຈາກການບໍ່ມີຫຼັກຖານຄັດຄ້ານ. Schwartz ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຖ້າຍົກລະດັບຄວາມເຊື່ອຂຶ້ນເລັກນ້ອຍ ຂອບເຂດຂອງການກະທຳທີ່ພ້ອມໃຫ້ລອງເຮັດກໍ່ຈະກວ້າງຂຶ້ນ ນີ້ແຫຼະຄືເຫດຜົນທີ່ລາວປະຕິບັດຕໍ່ຄວາມເຊື່ອຄືຄັນໂຍກທີ່ຕັ້ງໃຈດຶງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນທັດສະນະຄະຕິທີ່ດີງາມທີ່ພຽງແຕ່ຫວັງໃຫ້ມີ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ເພດານຄວາມເຊື່ອ (Belief ceiling)$$, 'body', $$ແນວຄິດທີ່ວ່າ ຂະໜາດຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານບັນລຸໄດ້ ຖືກຈຳກັດໂດຍຂະໜາດຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ສຳລັບຕົນເອງ — ດັ່ງນັ້ນເພດານຈະຖືກຍົກຂຶ້ນໄດ້ ດ້ວຍການຂະຫຍາຍຄວາມເຊື່ອຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈກ່ອນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ຫຼັກຖານມາຂະຫຍາຍໃຫ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ປ່ຽນຄຳຖາມທາງປະຕິບັດຈາກ "ຈະໄດ້ຜົນລັບທີ່ດີກວ່າແນວໃດ" ໄປສູ່ "ຈະເຊື່ອໃຫ້ໃຫຍ່ຂຶ້ນແນວໃດ ກ່ອນທີ່ຈະມີຫຼັກຖານພິສູດວ່າຄວນເຊື່ອ" — ຄຳສັ່ງທີ່ອຶດອັດແທ້ໆ ເພາະມັນຮຽກຮ້ອງໃຫ້ເຊື່ອກ່ອນຫຼັກຖານ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຫຼັງຈາກນັ້ນ. ສ່ວນທີ່ເຫຼືອຂອງປຶ້ມສ່ວນຫຼາຍ ຄືການໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າເປັນຫຍັງຄວາມອຶດອັດນັ້ນຈຶ່ງຄຸ້ມຄ່າທີ່ຈະທົນ ບວກກັບຊຸດນິໄສທີ່ຈັບຕ້ອງໄດ້ ສຳລັບການທົນນັ້ນ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ທ່ານແທບຈະບໍ່ບັນລຸຫຼາຍກວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຂັ້ນຕອນທຳອິດ ຄືການຍົກລະດັບຄວາມເຊື່ອ ບໍ່ແມ່ນລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ໄດ້ຮັບມັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Excusitis: ພະຍາດແຫ່ງການອ້າງເຫດຜົນຈົນເປັນນິໄສ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
