    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຂໍ້ສະຫຼຸບປິດທ້າຍຂອງ Schwartz ດຶງບົດກ່ອນໆເຂົ້າມາເປັນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງດຽວ: "ການຄິດໃຫຍ່" ບໍ່ແມ່ນລັກສະນະທີ່ຕິດຕົວ ທີ່ບາງຄົນເກີດມາມີ ແລະ ບາງຄົນບໍ່ມີ ແຕ່ຄືຊຸດການຕອບສະໜອງທີ່ເປັນນິໄສ — ຕໍ່ບັນຫາ, ຕໍ່ໂອກາດ ແລະ ຕໍ່ການປະເມີນຕົນເອງຂອງທ່ານ — ທີ່ສາມາດຝຶກຝົນຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ຈົນກາຍເປັນຄ່າເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ. ບໍ່ມີຫຍັງໃນປຶ້ມນີ້ ຮຽກຮ້ອງໃຫ້ມີການປ່ຽນແປງອັນຍິ່ງໃຫຍ່ພຽງຄັ້ງດຽວ — ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຕະຫຼອດປຶ້ມ ຄືການສະສົມ ສ້າງຂຶ້ນຈາກທາງເລືອກນ້ອຍໆທີ່ເຮັດຊ້ຳຫຼາຍຄັ້ງ ກ່ຽວກັບຄຳຖາມໃດທີ່ຈະຖາມ, ຄຳອະທິບາຍໃດທີ່ຈະຮັບເອົາ ແລະ ສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມໃດທີ່ຈະຮັກສາຕົນເອງໄວ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ລາວຍັງລະບຸຢ່າງຊັດເຈນວ່າ ນີ້ນຳໃຊ້ໄດ້ບໍ່ວ່າຜູ້ອ່ານກຳລັງດຳເນີນຢູ່ໃນຂະໜາດໃດກໍ່ຕາມ — ນິໄສເຫຼົ່ານີ້ຄືກັນ ບໍ່ວ່າ "ຄວາມໃຫຍ່" ທີ່ກ່າວເຖິງ ຄືການເລື່ອນຕຳແໜ່ງແບບພໍປານກາງ, ທຸລະກິດໃໝ່ ຫຼື ອາຊີບທີ່ປ່ຽນທິດທາງທັງໝົດ ເພາະກົນໄກ (ຄວາມເຊື່ອກຳນົດການລອງເຮັດ, ການລອງເຮັດກຳນົດຜົນລັບ) ບໍ່ປ່ຽນຕາມຂະໜາດຂອງຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານ ມີແຕ່ການນຳໃຊ້ສະເພາະຂອງມັນເທົ່ານັ້ນທີ່ປ່ຽນ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ການຄິດໃຫຍ່ບໍ່ແມ່ນບຸກຄະລິກກະພາບທີ່ບາງຄົນມີ — ມັນຄືຊຸດການຕອບສະໜອງທີ່ເປັນນິໄສ ຕໍ່ບັນຫາ, ໂອກາດ ແລະ ຕົນເອງ ທີ່ຝຶກຈົນເປັນອັດຕະໂນມັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ວິທີອ່ານປຶ້ມຫົວນີ້$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Magic of Thinking Big ຄືວຽກງານແຫ່ງການໂນ້ມນ້າວທາງປະຕິບັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນການສຶກສາຄົ້ນຄວ້າ ແລະ ຮູບແບບການຂຽນທີ່ໜັກແໜ້ນ ອີງໃສ່ເລື່ອງລາວນັ້ນແຫຼະ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ມັນອ່ານງ່າຍ ແລະ ພ້ອມກັນນັ້ນ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍຄວາມລະມັດລະວັງ. ໃຫ້ຄຳນຶງເຖິງສິ່ງຕໍ່ໄປນີ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ມັນຖືກພິມອອກໃນປີ 1959 ແລະ ມັນສະແດງອອກຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ.$$, 'body', $$ຕົວຢ່າງ ແລະ ສົມມຸດຖານທາງສັງຄົມຫຼາຍຢ່າງລ້າສະໄໝ — ຕົວລະຄອນສ່ວນຫຼາຍເປັນນັກທຸລະກິດ ແລະ ຜູ້ບໍລິຫານຜູ້ຊາຍເກືອບທັງໝົດ, ເລຂາ ແລະ ພັນລະຍາໃນບົດບາດສະໜັບສະໜູນ ແລະ ບໍລິບົດຄືອາເມຣິກາລ້ວນໆໃນຍຸກກາງສະຕະວັດ ຂອງການໄຕ່ບັນໄດອາຊີບໃນອົງກອນ ຊຶ່ງບໍ່ສອດຄ່ອງກັບເສັ້ນທາງອາຊີບ, ເສດຖະກິດ ຫຼື ບົດບາດຍິງຊາຍທີ່ຫຼາກຫຼາຍໃນປັດຈຸບັນ. ອ່ານຕົວຢ່າງເຫຼົ່ານີ້ ເປັນຮູບປະກອບຂອງກົນໄກທົ່ວໄປໃນຍຸກນັ້ນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນແບບຢ່າງໃຫ້ຮຽນແບບຕາມຕົວອັກສອນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຫຼັກ ຫຼຸດຄ່າຂໍ້ຈຳກັດທາງໂຄງສ້າງ.$$, 'body', $$ຄວາມເຊື່ອກຳນົດສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານລອງເຮັດ ແມ່ນຈິງ ແຕ່ປຶ້ມນີ້ເວົ້າໜ້ອຍຫຼາຍກ່ຽວກັບທຶນ, ການເຂົ້າເຖິງ, ການຈຳແນກ, ສຸຂະພາບ ແລະ ຊັບພະຍາກອນເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ — ທັງໝົດນີ້ກຳນົດສິ່ງທີ່ບັນລຸໄດ້ຈິງ ບໍ່ວ່າຄວາມເຊື່ອຈະເປັນແນວໃດ. ຄົນສອງຄົນທີ່ໝັ້ນໃຈເທົ່າກັນ ແຕ່ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນຈາກຕຳແໜ່ງທີ່ແຕກຕ່າງກັນຫຼາຍ ບໍ່ໄດ້ພົບກັບໂອກາດເທົ່າກັນ ແລະ ປຶ້ມນີ້ແທບບໍ່ໄດ້ພົວພັນກັບຊ່ອງຫວ່າງນັ້ນເລີຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Excusitis ຄືເຄື່ອງມືວິນິດໄສທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດແທ້ໆ — ແລະ ມີຄວາມສ່ຽງແທ້ໆ.$$, 'body', $$ການຕັ້ງຊື່ການສ້າງຂໍ້ອ້າງວ່າເປັນນິໄສທີ່ຄວນຢຸດ ມີຄຸນຄ່າ. ແຕ່ກອບຄິດດຽວກັນນີ້ ຖ້ານຳໃຊ້ໂດຍບໍ່ລະມັດລະວັງ ສາມາດປະຕິເສດຂໍ້ຈຳກັດທີ່ແທ້ຈິງ ແລະ ຖືກຕ້ອງຕາມກົດໝາຍ ວ່າເປັນພຽງຂໍ້ອ້າງ — ນີ້ຄືການວິພາກວິຈານມາດຕະຖານ ແລະ ຍຸດຕິທຳ ຕໍ່ວັນນະກຳຄິດບວກໂດຍທົ່ວໄປ. ເຄື່ອງມືວິນິດໄສນີ້ ໃຊ້ໄດ້ຜົນ ກໍ່ຕໍ່ເມື່ອນຳໃຊ້ຢ່າງຊື່ສັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນໃຊ້ເພື່ອປັດຂໍ້ຈຳກັດທີ່ແທ້ຈິງເປັນໂຄງສ້າງອອກໄປ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຫຼັກຖານແມ່ນເລື່ອງເລົ່າເກືອບທັງໝົດ.$$, 'body', $$Schwartz ສະໜັບສະໜູນຄຳກ່າວອ້າງຂອງລາວ ດ້ວຍເລື່ອງລາວຂອງນັກທຸລະກິດທີ່ມີຊື່ ແລະ ການພິກຟື້ນຂອງພວກເຂົາ ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນການຄົ້ນຄວ້າ ຫຼື ຂໍ້ມູນທີ່ຄວບຄຸມໄດ້. ເລື່ອງເລົ່າເຫຼົ່ານີ້ ຈື່ຈຳໄດ້ ແລະ ຊັດເຈນ ໃນຖານະຕົວຢ່າງຂອງກົນໄກ ແຕ່ອ່ອນແອກວ່າຫຼາຍ ໃນຖານະຫຼັກຖານພິສູດວ່າ ກົນໄກນັ້ນຄືຄຳອະທິບາຍຫຼັກ ຂອງຜົນລັບທີ່ບັນຍາຍໄວ້.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເມື່ອຖືເອົາຂໍ້ລະມັດລະວັງເຫຼົ່ານັ້ນໄວ້ ຄຸນປະໂຫຍດທີ່ຍືນຍົງຂອງປຶ້ມນີ້ ຍັງຄົງຢູ່ຂ້າມຍຸກສະໄໝ: ຄວາມເຊື່ອກຳນົດສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານລອງເຮັດແທ້ໆ, ການສ້າງຂໍ້ອ້າງແທ້ຈິງແມ່ນນິໄສທີ່ຄວນຕັ້ງຄຳຖາມກັບຕົນເອງ ແລະ ການຈັດການການຄິດ, ສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມ ແລະ ເປົ້າໝາຍຂອງທ່ານຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ຢູ່ພາຍໃນການຄວບຄຸມຂອງທ່ານຫຼາຍກວ່າທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ປະຕິບັດຄືວ່າມັນເປັນ. ອ່ານມັນເປັນຊຸດນິໄສທາງປະຕິບັດເພື່ອທົດສອບກັບຊີວິດຂອງທ່ານເອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນທິດສະດີທີ່ຄົບຖ້ວນ ຫຼື ບໍ່ລ້າສະໄໝ ຂອງວິທີທີ່ຄວາມສຳເລັດເກີດຂຶ້ນ.$$)
  ))
  ),
  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Belief ceiling$$, 'body', $$The idea that the size of your accomplishment is capped by the size of what you believe possible for yourself, so raising the belief deliberately is the first practical move.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Excusitis$$, 'body', $$Schwartz's term for the chronic, habitual manufacture of legitimate-sounding excuses that let a person avoid the discomfort of trying.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The four excusitis strains$$, 'body', $$Health, intelligence, age, and luck excusitis — the most common categories Schwartz identifies for dressing up avoidance as a reasonable explanation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$How-to-do-it thinking$$, 'body', $$The habit of asking "how can this be done" instead of listing reasons something can't, which keeps the search for a solution open rather than closing it prematurely.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Act-first principle$$, 'body', $$The claim that action produces confidence more reliably than confidence produces action, so the practical move under self-doubt is to act anyway in small ways.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Long-range goal (ten-year horizon)$$, 'body', $$A goal set far enough into the future that no single setback threatens it, giving you a fixed reference point against which a bad stretch reads as a deviation, not a verdict.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Setback-as-data$$, 'body', $$Treating a failure or piece of criticism as information about what to adjust in the plan, rather than as evidence that tells you to stop.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ເພດານຄວາມເຊື່ອ (Belief ceiling)$$, 'body', $$ແນວຄິດທີ່ວ່າ ຂະໜາດຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານບັນລຸໄດ້ ຖືກຈຳກັດໂດຍຂະໜາດຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ ດັ່ງນັ້ນການຍົກລະດັບຄວາມເຊື່ອຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ຄືການເຄື່ອນໄຫວທາງປະຕິບັດອັນທຳອິດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Excusitis$$, 'body', $$ຄຳສັບຂອງ Schwartz ສຳລັບການສ້າງຂໍ້ອ້າງທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງມີເຫດຜົນ ຈົນເປັນນິໄສ ເພື່ອໃຫ້ຄົນຫຼີກລ້ຽງຄວາມອຶດອັດຂອງການລອງເຮັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ສີ່ຮູບແບບຂອງ excusitis$$, 'body', $$Health, intelligence, age ແລະ luck excusitis — ໝວດໝູ່ທົ່ວໄປທີ່ສຸດ ທີ່ Schwartz ລະບຸ ສຳລັບການແຕ່ງໜ້າການຫຼີກລ້ຽງໃຫ້ເປັນຄຳອະທິບາຍທີ່ສົມເຫດສົມຜົນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການຄິດແບບຫາທາງແກ້$$, 'body', $$ນິໄສການຖາມ "ຈະເຮັດແນວໃດໃຫ້ໄດ້" ແທນທີ່ຈະລິດເຫດຜົນວ່າເປັນຫຍັງເຮັດບໍ່ໄດ້ ຊຶ່ງຮັກສາການຄົ້ນຫາທາງແກ້ໃຫ້ເປີດຢູ່ ບໍ່ແມ່ນປິດມັນກ່ອນເວລາອັນຄວນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຫຼັກການລົງມືເຮັດກ່ອນ (Act-first principle)$$, 'body', $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີ່ວ່າ ການລົງມືເຮັດ ສ້າງຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈໄດ້ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖືກວ່າ ຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈສ້າງການລົງມືເຮັດ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຂັ້ນຕອນປະຕິບັດ ເມື່ອຄວາມສົງໄສໃນຕົນເອງດັງທີ່ສຸດ ຄືການລົງມືເຮັດຢ່າງໃດກໍ່ຕາມ ໃນຮູບແບບນ້ອຍໆ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍໄລຍະຍາວ (ຂອບເຂດ 10 ປີ)$$, 'body', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຕັ້ງໄວ້ໄກພຽງພໍໃນອະນາຄົດ ຈົນບໍ່ມີຄວາມພ່າຍແພ້ຄັ້ງດຽວທີ່ຄຸກຄາມມັນໄດ້ ໃຫ້ຈຸດອ້າງອີງທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ ໃຫ້ວັດແທກຊ່ວງເວລາທີ່ບໍ່ດີ ວ່າເປັນການບ່ຽງເບນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄຳຕັດສິນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຄວາມພ່າຍແພ້ໃນຖານະຂໍ້ມູນ$$, 'body', $$ການຖືເອົາຄວາມລົ້ມເຫຼວ ຫຼື ຄຳວິຈານ ເປັນຂໍ້ມູນກ່ຽວກັບສິ່ງທີ່ຄວນປັບໃນແຜນການ ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນຫຼັກຖານທີ່ບອກໃຫ້ຢຸດ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Think of an ambition you quietly scaled down. Was the limit actually your ability, or was it what you believed was possible for someone like you?$$, $$Which of your own current explanations for not doing something might be excusitis — a reasonable-sounding excuse — rather than a genuine, structural constraint? How would you tell the difference honestly?$$, $$Schwartz treats belief as the primary lever on achievement. Where does that claim understate the role of money, access, discrimination, or starting resources in what people can actually accomplish?$$, $$Who in your current environment quietly lowers what feels like a normal ambition to you, and who raises it — and how much of that is deliberate on your part versus just where you happen to be?$$, $$Recall a piece of criticism you received recently. What was the useful fraction worth acting on, and what was the part worth setting aside — and how did you actually decide which was which?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ຄິດເຖິງຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານໜຶ່ງທີ່ທ່ານຫຼຸດຂະໜາດລົງຢ່າງງຽບໆ. ຂອບເຂດນັ້ນແມ່ນຄວາມສາມາດຂອງທ່ານແທ້ໆ ຫຼື ແມ່ນສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ສຳລັບຄົນແບບທ່ານ?$$, $$ຄຳອະທິບາຍປັດຈຸບັນຂອງທ່ານເອງ ສຳລັບການບໍ່ເຮັດບາງສິ່ງ ອັນໃດອາດແມ່ນ excusitis — ຂໍ້ອ້າງທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງມີເຫດຜົນ — ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນຂໍ້ຈຳກັດທາງໂຄງສ້າງທີ່ແທ້ຈິງ? ທ່ານຈະແຍກແຍະຢ່າງຊື່ສັດແນວໃດ?$$, $$Schwartz ຖືວ່າ ຄວາມເຊື່ອຄືຄັນໂຍກຫຼັກຕໍ່ຄວາມສຳເລັດ. ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງນັ້ນ ຫຼຸດຄ່າບົດບາດຂອງເງິນ, ການເຂົ້າເຖິງ, ການຈຳແນກ ຫຼື ຊັບພະຍາກອນເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ ໃນສິ່ງທີ່ຄົນເຮົາບັນລຸໄດ້ຈິງ ຢູ່ບ່ອນໃດ?$$, $$ໃນສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມປັດຈຸບັນຂອງທ່ານ ໃຜທີ່ຄ່ອຍໆຫຼຸດສິ່ງທີ່ຮູ້ສຶກເປັນຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານປົກກະຕິ ແລະ ໃຜທີ່ຍົກລະດັບມັນ — ແລະ ນັ້ນເປັນສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານຕັ້ງໃຈເລືອກຫຼາຍປານໃດ ທຽບກັບພຽງແຕ່ບັງເອີນຢູ່ບ່ອນນັ້ນ?$$, $$ຄິດເຖິງຄຳວິຈານທີ່ທ່ານໄດ້ຮັບບໍ່ດົນມານີ້. ສ່ວນໃດທີ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດຄວນຄ່າແກ່ການລົງມືເຮັດ ແລະ ສ່ວນໃດຄວນປ່ອຍໄວ້ — ແລະ ທ່ານຕັດສິນໃຈແທ້ໆແນວໃດວ່າອັນໃດເປັນອັນໃດ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$You rarely accomplish more than you believe is possible — belief sets the practical ceiling on what you even attempt.$$, $$Excusitis is the habitual, often unconscious manufacture of legitimate-sounding reasons not to try, persist, or succeed.$$, $$The four common strains are health, intelligence, age, and luck excusitis — each dresses up avoidance as realism.$$, $$Ask "how can this be done" instead of listing reasons it can't — the question you ask determines whether you keep searching.$$, $$Confidence is built by acting, not felt first and then acted on — act while still unsure and let evidence catch up.$$, $$How you regard other people changes what they show you — treating people as capable of more tends to draw more out of them.$$, $$Environment is a constant, wordless suggestion about what's normal to attempt, so choose your surroundings deliberately.$$, $$A goal set on a long horizon, like a ten-year plan, survives a bad week; a short-term-only goal treats every setback as a verdict.$$, $$A setback is data, not a verdict — the habit worth building is adjusting the plan, not repeating it unchanged or quitting.$$, $$Big thinking is a set of practiced habits of mind applied to problems, opportunities, and yourself — not a fixed personality trait.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ທ່ານແທບຈະບໍ່ບັນລຸຫຼາຍກວ່າສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າເປັນໄປໄດ້ — ຄວາມເຊື່ອກຳນົດເພດານທາງປະຕິບັດຂອງແມ່ນແຕ່ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານກ້າລອງ.$$, $$Excusitis ຄືການສ້າງເຫດຜົນທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງມີເຫດຜົນຈົນເປັນນິໄສ ແລະ ມັກບໍ່ຮູ້ໂຕ ເພື່ອບໍ່ຕ້ອງລອງ, ອົດທົນ ຫຼື ປະສົບຄວາມສຳເລັດ.$$, $$ສີ່ຮູບແບບຫຼັກຄື excusitis ດ້ານສຸຂະພາບ, ສະຕິປັນຍາ, ອາຍຸ ແລະ ໂຊກ — ແຕ່ລະອັນແຕ່ງໜ້າການຫຼີກລ້ຽງໃຫ້ເປັນຄວາມເປັນຈິງ.$$, $$ຖາມ "ຈະເຮັດແນວໃດໃຫ້ໄດ້" ແທນທີ່ຈະລິດເຫດຜົນວ່າເປັນຫຍັງເຮັດບໍ່ໄດ້ — ຄຳຖາມທີ່ທ່ານຖາມ ກຳນົດວ່າ ທ່ານຈະຄົ້ນຫາຕໍ່ໄປບໍ.$$, $$ຄວາມໝັ້ນໃຈຖືກສ້າງຈາກການລົງມືເຮັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຮູ້ສຶກກ່ອນແລ້ວຈຶ່ງເຮັດ — ໃຫ້ລົງມືເຮັດຂະນະທີ່ຍັງບໍ່ໝັ້ນໃຈ ແລ້ວປ່ອຍໃຫ້ຫຼັກຖານຕາມທັນ.$$, $$ວິທີທີ່ທ່ານມອງຄົນອື່ນ ປ່ຽນສິ່ງທີ່ພວກເຂົາສະແດງອອກໃຫ້ທ່ານເຫັນ — ການປະຕິບັດຕໍ່ຄົນວ່າມີຄວາມສາມາດຫຼາຍກວ່າ ມັກດຶງເອົາສິ່ງນັ້ນອອກມາຈິງ.$$, $$ສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມ ຄືການແນະນຳແບບງຽບໆ ແລະ ຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ ວ່າຫຍັງຄືປົກກະຕິທີ່ຈະລອງເຮັດ ດັ່ງນັ້ນ ໃຫ້ເລືອກສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມຂອງທ່ານຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ.$$, $$ເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຕັ້ງໄວ້ໄກ ເຊັ່ນແຜນ 10 ປີ ຢູ່ລອດຈາກອາທິດທີ່ບໍ່ດີ; ເປົ້າໝາຍໄລຍະສັ້ນລ້ວນໆ ຖືທຸກຄວາມພ່າຍແພ້ເປັນຄຳຕັດສິນ.$$, $$ຄວາມພ່າຍແພ້ຄືຂໍ້ມູນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄຳຕັດສິນ — ນິໄສທີ່ຄວນສ້າງ ຄືການປັບແຜນການ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເຮັດຊ້ຳໂດຍບໍ່ປ່ຽນ ຫຼື ຢຸດ.$$, $$ການຄິດໃຫຍ່ ຄືຊຸດນິໄສທາງຄວາມຄິດທີ່ຝຶກຝົນ ນຳໃຊ້ກັບບັນຫາ, ໂອກາດ ແລະ ຕົນເອງ — ບໍ່ແມ່ນລັກສະນະນິໄສທີ່ຕິດຕົວ.$$]
where book_id = 'c8fcdbfc-9efa-4946-af8a-dbbc0a12cbcb';

-- Elon Musk — Walter Isaacson
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 18,
  summary_en = $$A biography tracing Musk's path from early internet startups to reshaping cars and rockets.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ຊີວະປະຫວັດທີ່ຕິດຕາມເສັ້ນທາງຂອງ Musk ຈາກສະຕາດອັບອິນເຕີເນັດ ຫາການປ່ຽນວົງການລົດ ແລະ ຈະຫຼວດ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Ashlee Vance's biography follows Elon Musk from a difficult childhood in South Africa through Zip2 and PayPal, and into the two ventures that made him globally famous: SpaceX and Tesla. Built on unusually close access to Musk and the people around him, the book is at its strongest describing how a young internet fortune was gambled, nearly lost, and eventually multiplied inside two of the most capital-hungry, failure-prone industries in existence. This lesson works through that arc — the childhood, the early companies, the near-collapse of 2008, Musk's first-principles thinking, and his demanding management style — and closes with a candid look at what the book, written in 2015, does and does not capture about Musk today.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຊີວະປະຫວັດຂອງ Ashlee Vance ຕິດຕາມ Elon Musk ຈາກໄວເດັກທີ່ຫຍຸ້ງຍາກໃນອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ ຜ່ານ Zip2 ແລະ PayPal ໄປສູ່ສອງທຸລະກິດທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ລາວມີຊື່ສຽງໄປທົ່ວໂລກ: SpaceX ແລະ Tesla. ສ້າງຂຶ້ນຈາກການເຂົ້າເຖິງ Musk ແລະ ຄົນອ້ອມຂ້າງລາວຢ່າງໃກ້ຊິດຜິດປົກກະຕິ ປຶ້ມຫົວນີ້ເກັ່ງທີ່ສຸດໃນການບອກເລົ່າວ່າ ໂຊກລາບຈາກອິນເຕີເນັດຕອນຫນຸ່ມຖືກເອົາໄປສ່ຽງ, ເກືອບເສຍໝົດ ແລະ ໃນທີ່ສຸດເພີ່ມທະວີຄູນ ໃນສອງວົງການທີ່ຕ້ອງການທຶນຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດ ແລະ ມີອັດຕາລົ້ມເຫຼວສູງທີ່ສຸດອັນໜຶ່ງ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະໄລ່ຕາມເສັ້ນທາງນັ້ນ — ໄວເດັກ, ບໍລິສັດຍຸກຕົ້ນ, ການເກືອບລົ້ມລະລາຍປີ 2008, ການຄິດແບບຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານຂອງ Musk ແລະ ແບບການບໍລິຫານທີ່ຮຽກຮ້ອງສູງຂອງລາວ — ແລ້ວປິດທ້າຍດ້ວຍການເບິ່ງຢ່າງກົງໄປກົງມາວ່າ ປຶ້ມທີ່ຂຽນໃນປີ 2015 ນີ້ ບອກຫຍັງແດ່ ແລະ ບໍ່ໄດ້ບອກຫຍັງແດ່ ກ່ຽວກັບ Musk ໃນປັດຈຸບັນ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$A childhood shaped by isolation and books$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance opens the biography in Pretoria, South Africa, where Musk grew up in a household marked by his parents' divorce and a strained, at times harshly critical relationship with his father, Errol. School offered little refuge either — Vance recounts an environment where Musk was reportedly bullied badly enough, on at least one occasion, to require hospital treatment. The overall picture is of a bright, socially awkward child who did not fit comfortably into his surroundings.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$What filled the gap, in Vance's telling, was reading. Musk reportedly worked through encyclopedias and consumed large amounts of science fiction, including the kind of sweeping, civilization-scale stories in which a single person or a small institution can alter the fate of an entire society. Vance treats this habit as more than a charming childhood detail — the scale of ambition Musk would later bring to real companies mirrors the scale of the stories he grew up immersed in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The chapter's throughline is that self-reliance was less a personality trait Musk chose than a skill an unprotected childhood forced him to develop early, well before there was any company to apply it to.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Long before he built anything, a difficult childhood and an outsized reading habit taught Musk both self-reliance and a taste for civilization-scale ambition.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Leaving South Africa: Canada, Penn, and a deliberate route out$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$As a teenager, Musk set his sights on leaving South Africa, and Vance describes a specific, engineered route: first to Canada, using family connections to establish residency there, then a string of unglamorous jobs to support himself while he worked his way toward university and, eventually, toward the United States.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He went on to study at the University of Pennsylvania, pursuing both physics and business — a combination Vance frames as an early signal of the pattern that would define Musk's career: pairing technical fluency with business execution rather than settling into just one of the two.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Even his path out of South Africa was engineered like one of his later companies — a deliberate, multi-step route toward where he actually wanted to end up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Stanford for two days: choosing the internet over a PhD$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$After Penn, Musk was accepted into a graduate program at Stanford focused on materials science and energy storage. Vance's account of what happened next is one of the book's sharper anecdotes: Musk arrived in California in the mid-1990s just as the commercial internet was taking off, and concluded within days that the opportunity in front of him could not wait for a doctorate.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He withdrew from the program almost immediately and, with his brother Kimbal, set out to build a company instead. Vance frames the decision less as recklessness than as an early demonstration of a trait that recurs throughout the book: Musk's willingness to abandon a safe, credentialed path the moment he judged the timing of an opportunity to be more urgent than the credential itself.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Musk did not leave academia out of impatience alone — he made a timing bet on the internet, and judged the PhD to be the wrong use of a narrow window.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Zip2 — the first company, the first fortune$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Musk and Kimbal built Zip2 as an early online city-guide and business-directory service — in effect helping newspapers put local listings and maps online at a time when most media companies had no internet strategy of their own.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Zip2$$, 'body', $$An early web company that produced online city guides and business directories for newspaper publishers, later sold for a substantial sum — Musk's first real infusion of capital.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The company sold years later for tens of millions of dollars. Musk had already been eased out of the CEO role before the sale, but the payout still gave him, for the first time, real money of his own — capital he treated less as a reward to bank than as fuel for a far riskier next venture.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Zip2 mattered less for what it built than for what it funded next.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$X.com, PayPal, and the eBay sale$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Musk put much of his Zip2 money into a new venture, X.com, with ambitions that went beyond simple online payments — Musk imagined it eventually growing into a full online financial-services hub.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$PayPal (X.com)$$, 'body', $$X.com merged with rival startup Confinity; the combined company was later renamed PayPal after an internal power struggle, and it was sold to eBay in a deal that made Musk, still a major shareholder, considerably wealthier.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$X.com merged with Confinity, and the combined company was eventually renamed PayPal after Musk's more expansive banking vision lost out internally. Musk himself was ousted as CEO during a period of boardroom conflict, an episode Vance treats as an early lesson in how cofounders and investors could turn against him even as the company he helped start kept climbing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$PayPal's sale to eBay left Musk — despite the ouster, still a large shareholder — with the fortune that would fund the rest of the book's story.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Being pushed out of the company he helped build did not stop Musk from profiting handsomely from it, or from immediately hunting for the next, bigger bet.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$First-principles thinking — Musk's core mental model$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A recurring idea running through Vance's account of Musk's decision-making is what Musk calls reasoning from first principles: instead of accepting an industry's conventional wisdom about what something must cost or how it must be built, break the problem down to underlying physics and raw material cost, then reason back up from there.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$First-principles thinking$$, 'body', $$An approach that strips a problem down to fundamental physical and material facts rather than reasoning by analogy from how an industry has always operated or what things have always cost.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance illustrates the approach with Musk's well-known rocket example: the raw commodity materials that make up a rocket amount to a small fraction of what a finished, assembled rocket has historically cost to buy. To Musk, that gap was not simply a fact of nature — it was evidence of inefficiency, bureaucracy, and assumptions baked into an industry that had gone largely unchallenged for decades. Vance shows the same logic shaping Musk's approach to electric vehicle batteries at Tesla: rather than accept prevailing assumptions about what a battery pack must cost, break it down to its raw material inputs and ask why the finished price sits so far above that floor.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Musk's signature move is refusing to accept "that is just what it costs" as an answer, and asking what the raw materials actually cost instead.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Betting a fortune on two of the hardest industries alive$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Most people who make a fortune from an internet company either start another internet company or settle into a comfortable life. Vance treats what Musk did next as the defining decision of his career: he put the bulk of his personal wealth into two industries famous for enormous capital requirements, high failure rates, and deep hostility to new entrants — rockets and cars.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Multiplanetary species$$, 'body', $$SpaceX's stated long-run purpose beyond simply launching payloads: spreading human civilization across more than one planet as a hedge against risks that could otherwise end it on Earth alone.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$SpaceX was founded with the explicit near-term goal of reducing the cost of reaching orbit, framed by Musk within a far larger purpose than a launch-services business: making humanity a multiplanetary species. Around the same period, Musk invested in and gradually took an increasingly hands-on leadership role at Tesla, then a small startup trying to prove an electric car could be genuinely desirable rather than merely dutiful.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance is careful to note how strange this looked at the time: aerospace and automobile manufacturing had not seen a wave of successful new entrants in decades, precisely because the capital requirements and failure rates scared off nearly everyone with the money to attempt it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Instead of protecting his winnings, Musk moved them straight into two of the industries best known for destroying fortunes.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$2008 — the year both companies nearly died at once$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The book's most tense stretch covers 2008, when SpaceX and Tesla both came close to collapse in the same year, funded out of a personal fortune that was visibly running out.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Reusable rockets$$, 'body', $$A long-run SpaceX goal, recovering and reflying rocket stages instead of discarding them after a single launch, aimed at cutting the cost of reaching orbit — part of the larger bet the company's survival in 2008 was protecting.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$SpaceX's early Falcon 1 rocket had failed on three consecutive launch attempts, each failure burning through cash and credibility. Vance describes the fourth attempt as, in effect, the company's last realistic shot — another failure would very likely have ended SpaceX outright. At the same time, Tesla was struggling to get its first car, the Roadster, into reliable production, burning cash rapidly during a global financial crisis that made new investment far harder to raise.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance recounts Musk personally dividing his remaining money between the two companies during a period of severe personal strain, including a marriage ending around the same time. The fourth Falcon 1 launch succeeded, helping SpaceX secure a significant NASA contract, and Tesla closed a last-minute financing round that, by some accounts, left very little runway to spare.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$In 2008, Musk was one more failure away from losing both companies in the same year, and he kept both alive with what was left of his own money.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$A demanding, punishing, and sometimes inspiring boss$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance's portrait of Musk as a manager is deliberately two-sided. Employees describe extremely long hours as the baseline expectation, blunt and sometimes harsh criticism, and a leader who would personally challenge technical decisions down to a granular engineering level rather than delegate and simply wait for results.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Vertical integration$$, 'body', $$Musk's preference for building and controlling as much of the manufacturing process in-house as possible, rather than outsourcing it — part of the same hands-on instinct that shapes his management style.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Some employees describe the experience as the most demanding and most meaningful work of their careers, pulled forward by a mission that felt larger than an ordinary job. Others describe it as punishing, pointing to burnout, high turnover, and a culture where falling short of Musk's expectations could mean an abrupt, public exit. Vance does not resolve the tension between these accounts — he presents both and lets the contradiction stand as part of an honest picture of what building at this scale, in Musk's version of leadership, actually costs.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Vance's Musk is not simply visionary or simply harsh — credible sources describe him as both, often within the same week.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Mission over profit$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Musk frames his companies' purposes in terms that go well beyond ordinary profit-seeking. Tesla, together with Musk's involvement in SolarCity, is framed around accelerating the world's transition to sustainable energy; SpaceX is framed around making humanity multiplanetary.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Sustainable energy transition$$, 'body', $$The mission Musk assigns to Tesla and SolarCity: accelerating the shift away from fossil fuels toward electric transport and renewable power, framed as an urgent civilizational goal rather than a conventional business objective.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance treats this mission language as more than marketing. It functions as a recruiting tool that lets Musk ask employees to accept lower pay, longer hours, and higher personal risk than a comparable job elsewhere would require, on the argument that the work matters on a civilizational timescale rather than a quarterly one. Whether that framing reflects sincere belief, effective strategy, or some blend of both is a question Vance raises without fully settling.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Calling the work a mission rather than a job is also, functionally, how Musk gets people to tolerate demands an ordinary job never could.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$An outlier case study in ambition and risk$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Stepping back, Vance situates Musk as a genuinely unusual figure even among successful entrepreneurs: someone willing to put an entire personal fortune at risk twice, simultaneously, in industries structurally hostile to new entrants, while personally embedding himself in technical details most chief executives leave to others.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The book resists reducing this to a simple hero narrative. Vance's Musk is driven by something that reads, at points, like restlessness or even compulsion as much as calculation, and the same intensity that produced extraordinary results also produced real costs to the people and relationships around him.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$What makes Musk unusual, in Vance's telling, is not any single decision but the sheer consistency of extreme risk tolerance across every major decision of his career.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Published in 2015, Vance's biography is a rich, detailed early account, but it should be read as exactly that — early. It follows Musk through the SpaceX and Tesla survival years and into their first real successes, but it predates a large share of what many readers now associate with Musk's public life, including his ownership of X (formerly Twitter), his political involvement, and a range of later controversies at Tesla and elsewhere. Treat it as a portrait of a founder in an earlier, more narrowly technical-and-entrepreneurial phase of his story, not a complete or current account.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Vance had significant direct access.$$, 'body', $$Musk and people close to him cooperated with the reporting, which produced rich, specific detail unavailable to outside biographers — but access of that kind also tends to shape a portrayal, and readers should weigh the book's more sympathetic passages with that in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The management culture the book documents deserves scrutiny, not just admiration.$$, 'body', $$Vance reports the harsh, all-consuming work culture at Musk's companies fairly critically, but the book's narrative momentum — building toward triumph after each crisis — can still read as glamorizing the intensity even while it is describing real human cost to employees.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Musk's story is an extreme outlier, not a template.$$, 'body', $$The specific combination the book documents — a personal fortune from an earlier win, an unusual tolerance for total loss, and entry into industries that punish undercapitalized competitors — is not a formula available to most founders. Read it as one extraordinary case, not a repeatable playbook.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It is a narrower story than "everything about Musk."$$, 'body', $$Even within its own timeframe, the book centers on SpaceX and Tesla as the main narrative engines; other ventures and aspects of Musk's personal life receive comparatively less depth.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read with those limits in mind, the book still does something few others have managed since: it documents, in close and credible detail, what it actually looked like inside two companies that came close to failing before becoming two of the most consequential companies of their era.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ໄວເດັກທີ່ຫຼໍ່ຫຼອມດ້ວຍຄວາມໂດດດ່ຽວ ແລະ ປຶ້ມ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Vance ເປີດເລື່ອງຢູ່ Pretoria ປະເທດອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ ບ່ອນທີ່ Musk ເຕີບໃຫຍ່ໃນຄອບຄົວທີ່ພໍ່ແມ່ຢ່າຮ້າງກັນ ແລະ ມີຄວາມສຳພັນທີ່ຕຶງຄຽດກັບພໍ່ຂອງລາວ Errol ຊຶ່ງບາງຄັ້ງເຄັ່ງຄັດຮ້າຍແຮງ. ໂຮງຮຽນກໍ່ບໍ່ໄດ້ເປັນບ່ອນປອດໄພ — Vance ເລົ່າເຖິງສະພາບແວດລ້ອມທີ່ Musk ຖືກລັງແກຢ່າງໜັກ ຈົນຢ່າງໜ້ອຍໜຶ່ງຄັ້ງຕ້ອງໄດ້ຮັບການປິ່ນປົວຢູ່ໂຮງໝໍ. ພາບລວມແມ່ນເດັກນ້ອຍທີ່ສະຫຼາດແຕ່ບໍ່ຄ່ອງແຄ້ວທາງສັງຄົມ ແລະ ບໍ່ລົງຕົວກັບສະພາບແວດລ້ອມອ້ອມຂ້າງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ສິ່ງທີ່ຕື່ມຊ່ອງຫວ່າງນັ້ນ ຕາມການເລົ່າຂອງ Vance ຄືການອ່ານ. Musk ຖືກລາຍງານວ່າອ່ານສາລານຸກົມຈົນຈົບ ແລະ ອ່ານນິຍາຍວິທະຍາສາດຈຳນວນຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ຮວມທັງເລື່ອງລາວລະດັບອາລະຍະທຳ ທີ່ຄົນຄົນດຽວ ຫຼື ສະຖາບັນນ້ອຍໆ ສາມາດປ່ຽນຊະຕາກຳຂອງທັງສັງຄົມໄດ້. Vance ຖືວ່ານິໄສນີ້ບໍ່ແມ່ນພຽງລາຍລະອຽດໜ້າຮັກຂອງໄວເດັກ — ຂະໜາດຂອງຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານທີ່ Musk ຈະນຳມາໃຊ້ກັບບໍລິສັດຈິງໃນພາຍຫຼັງ ສະທ້ອນຂະໜາດຂອງເລື່ອງລາວທີ່ລາວອ່ານຕັ້ງແຕ່ນ້ອຍ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຫົວຂໍ້ຫຼັກຂອງບົດນີ້ ຄື ຄວາມສາມາດເພິ່ງພາຕົນເອງ ບໍ່ແມ່ນລັກສະນະນິໄສທີ່ Musk ເລືອກເອົາ ແຕ່ເປັນທັກສະທີ່ໄວເດັກທີ່ບໍ່ມີການປົກປ້ອງບັງຄັບໃຫ້ລາວພັດທະນາຂຶ້ນຕັ້ງແຕ່ຍັງນ້ອຍ ກ່ອນທີ່ຈະມີບໍລິສັດໃດໆໃຫ້ນຳໄປໃຊ້.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ດົນນານກ່ອນທີ່ລາວຈະສ້າງຫຍັງເລີຍ ໄວເດັກທີ່ຫຍຸ້ງຍາກ ແລະ ນິໄສການອ່ານທີ່ຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ໄດ້ສອນ Musk ທັງຄວາມເພິ່ງພາຕົນເອງ ແລະ ລົດຊາດຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານລະດັບອາລະຍະທຳ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ອອກຈາກອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້: ການາດາ, Penn ແລະ ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ວາງແຜນໄວ້$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຕອນເປັນໄວລຸ້ນ Musk ຕັ້ງໃຈຢາກອອກຈາກອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ ແລະ Vance ອະທິບາຍເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ຊັດເຈນ ຄືກັບອອກແບບໄວ້ລ່ວງໜ້າ: ໄປການາດາກ່ອນ ໂດຍໃຊ້ຄວາມສຳພັນຄອບຄົວເພື່ອຕັ້ງຖິ່ນຖານຢູ່ທີ່ນັ້ນ ຈາກນັ້ນເຮັດວຽກຫຼາຍຢ່າງທີ່ບໍ່ຫຼູຫຼາເພື່ອລ້ຽງຕົນເອງ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ຄ່ອຍໆເດີນໄປສູ່ມະຫາວິທະຍາໄລ ແລະ ໃນທີ່ສຸດ ໄປສູ່ສະຫະລັດອາເມຣິກາ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ລາວໄດ້ເຂົ້າຮຽນທີ່ University of Pennsylvania ໂດຍຮຽນທັງຟີຊິກ ແລະ ທຸລະກິດ — ການປະສົມສອງດ້ານນີ້ Vance ຖືວ່າເປັນສັນຍານແຕ່ຫົວທີ ຂອງຮູບແບບທີ່ຈະກຳນົດອາຊີບຂອງ Musk ໃນພາຍຫຼັງ: ຈັບຄູ່ຄວາມຄ່ອງແຄ້ວດ້ານເຕັກນິກ ກັບການລົງມືເຮັດທຸລະກິດ ແທນທີ່ຈະເລືອກເອົາພຽງດ້ານໃດດ້ານໜຶ່ງ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ແມ່ນແຕ່ເສັ້ນທາງອອກຈາກອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ຂອງລາວ ກໍ່ຖືກອອກແບບຄືກັນກັບບໍລິສັດຂອງລາວໃນພາຍຫຼັງ — ເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ຕັ້ງໃຈ, ຫຼາຍຂັ້ນຕອນ ໄປສູ່ບ່ອນທີ່ລາວຢາກໄປແທ້ໆ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Stanford ພຽງສອງມື້: ເລືອກອິນເຕີເນັດແທນປະລິນຍາເອກ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຫຼັງຈາກ Penn Musk ໄດ້ຮັບການຮັບເຂົ້າຮຽນລະດັບບັນທິດ ຢູ່ Stanford ດ້ານວິທະຍາສາດວັດສະດຸ ແລະ ການເກັບພະລັງງານ. ການເລົ່າຂອງ Vance ກ່ຽວກັບສິ່ງທີ່ເກີດຂຶ້ນຕໍ່ມາ ເປັນໜຶ່ງໃນເລື່ອງທີ່ໂດດເດັ່ນທີ່ສຸດຂອງປຶ້ມ: Musk ມາຮອດ California ໃນຊ່ວງກາງຊຸມປີ 1990 ພໍດີກັບຊ່ວງອິນເຕີເນັດເຊີງພານິດກຳລັງລະເບີດຂຶ້ນ ແລະ ພາຍໃນສອງສາມມື້ ລາວກໍ່ສະຫຼຸບວ່າ ໂອກາດທີ່ຢູ່ຕໍ່ໜ້າບໍ່ສາມາດລໍຖ້າໃຫ້ຮຽນຈົບປະລິນຍາເອກໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ລາວຖອນຕົວອອກຈາກໂຄງການເກືອບທັນທີ ແລະ ຮ່ວມກັບນ້ອງຊາຍ Kimbal ອອກໄປສ້າງບໍລິສັດແທນ. Vance ບໍ່ໄດ້ຖືວ່າການຕັດສິນໃຈນີ້ເປັນຄວາມບໍ່ຍັ້ງຄິດ ແຕ່ເປັນການສະແດງອອກແຕ່ຫົວທີ ຂອງລັກສະນະນິໄສທີ່ຈະປາກົດຊ້ຳຫຼາຍຄັ້ງຕະຫຼອດປຶ້ມ: ຄວາມເຕັມໃຈຂອງ Musk ທີ່ຈະປະຖິ້ມເສັ້ນທາງທີ່ປອດໄພ ມີໃບຢັ້ງຢືນ ທັນທີທີ່ລາວຕັດສິນວ່າຈັງຫວະເວລາຂອງໂອກາດນັ້ນສຳຄັນກວ່າໃບຢັ້ງຢືນເອງ.$$)
