    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ມັນເປັນເລື່ອງລາວທີ່ແຄບກວ່າ "ທຸກຢ່າງກ່ຽວກັບ Musk".$$, 'body', $$ແມ່ນແຕ່ພາຍໃນຊ່ວງເວລາຂອງມັນເອງ ປຶ້ມກໍ່ຍັງເນັ້ນໃສ່ SpaceX ແລະ Tesla ເປັນເຄື່ອງຈັກຫຼັກຂອງເລື່ອງລາວ ສ່ວນທຸລະກິດອື່ນ ແລະ ດ້ານຊີວິດສ່ວນຕົວຂອງ Musk ໄດ້ຮັບການລົງເລິກໜ້ອຍກວ່າ.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ອ່ານພ້ອມກັບຂໍ້ຈຳກັດເຫຼົ່ານີ້ ປຶ້ມຫົວນີ້ກໍ່ຍັງເຮັດໃນສິ່ງທີ່ໜ້ອຍຄົນຈະເຮັດໄດ້ຄືກັນຕັ້ງແຕ່ນັ້ນມາ: ມັນບັນທຶກ ດ້ວຍລາຍລະອຽດທີ່ໃກ້ຊິດ ແລະ ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖື ວ່າພາຍໃນສອງບໍລິສັດທີ່ເກືອບລົ້ມເຫຼວ ກ່ອນຈະກາຍເປັນສອງບໍລິສັດທີ່ສຳຄັນທີ່ສຸດແຫ່ງຍຸກ ມີໜ້າຕາເປັນແນວໃດແທ້.$$)
  ))
  ),
  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Zip2$$, 'body', $$An early web company producing online city guides and business directories for newspaper publishers, co-founded by Musk and later sold for a substantial sum — his first real capital.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$PayPal (X.com)$$, 'body', $$Musk's online payments venture X.com merged with rival Confinity and was renamed PayPal; sold to eBay in a deal that made Musk considerably wealthier despite his earlier ouster as CEO.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$First-principles thinking$$, 'body', $$Reasoning that strips a problem down to fundamental physical and material facts, rather than reasoning by analogy from industry convention or historical cost.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Multiplanetary species$$, 'body', $$SpaceX's stated long-run purpose: spreading human civilization beyond Earth as a hedge against risks that could otherwise end it on a single planet.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Reusable rockets$$, 'body', $$A long-run SpaceX goal of recovering and reflying rocket stages instead of discarding them after one launch, aimed at cutting the cost of reaching orbit.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Vertical integration$$, 'body', $$Building and controlling as much of the manufacturing process in-house as possible rather than outsourcing it — a hallmark of Musk's operating approach at Tesla and SpaceX.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Sustainable energy transition$$, 'body', $$The mission Musk assigns to Tesla and SolarCity: accelerating the shift away from fossil fuels toward electric transport and renewable power.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Zip2$$, 'body', $$ບໍລິສັດເວັບຍຸກຕົ້ນ ຜະລິດຄູ່ມືແນະນຳເມືອງ ແລະ ບັນຊີລາຍຊື່ທຸລະກິດອອນລາຍໃຫ້ຜູ້ພິມໜັງສືພິມ ຮ່ວມກໍ່ຕັ້ງໂດຍ Musk ຕໍ່ມາຖືກຂາຍໄປໃນມູນຄ່າຫຼວງຫຼາຍ — ທຶນກ້ອນທຳອິດຂອງລາວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$PayPal (X.com)$$, 'body', $$ທຸລະກິດການຊຳລະເງິນອອນລາຍຂອງ Musk ຊື່ X.com ຮ່ວມກິດຈະການກັບ Confinity ຄູ່ແຂ່ງ ແລະ ຖືກປ່ຽນຊື່ເປັນ PayPal; ຂາຍໃຫ້ eBay ໃນສັນຍາທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ Musk ຮັ່ງມີຂຶ້ນຢ່າງຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ເຖິງແມ່ນຖືກເບືອນອອກຈາກຕຳແໜ່ງ CEO ກ່ອນໜ້ານັ້ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການຄິດແບບຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານ (First-principles thinking)$$, 'body', $$ວິທີການຄິດທີ່ແຍກບັນຫາລົງໄປຫາຂໍ້ເທັດຈິງທາງຟີຊິກ ແລະ ວັດຖຸດິບພື້ນຖານ ແທນທີ່ຈະໃຫ້ເຫດຜົນຕາມການປຽບທຽບກັບແບບແຜນ ຫຼື ລາຄາທີ່ອຸດສາຫະກຳເຄີຍໃຊ້ມາ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຊະນິດຫຼາຍດາວ (Multiplanetary species)$$, 'body', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍໄລຍະຍາວທີ່ SpaceX ປະກາດ: ຂະຫຍາຍອາລະຍະທຳມະນຸດອອກນອກໂລກ ເປັນປະກັນຄວາມສ່ຽງທີ່ອາດຈະທຳລາຍມະນຸດຊາດ ຖ້າຢູ່ພຽງດາວດວງດຽວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ຈະຫຼວດນຳກັບມາໃຊ້ໃໝ່ (Reusable rockets)$$, 'body', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍໄລຍະຍາວຂອງ SpaceX ໃນການເກັບກູ້ ແລະ ນຳຂັ້ນຈະຫຼວດກັບມາໃຊ້ອີກ ແທນທີ່ຈະຖິ້ມຫຼັງໃຊ້ຄັ້ງດຽວ ເພື່ອຫຼຸດຕົ້ນທຶນໃນການໄປເຖິງວົງໂຄຈອນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການລວມແນວຕັ້ງ (Vertical integration)$$, 'body', $$ການສ້າງ ແລະ ຄວບຄຸມຂະບວນການຜະລິດໃຫ້ຫຼາຍທີ່ສຸດເທົ່າທີ່ຈະເປັນໄປໄດ້ພາຍໃນອົງກອນເອງ ແທນທີ່ຈະຈ້າງພາຍນອກ — ລັກສະນະເດັ່ນຂອງແນວທາງການດຳເນີນງານຂອງ Musk ຢູ່ Tesla ແລະ SpaceX.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການປ່ຽນຜ່ານພະລັງງານແບບຍືນຍົງ$$, 'body', $$ພາລະກິດທີ່ Musk ມອບໃຫ້ Tesla ແລະ SolarCity: ເລັ່ງການປ່ຽນຈາກເຊື້ອໄຟຟອດຊິວ ໄປສູ່ການຂົນສົ່ງດ້ວຍໄຟຟ້າ ແລະ ພະລັງງານໝູນວຽນ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Musk's first-principles thinking produced real breakthroughs in rockets and batteries. Where might the same instinct to ignore "how the industry has always done it" lead to costly mistakes instead?$$, $$Vance reports Musk's management style as both inspiring and punishing, sometimes from the same employees. Do you think extreme intensity is a necessary cost of extreme ambition, or a separate choice Musk could have made differently?$$, $$Musk could only make his bet on SpaceX and Tesla because he already had a fortune from PayPal to risk. How much of his story is a repeatable lesson, and how much depends on a starting position most people will never have?$$, $$The book frames Tesla and SpaceX around civilizational missions rather than ordinary profit-seeking. Where is that framing genuinely motivating, and where might it be used to justify demands a normal employer could not?$$, $$Published in 2015, the book captures Musk mid-story. Which parts of the portrait it paints — the risk tolerance, the first-principles habit, the demanding leadership — do you think held steady in the years since, and which do you think changed?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ການຄິດແບບຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານຂອງ Musk ໄດ້ສ້າງຄວາມກ້າວໜ້າແທ້ໆໃນຈະຫຼວດ ແລະ ແບັດເຕີຣີ. ໃນຈຸດໃດແດ່ ທີ່ສັນຊາດຕະຍານແບບບໍ່ສົນໃຈ "ວິທີທີ່ອຸດສາຫະກຳເຄີຍເຮັດມາ" ອາດພາໄປສູ່ຄວາມຜິດພາດທີ່ແພງແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນຄວາມກ້າວໜ້າ?$$, $$Vance ລາຍງານແບບການບໍລິຫານຂອງ Musk ວ່າທັງເປັນແຮງບັນດານໃຈ ແລະ ໂຫດຮ້າຍ ບາງເທື່ອຈາກພະນັກງານຄົນດຽວກັນ. ທ່ານຄິດວ່າຄວາມເຂັ້ມຂຸ້ນສຸດຂີດ ເປັນຕົ້ນທຶນທີ່ຈຳເປັນຂອງຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານສຸດຂີດ ຫຼືເປັນທາງເລືອກແຍກຕ່າງຫາກທີ່ Musk ສາມາດເລືອກແຕກຕ່າງໄດ້?$$, $$Musk ສາມາດເດີມພັນກັບ SpaceX ແລະ Tesla ໄດ້ ພຽງເພາະລາວມີໂຊກລາບຈາກ PayPal ໃຫ້ສ່ຽງຢູ່ແລ້ວ. ເລື່ອງລາວຂອງລາວມີສ່ວນໃດແດ່ທີ່ເປັນບົດຮຽນທີ່ເຮັດຊ້ຳໄດ້ ແລະ ສ່ວນໃດແດ່ທີ່ຂຶ້ນກັບຈຸດເລີ່ມຕົ້ນທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຈະບໍ່ມີວັນມີ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມກຳນົດ Tesla ແລະ SpaceX ອ້ອມຮອບພາລະກິດລະດັບອາລະຍະທຳ ແທນທີ່ຈະເປັນການສະແຫວງຫາກຳໄລທຳມະດາ. ກອບຄິດແບບນັ້ນສ້າງແຮງບັນດານໃຈຢ່າງແທ້ຈິງໃນຈຸດໃດ ແລະ ອາດຖືກໃຊ້ເພື່ອສະໜັບສະໜູນຄວາມຮຽກຮ້ອງທີ່ນາຍຈ້າງທົ່ວໄປເຮັດບໍ່ໄດ້ໃນຈຸດໃດ?$$, $$ພິມອອກໃນປີ 2015 ປຶ້ມນີ້ບັນທຶກ Musk ໃນລະຫວ່າງທາງຂອງເລື່ອງລາວ. ພາບໃນປຶ້ມສ່ວນໃດແດ່ — ຄວາມທົນທານຕໍ່ຄວາມສ່ຽງ, ນິໄສການຄິດແບບຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານ, ການນຳແບບຮຽກຮ້ອງສູງ — ທີ່ທ່ານຄິດວ່າຍັງຄົງຢູ່ໃນຫຼາຍປີຕໍ່ມາ ແລະ ສ່ວນໃດແດ່ທີ່ທ່ານຄິດວ່າໄດ້ປ່ຽນໄປ?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$A difficult, isolated childhood in South Africa and an intense reading habit shaped Musk's self-reliance and civilization-scale ambition long before he built a company.$$, $$Musk engineered his own path out of South Africa — Canada, then the University of Pennsylvania — as deliberately as he would later engineer a company.$$, $$He quit a Stanford PhD program within days, judging the timing of the early internet to be more urgent than the credential.$$, $$Zip2, an early online city-guide and directory service, gave Musk his first real capital after selling for tens of millions of dollars.$$, $$X.com became PayPal after a merger and internal power struggle; Musk was ousted as CEO but still profited substantially when eBay bought the company.$$, $$Musk's first-principles thinking means breaking a problem down to raw physics and material costs instead of accepting industry-standard assumptions about what things must cost.$$, $$After PayPal, Musk bet his fortune on two of the hardest industries to enter — rockets at SpaceX and electric cars at Tesla — both known for high capital needs and high failure rates.$$, $$In 2008, three failed SpaceX launches and Tesla's production and financing struggles brought both companies to the edge of collapse in the same year.$$, $$Musk's management style is reported as both inspiring and punishing — extreme hours and hands-on technical demands that some employees describe as the best work of their lives and others describe as burnout-inducing.$$, $$Musk frames Tesla and SpaceX around civilizational missions — sustainable energy and multiplanetary survival — which also functions as a way to justify unusually demanding terms of work.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ໄວເດັກທີ່ຫຍຸ້ງຍາກ ແລະ ໂດດດ່ຽວໃນອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ ພ້ອມນິໄສການອ່ານທີ່ເຂັ້ມຂຸ້ນ ໄດ້ຫຼໍ່ຫຼອມຄວາມເພິ່ງພາຕົນເອງ ແລະ ຄວາມທະເຍີທະຍານລະດັບອາລະຍະທຳຂອງ Musk ດົນນານກ່ອນລາວຈະສ້າງບໍລິສັດ.$$, $$Musk ອອກແບບເສັ້ນທາງອອກຈາກອາຟຣິກາໃຕ້ດ້ວຍຕົນເອງ — ການາດາ ຈາກນັ້ນ University of Pennsylvania — ຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ ຄືກັບທີ່ລາວຈະອອກແບບບໍລິສັດໃນພາຍຫຼັງ.$$, $$ລາວລາອອກຈາກໂຄງການປະລິນຍາເອກທີ່ Stanford ພາຍໃນສອງສາມມື້ ໂດຍຕັດສິນວ່າຈັງຫວະເວລາຂອງອິນເຕີເນັດຍຸກຕົ້ນສຳຄັນກວ່າໃບຢັ້ງຢືນ.$$, $$Zip2 ບໍລິການແນະນຳເມືອງ ແລະ ບັນຊີລາຍຊື່ອອນລາຍຍຸກຕົ້ນ ໃຫ້ທຶນກ້ອນທຳອິດແທ້ໆແກ່ Musk ຫຼັງຈາກຖືກຂາຍໄປໃນມູນຄ່າຫຼາຍສິບລ້ານໂດລາ.$$, $$X.com ກາຍເປັນ PayPal ຫຼັງການຮ່ວມກິດຈະການ ແລະ ການແກ່ງແຍ້ງອຳນາດພາຍໃນ; Musk ຖືກເບືອນອອກຈາກຕຳແໜ່ງ CEO ແຕ່ຍັງໄດ້ກຳໄລຢ່າງຫຼວງຫຼາຍ ເມື່ອ eBay ຊື້ບໍລິສັດ.$$, $$ການຄິດແບບຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານຂອງ Musk ໝາຍເຖິງການແຍກບັນຫາລົງໄປຫາຟີຊິກ ແລະ ຕົ້ນທຶນວັດຖຸດິບ ແທນທີ່ຈະຍອມຮັບຂໍ້ສົມມຸດຕິຖານມາດຕະຖານຂອງອຸດສາຫະກຳວ່າສິ່ງໃດຕ້ອງມີລາຄາເທົ່າໃດ.$$, $$ຫຼັງ PayPal Musk ເດີມພັນໂຊກລາບຂອງລາວໃນສອງອຸດສາຫະກຳທີ່ຍາກທີ່ສຸດໃນການເຂົ້າສູ່ — ຈະຫຼວດຢູ່ SpaceX ແລະ ລົດໄຟຟ້າຢູ່ Tesla — ທັງສອງຂຶ້ນຊື່ວ່າຕ້ອງການທຶນສູງ ແລະ ອັດຕາລົ້ມເຫຼວສູງ.$$, $$ໃນປີ 2008 ຈະຫຼວດ SpaceX ລົ້ມເຫຼວສາມຄັ້ງຕິດຕໍ່ກັນ ພ້ອມກັບບັນຫາການຜະລິດ ແລະ ການລະດົມທຶນຂອງ Tesla ໄດ້ພາທັງສອງບໍລິສັດເຂົ້າໃກ້ຄວາມລົ້ມລະລາຍໃນປີດຽວກັນ.$$, $$ແບບການບໍລິຫານຂອງ Musk ຖືກລາຍງານວ່າທັງເປັນແຮງບັນດານໃຈ ແລະ ໂຫດຮ້າຍ — ຊົ່ວໂມງເຮັດວຽກອັນສຸດຂີດ ແລະ ຄວາມຮຽກຮ້ອງດ້ານເຕັກນິກແບບລົງມືເອງ ທີ່ພະນັກງານບາງຄົນຖືວ່າເປັນວຽກທີ່ດີທີ່ສຸດໃນຊີວິດ ແຕ່ບາງຄົນຖືວ່າກໍ່ໃຫ້ເກີດອາການໝົດໄຟ.$$, $$Musk ກຳນົດ Tesla ແລະ SpaceX ອ້ອມຮອບພາລະກິດລະດັບອາລະຍະທຳ — ພະລັງງານແບບຍືນຍົງ ແລະ ການຢູ່ລອດແບບຫຼາຍດາວ — ຊຶ່ງກໍ່ເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນເຫດຜົນສະໜັບສະໜູນເງື່ອນໄຂການເຮັດວຽກທີ່ຮຽກຮ້ອງສູງຜິດປົກກະຕິ.$$]
where book_id in ('255be173-29f9-43e1-8793-aaed91da7033', '1edfa8ad-9f85-452f-a3b3-cddb3b1eb081');

-- Broken Money — Lyn Alden
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 19,
  summary_en = $$A sweeping history of monetary technology - from barter and commodity money through gold, paper claims, and the fiat dollar system - that argues today's money is a transitional stage, not a permanent endpoint.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ປະຫວັດສາດເຕັກໂນໂລຢີການເງິນຢ່າງກວ້າງຂວາງ - ຈາກການແລກປ່ຽນສິນຄ້າ ແລະ ເງິນສິນຄ້າ ຜ່ານຄຳ, ໃບຮັບຮອງເຈ້ຍ ຫາລະບົບໂດລາເງິນເຈ້ຍ - ທີ່ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າເງິນຕາປັດຈຸບັນເປັນຂັ້ນຕອນຊົ່ວຄາວ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຈຸດສິ້ນສຸດຖາວອນ.$$,
  deck_en = $$Lyn Alden traces money's evolution as a technology: from the coincidence-of-wants problem that barter never solved, through gold's centuries-long reign, to the paper claims and central banking that gradually separated money from anything scarce, and finally to the pure fiat system born in 1971. Her central test is simple - does a money work as a medium of exchange, a unit of account, and a store of value, all at once, and for a long time? This lesson walks through that history, the fiat era's structural strains that Alden emphasizes, and Bitcoin's attempt to reintroduce hardness into a digital ledger, before weighing the argument's strengths against its open questions.$$,
  deck_lo = $$Lyn Alden ຕິດຕາມວິວັດທະນາການຂອງເງິນໃນຖານະເຕັກໂນໂລຢີໜຶ່ງ: ຈາກບັນຫາຄວາມບັງເອີນຂອງຄວາມຕ້ອງການ ທີ່ການແລກປ່ຽນສິນຄ້າໂດຍກົງບໍ່ເຄີຍແກ້ໄດ້, ຜ່ານຍຸກທີ່ຄຳຄອງອຳນາດເປັນເງິນມາຫຼາຍສະຕະວັດ, ຫາໃບຮັບຮອງເຈ້ຍ ແລະ ທະນາຄານກາງທີ່ຄ່ອຍໆແຍກເງິນອອກຈາກສິ່ງທີ່ຫາຍາກ, ແລະ ສຸດທ້າຍຫາລະບົບເງິນເຈ້ຍລ້ວນໆທີ່ເກີດຂຶ້ນໃນປີ 1971. ບົດທົດສອບຫຼັກຂອງລາວງ່າຍໆ: ເງິນນັ້ນເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນສື່ກາງແລກປ່ຽນ, ຫົວໜ່ວຍບັນຊີ ແລະ ບ່ອນເກັບມູນຄ່າ ພ້ອມກັນ ແລະ ຕໍ່ເນື່ອງເປັນເວລາດົນບໍ? ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະນຳທ່ານຜ່ານປະຫວັດສາດນັ້ນ, ຄວາມກົດດັນທາງໂຄງສ້າງຂອງຍຸກເງິນເຈ້ຍທີ່ Alden ເນັ້ນໜັກ, ແລະ ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມຂອງ Bitcoin ໃນການນຳຄວາມແຂງກັບຄືນສູ່ບັນຊີແຍກປະເພດແບບດິຈິຕອນ ກ່ອນຈະຊັ່ງນ້ຳໜັກຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງນີ້ທຽບກັບຄຳຖາມທີ່ຍັງເປີດຢູ່.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Money as evolving technology: the three-function test$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden opens by treating money not as a fixed law of economics but as a technology - a tool people converged on to solve a coordination problem, one that has been re-engineered many times across history and will likely be re-engineered again. The question she keeps returning to is not "what is money" in the abstract but "what has consistently worked," judged against three jobs any money is asked to do.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Medium of exchange.$$, 'body', $$Something widely accepted in trade, so people do not have to swap goods directly for other goods.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Unit of account.$$, 'body', $$A common measuring stick for prices, so the value of unlike things - a haircut, a hectare, a hard drive - can be compared on the same scale.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Store of value.$$, 'body', $$Something that holds purchasing power between the moment it is earned and the moment it is spent, whether that gap is a week or a lifetime.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A form of money can technically function as one or two of these without qualifying as good money overall. Alden stresses that history's durable monies satisfied all three reasonably well at once, and that monies which excel at only one or two eventually get displaced or supplemented by something that does better across all three.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Good money$$, 'body', $$In Alden's framing, a monetary good that serves as medium of exchange, unit of account, and store of value simultaneously, without one function badly undermining another - for instance, a money that is easy to spend but constantly loses value undermines its own usefulness as a store of value and, eventually, as a stable unit of account.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This three-function test becomes the ruler she applies across the whole book - to cattle and salt, to gold and silver, to the dollar, and eventually to Bitcoin - and it is the frame worth holding onto as the history unfolds, because the book's implicit argument is that no monetary technology is final; each one is simply the best available solution until a better one displaces it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Money is judged by whether it works as a medium of exchange, a unit of account, and a store of value at once - and history's monies have repeatedly been replaced by better solutions to that same three-part problem.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Before money: barter's limits and early commodity money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Direct barter's fatal flaw is the double coincidence of wants: a farmer with surplus grain who wants a pair of shoes must find a shoemaker who wants grain, at the same time, in matching quantities. As soon as a community trades in more than a handful of goods, satisfying that coincidence becomes rare enough that most potentially valuable trades simply never happen, and the division of labor - the basis of a more productive economy - stalls.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Communities' response was to converge on some good that nearly everyone would accept, even people with no direct use for it, because they trusted they could pass it on later. Alden surveys the wide variety of goods that have served this role: cattle and other livestock, salt, cowrie shells, and large stone discs, among others, each chosen because it was relatively scarce, recognizable, or otherwise suited to the community that used it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Each of these commodity monies eventually ran into limits that kept it from becoming durable, general-purpose money. Cattle are divisible only by killing the animal and impossible to split for a small purchase. Salt and shells hold their value only as long as supply stays naturally constrained by geography; once new sources or trade routes are found, the value collapses. Alden's point is that these were not naive choices - they were the best available option at the time - but each one eventually met a good that beat it on one of the three functions, usually store of value, and lost its monetary role as a result.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Barter fails on the coincidence of wants, and the commodity monies that first solved it - cattle, salt, shells, stone - all eventually lost the role once something more durable, portable, or scarce came along.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Why metals won: the properties of a good money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden lays out the specific physical properties that let a good serve reliably as money, and argues that metals - gold and silver above all - satisfied more of them simultaneously than anything that came before.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Divisibility.$$, 'body', $$Can be split into smaller units and recombined without losing value, so it can price both a loaf of bread and a house.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Durability.$$, 'body', $$Does not decay, rot, or corrode, so value earned today can still be there decades later.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Portability.$$, 'body', $$Holds a lot of value relative to its weight and bulk, so it can move with its owner or across long trade routes.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Scarcity.$$, 'body', $$Cannot easily be produced in large new quantities relative to what already exists, so existing holders are not casually diluted.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Fungibility.$$, 'body', $$Any unit is interchangeable with any other of the same quantity and purity - one ounce is worth exactly the same as any other ounce.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Verifiability.$$, 'body', $$Its authenticity and quantity can be checked relatively easily, so it is hard to counterfeit or misrepresent.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Gold in particular combines chemical inertness (it does not tarnish or corrode, so virtually every ounce ever mined still exists somewhere) with a mining industry that has never been able to expand the existing stockpile by more than a small percentage in any given year, even during gold rushes. That combination gives it an unusually high ratio of existing stock to new annual production, which is what makes it resistant to the kind of debasement that undermined cattle, salt, and shells. Silver shares many of the same properties at a lower value density, which suited it to smaller everyday transactions once gold became the preferred store of value for larger sums.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden treats the near-universal, independent convergence of separated civilizations on gold and silver as money - societies with no contact with one another arriving at the same answer - as strong evidence that these properties, not custom or decree, are what made metals win.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Gold and silver did not become money by decree - separated civilizations converged on them independently because they combine divisibility, durability, portability, scarcity, fungibility, and verifiability better than any alternative.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$From gold in hand to paper claims$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Gold solved store of value and, to a large degree, unit of account, but it is heavy and slow to move, which makes it a poor medium of exchange for anything beyond local, in-person trade. Alden traces how this gap was closed not by replacing gold but by layering paper claims on top of it: goldsmiths and early bankers would hold gold in secure storage and issue a receipt for it, and those receipts - lighter, easier to transport, and easier to divide - began circulating as money in their own right.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This solved the portability problem, but it introduced a structural vulnerability that had not existed when gold itself was the money changing hands.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Fractional reserve banking$$, 'body', $$The practice of holding only a portion of deposited gold (or currency) in reserve while lending out or issuing claims against the rest, on the assumption that not all depositors will demand redemption at the same time.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Once a goldsmith or bank realized that depositors rarely all showed up to redeem their gold simultaneously, it became possible - and profitable - to issue more paper claims than there was gold to back them. This let credit expand well beyond the physical stock of metal, but it also meant the system's stability now depended on confidence: if enough holders of paper claims lost faith and demanded gold at once, the reserve would be exposed as insufficient, triggering a bank run. Alden treats this moment - the point where money's backing became a promise rather than a physical object in hand - as the hinge on which the rest of the book's history turns.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Paper claims on gold solved portability but replaced physical certainty with trust in an issuer, and fractional reserves made that trust breakable under stress.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The classical gold standard and its collapse in the First World War$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$By the late nineteenth century, a large share of the trading world had formalized the gold-claim system into the classical gold standard: national currencies were defined as a fixed weight of gold, freely convertible into it, which pinned exchange rates between countries and let capital and trade move across borders with limited currency risk. Alden describes this era's price stability and financial integration as an unusual, if imperfect, high point in monetary history.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The system's discipline rested on convertibility actually holding - a government could not print far beyond what its gold reserves could back without triggering redemption pressure it could not meet. That discipline broke in the summer of 1914: as the major European powers entered the First World War, they suspended convertibility of their currencies into gold in order to finance the war through money creation and borrowing rather than through taxation alone, which would have been slower and more politically constrained.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The interwar years that followed were an extended, uneven attempt to put the pieces back together - countries returning to gold at exchange rates that no longer matched how much currency had actually been printed, competitive devaluations, and episodes of severe currency collapse in the countries hit hardest. Alden treats 1914 as the moment the modern world's relationship with hard money began to fracture, decades before it broke completely.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The classical gold standard's stability depended on convertibility actually holding, and that discipline was the first casualty of the First World War, not the last.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Bretton Woods: the dollar standing in for gold$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The 1944 Bretton Woods agreement built a new system out of the wreckage of the interwar years: rather than every currency being independently convertible into gold, the US dollar alone was pegged to gold at a fixed rate, and other major currencies were pegged to the dollar. In effect, the world adopted a dollar standard with a gold backstop one step removed - only foreign central banks and governments, not the public, could actually redeem dollars for gold.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This arrangement let the US run its economy and its currency somewhat more flexibly than a direct domestic gold standard would have allowed, while still giving the rest of the world a nominal anchor through the dollar's promised convertibility. Alden frames it as a workable compromise for a period, one that helped rebuild global trade after two world wars, but one carrying an inherent strain: American domestic spending and foreign-policy commitments were not bound by the same constraint that gold convertibility had once imposed directly.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$As US spending grew through the 1950s and 1960s, the number of dollars held abroad increasingly exceeded what US gold reserves could plausibly cover if foreign governments all sought redemption. Some governments began doing exactly that, testing whether the promise behind Bretton Woods still held.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Bretton Woods pegged the world to a dollar that was pegged to gold - a system that worked only as long as America issued fewer dollar claims than its gold reserves could actually redeem.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The Nixon Shock and the birth of pure fiat money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$In August 1971, facing mounting foreign demands to redeem dollars for gold at a rate the US could not sustainably meet, President Nixon suspended the dollar's convertibility into gold - a move announced as temporary that was never reversed. Alden treats this as the decisive break in the entire history the book has been building toward: for the first time, the world's dominant currency, and by extension the currencies pegged to it, had no formal, redeemable link to a physically scarce asset at all.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$What replaced the old anchor was not a new commodity backing but pure policy discretion: from 1971 onward, the supply of the world's reserve currency has been set by the decisions of central banks and governments rather than by how much of anything could be mined, found, or physically constrained. Alden is careful to note this was not a single dramatic collapse but a quiet procedural announcement, which is part of why she thinks its significance is still underappreciated - it did not feel, at the time, like the end of an era, even though in her account it was exactly that.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$She frames everything that follows in the book - the character of the modern financial system, the behavior of savers, the growth of debt - as downstream of this one architectural fact: money's supply is no longer constrained by anything outside the decisions of the people who issue it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The Nixon Shock did not look dramatic at the time, but it ended, without formal replacement, the last link between the world's reserve currency and anything physically scarce.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The fiat era's report card$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$With the anchor gone, Alden argues, several patterns became structurally more likely, even if not inevitable in any given year. The first is chronic currency debasement: because a fiat currency's supply is a policy variable rather than a physical constraint, and because governments generally find it easier to finance obligations partly through money creation than through taxation alone, the purchasing power of fiat currencies has tended to erode steadily over long periods, in a way gold-linked currencies did not.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The second is the growth of the financial sector and of debt relative to the productive economy. When credit can expand without a hard physical constraint, borrowing becomes cheaper and more available, and Alden traces how debt levels - government, corporate, and household - and the size of the financial industry itself have both grown considerably faster than output over the fiat era, a pattern she treats as a structural consequence of the monetary regime rather than a coincidence of the period.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The third, and the one she treats most pointedly, is a link to widening wealth inequality. Newly created money and newly available credit do not raise all prices uniformly or simultaneously; they tend to inflate the prices of financial assets - stocks, real estate, and other property - before they show up as broader consumer-price inflation. People who already own such assets benefit from that inflation directly; people whose income is mostly wages, and who hold their savings mostly in cash or bonds, do not participate in the same way and in real terms fall further behind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The fourth is the difficulty of saving itself. A currency engineered, in Alden's account, to lose purchasing power steadily makes simple saving - holding cash or low-risk bonds - a losing proposition over any long horizon, which structurally pushes ordinary savers toward riskier assets just to preserve what they have, whether or not they are equipped to manage that risk.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Alden ties four fiat-era patterns together - chronic debasement, financialization, widening inequality, and the erosion of simple saving - as structural consequences of a currency with no physical anchor, not separate coincidences.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The eurodollar system: the hidden plumbing of global finance$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$One of the book's more technical contributions is its account of the eurodollar system - the vast network of dollar-denominated deposits, loans, and credit that exists largely outside the direct regulatory reach of the US banking system, held and created by banks around the world.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden explains that a dollar does not need to physically leave the United States, or even pass through the Federal Reserve's direct oversight, to be lent, borrowed, or multiplied through the banking system - a bank in London or Singapore can create a dollar-denominated deposit through ordinary credit creation, and that new dollar-denominated claim then circulates through a web of correspondent banking relationships alongside dollars issued domestically.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The consequence, in her account, is a global dollar-based financial system whose true scale and structure are considerably harder to observe and measure than official money-supply statistics suggest, since so much of it is created through layered bank credit rather than through anything a central bank directly controls. This opacity is not, in her telling, a conspiracy so much as an emergent property of a system that grew organically around the dollar's role as the world's reserve currency - but it does mean that the practical money supply, and the fragility built into it, is considerably larger and harder to see than most people appreciate, and it periodically produces acute dollar shortages abroad during global stress, even though the Federal Reserve is not directly managing that offshore dollar credit.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Much of the world's dollar-based credit is created offshore, through ordinary bank lending, in a system large enough to matter and opaque enough that its true scale is hard for anyone - including regulators - to fully see.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Digital cash before Bitcoin$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The desire for a genuinely digital form of money is much older than Bitcoin. Alden surveys decades of earlier attempts, ranging from centrally issued digital currencies to cryptographically sophisticated proposals for private digital cash, and finds that all of them ran into the same wall.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A digital file is trivially copyable. Unlike a physical coin, which can only be in one place at a time, a digital token can in principle be copied and spent twice - or a thousand times - unless something prevents it. This is the double-spend problem, and every pre-Bitcoin proposal solved it the same way: by appointing a trusted central party to keep the definitive ledger of who owns what, checking each transaction against it, and rejecting any attempt to spend the same unit twice.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$That solution worked technically, but it reintroduced exactly the vulnerability paper gold claims had introduced centuries earlier: a central point that had to be trusted, that could be pressured, shut down, hacked, or have its rules changed unilaterally. Alden's account of these earlier systems is not dismissive - some were technically elegant and genuinely ahead of their time - but she treats their common failure to remove the need for a trusted central party as the missing piece that kept digital cash from ever displacing the banking system it was meant to route around.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Every attempt at digital cash before Bitcoin solved the double-spend problem the same way - with a trusted central party - which reintroduced the very point of control digital money was supposed to remove.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Bitcoin's proposed solution$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden frames Bitcoin as an attempt to solve the double-spend problem without a trusted central party at all, by having a decentralized network of participants maintain and agree on the ledger collectively instead.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$A distributed ledger.$$, 'body', $$Every participant running the software holds a full copy of the transaction history, rather than trusting one institution's private record.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Proof of work.$$, 'body', $$Adding new transactions to the ledger requires expending real, verifiable computational effort, which makes rewriting history costly in a way that is easy for others to check but hard to fake.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$A fixed, transparent issuance schedule.$$, 'body', $$New bitcoin is created at a predetermined, publicly known rate that slows over time toward a hard cap of 21 million, so no participant or authority can unilaterally decide to issue more.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Open verifiability.$$, 'body', $$Anyone can run software that independently checks the entire ledger's validity, without needing to trust any single participant's word for it.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Measured against the same three-function test and the same list of properties from earlier chapters, Alden argues Bitcoin scores unusually well on scarcity, portability, and verifiability - a fixed, well-known supply schedule that no government or company can alter unilaterally; value that can move globally in minutes without physical transport; and a ledger anyone can audit without trusting an intermediary. Divisibility and durability, she notes, are essentially solved by its digital nature, since a bitcoin can be divided into very small units and does not physically decay.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Bitcoin's core claim is that a distributed ledger, proof of work, and a fixed issuance schedule can deliver gold-like scarcity and verifiability in a form that moves like a digital file.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Weighing Bitcoin fairly$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden is openly sympathetic to Bitcoin's design, but the book is more useful if its open questions are held alongside its claims rather than skipped past, and she does address a number of them directly.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Volatility.$$, 'body', $$Bitcoin's price has moved through large swings in both directions, which sits uneasily with its aspiration to be a stable store of value; whether that volatility is a temporary feature of an immature, thin market or something more structural is not yet settled.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Energy use.$$, 'body', $$Proof of work consumes meaningful amounts of electricity, and reasonable people disagree over whether that cost is a justified price for a decentralized, trust-minimized ledger or a wasteful expense relative to the problem being solved.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Scalability.$$, 'body', $$The base network can only process a limited number of transactions directly, and whether additional layers built on top can extend it to everyday, high-volume payments without reintroducing the trusted intermediaries it was designed to avoid remains a live engineering and design question.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Regulatory uncertainty.$$, 'body', $$Governments' treatment of Bitcoin varies widely and continues to shift, and a currency-like asset outside state control naturally invites scrutiny, restriction, or in some jurisdictions active hostility, any of which could shape its usefulness going forward.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Adoption risk.$$, 'body', $$Bitcoin's usefulness as money depends heavily on how widely it continues to be accepted and held; its historical trajectory is encouraging to supporters but still short, by the standard of gold's multi-thousand-year track record, and nothing guarantees the trend continues.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden's own position, made fairly explicit across the book, is that Bitcoin is very likely to keep growing in relevance as a monetary technology - but she frames this as a reasoned bet on a young system with a strong design, not as a settled outcome, and a careful reader should hold that distinction even where the book's tone is confident.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Alden's own outlook is bullish, but the honest version of her argument treats volatility, energy use, scalability, regulation, and adoption as genuinely open questions, not settled ones.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Broken Money is written by an analyst with a clear point of view - Alden is openly sympathetic to hard, scarce money and skeptical of the fiat system's long-term stability - and the book reads, in its second half especially, as a sustained argument rather than a neutral survey of monetary history. That does not make it wrong, but it is worth reading with the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It is an argument, not neutral history.$$, 'body', $$The selection of evidence throughout - which episodes get emphasis, which get a paragraph - tracks Alden's thesis that fiat money is structurally prone to debasement and that harder monies are generally preferable. A reader should notice where the narrative is building toward a conclusion rather than simply reporting one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The inequality argument is disputed.$$, 'body', $$Mainstream economists broadly agree that asset-price inflation has contributed to wealth concentration, but many attribute rising inequality substantially to other forces as well - globalization, technological change, tax policy, market structure - and dispute how much weight belongs on monetary policy specifically. Alden's framing gives monetary policy an outsized, near-singular causal role that many specialists would not fully endorse.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Central bank motives are characterized more simply than most practitioners would accept.$$, 'body', $$The book sometimes describes central bank and government behavior in terms that read as more deliberate or self-interested than the mainstream account, which more often emphasizes officials responding, imperfectly, to genuinely difficult tradeoffs between employment, growth, and price stability rather than pursuing debasement as a goal in itself.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Bitcoin's future role is a forecast, not an established fact.$$, 'body', $$However carefully reasoned, claims about what Bitcoin will become are predictions about an unresolved future, not settled history, and should be read with the same skepticism any reader would apply to a confident forecast about a young technology.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The monetary history is simplified for a general audience.$$, 'body', $$The account of the classical gold standard, Bretton Woods, and the eurodollar system is broadly accurate but compresses genuinely contested academic debates - for instance, economic historians still argue about how much the gold standard itself contributed to the length and depth of the Great Depression - into a cleaner narrative than specialists would offer.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read with those caveats in view, the book remains a genuinely useful, well-organized history of monetary technology, and its central three-function framework is a durable tool worth keeping regardless of which conclusions about Bitcoin's future you personally find persuasive.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ເງິນເປັນເຕັກໂນໂລຢີທີ່ວິວັດທະນາການ: ບົດທົດສອບສາມໜ້າທີ່$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Alden ເລີ່ມຕົ້ນໂດຍການປະຕິບັດຕໍ່ເງິນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເປັນກົດເສດຖະສາດທີ່ຄົງທີ່ ແຕ່ເປັນເຕັກໂນໂລຢີໜຶ່ງ - ເຄື່ອງມືທີ່ຄົນເຮົາຫັນມາໃຊ້ຮ່ວມກັນເພື່ອແກ້ບັນຫາການປະສານງານ ຊຶ່ງຖືກອອກແບບໃໝ່ຫຼາຍຄັ້ງຕະຫຼອດປະຫວັດສາດ ແລະ ມີແນວໂນ້ມຈະຖືກອອກແບບໃໝ່ອີກ. ຄຳຖາມທີ່ລາວກັບຄືນມາຫາຢູ່ຕະຫຼອດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນ "ເງິນຄືຫຍັງ" ໃນທາງນາມມະທຳ ແຕ່ແມ່ນ "ຫຍັງທີ່ໄດ້ຜົນຢ່າງຕໍ່ເນື່ອງ" ໂດຍວັດແທກຈາກໜ້າວຽກສາມຢ່າງທີ່ເງິນທຸກຊະນິດຖືກຄາດຫວັງໃຫ້ເຮັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ສື່ກາງແລກປ່ຽນ.$$, 'body', $$ບາງສິ່ງທີ່ຖືກຍອມຮັບຢ່າງກວ້າງຂວາງໃນການຄ້າ ເພື່ອບໍ່ໃຫ້ຄົນຕ້ອງແລກສິນຄ້າໂດຍກົງກັບສິນຄ້າອື່ນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຫົວໜ່ວຍບັນຊີ.$$, 'body', $$ໄມ້ວັດແທກລາຄາຮ່ວມກັນ ເພື່ອໃຫ້ມູນຄ່າຂອງສິ່ງທີ່ຕ່າງກັນ - ການຕັດຜົມ, ເນື້ອທີ່ດິນໜຶ່ງເຮັກຕາ, ຮາດດິດ - ສາມາດປຽບທຽບກັນໄດ້ໃນມາດຕະຖານດຽວກັນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ບ່ອນເກັບມູນຄ່າ.$$, 'body', $$ບາງສິ່ງທີ່ຮັກສາກຳລັງຊື້ໄວ້ໄດ້ ລະຫວ່າງເວລາທີ່ຫາໄດ້ ກັບເວລາທີ່ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍ ບໍ່ວ່າຊ່ອງຫວ່າງນັ້ນຈະເປັນໜຶ່ງອາທິດ ຫຼື ໝົດຊີວິດ.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຮູບແບບເງິນໜຶ່ງ ອາດເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ໜຶ່ງ ຫຼື ສອງຢ່າງໄດ້ ໂດຍບໍ່ຖືວ່າເປັນເງິນທີ່ດີໂດຍລວມ. Alden ເນັ້ນວ່າ ເງິນທີ່ຄົງທົນໃນປະຫວັດສາດ ລ້ວນເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ທັງສາມຢ່າງໄດ້ດີພໍສົມຄວນພ້ອມກັນ ແລະ ເງິນທີ່ເກັ່ງພຽງໜຶ່ງ ຫຼື ສອງໜ້າທີ່ ໃນທີ່ສຸດຈະຖືກແທນທີ່ ຫຼື ເສີມດ້ວຍບາງສິ່ງທີ່ດີກວ່າໃນທັງສາມດ້ານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ເງິນທີ່ດີ$$, 'body', $$ໃນມຸມມອງຂອງ Alden ຄືສິນຄ້າທາງການເງິນທີ່ເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນສື່ກາງແລກປ່ຽນ, ຫົວໜ່ວຍບັນຊີ ແລະ ບ່ອນເກັບມູນຄ່າ ພ້ອມກັນ ໂດຍໜ້າທີ່ໜຶ່ງບໍ່ບັ່ນທອນອີກໜ້າທີ່ໜຶ່ງຢ່າງໜັກ - ຕົວຢ່າງ ເງິນທີ່ໃຊ້ຈ່າຍງ່າຍ ແຕ່ເສຍມູນຄ່າຢູ່ຕະຫຼອດ ຈະບັ່ນທອນປະໂຫຍດຂອງຕົນເອງໃນຖານະບ່ອນເກັບມູນຄ່າ ແລະ ໃນທີ່ສຸດ ໃນຖານະຫົວໜ່ວຍບັນຊີທີ່ໝັ້ນຄົງ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ບົດທົດສອບສາມໜ້າທີ່ນີ້ ກາຍເປັນໄມ້ບັນທັດທີ່ Alden ໃຊ້ຕະຫຼອດທັງປຶ້ມ - ກັບງົວ ແລະ ເກືອ, ກັບຄຳ ແລະ ເງິນ, ກັບໂດລາ ແລະ ໃນທີ່ສຸດກັບ Bitcoin - ແລະ ມັນຄືກອບທີ່ຄວນຈື່ໄວ້ໃນຂະນະທີ່ປະຫວັດສາດເປີດອອກ ເພາະຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງແຝງຂອງປຶ້ມ ຄືວ່າ ບໍ່ມີເຕັກໂນໂລຢີການເງິນໃດເປັນຈຸດສຸດທ້າຍ - ແຕ່ລະອັນເປັນພຽງທາງອອກທີ່ດີທີ່ສຸດເທົ່າທີ່ມີ ຈົນກວ່າອັນທີ່ດີກວ່າຈະເຂົ້າມາແທນທີ່.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ເງິນຖືກຕັດສິນຈາກການເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ເປັນສື່ກາງແລກປ່ຽນ, ຫົວໜ່ວຍບັນຊີ ແລະ ບ່ອນເກັບມູນຄ່າ ພ້ອມກັນ - ແລະ ເງິນໃນປະຫວັດສາດ ຖືກແທນທີ່ຊ້ຳແລ້ວຊ້ຳອີກໂດຍທາງອອກທີ່ດີກວ່າຕໍ່ບັນຫາສາມສ່ວນອັນດຽວກັນນີ້.$$),
